var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"toml/#What-goes-in-the-TOML","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"RepliBuild does everything except what has to be stated, and everything that has to be stated goes in replibuild.toml. That is the design. There is no second file, no script to maintain, no generated code to patch. If the wrapper is wrong or incomplete, the fix is a line in this file.\n\nThis page goes through a library the way you should: one question at a time, and for each, how to tell whether your library needs it, what to write, and what happens if you leave it out. Every key is the lookup table for when you know which key you want.","title":"What goes in the TOML"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#What-you-state,-and-what-you-never-state","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"You never describe the library's functions, structs, enums or classes, how its files depend on each other, or how a call is made. RepliBuild compiles the library and takes all of that from the result, so it cannot disagree with the binary.\n\nWhat you state is what no tool can work out, because it is not in the source tree's shape or in a compiled library:\n\nwhere the source is, and which files in it are not the library;\nthe flags and defines the library's own build system would pass;\nwhat the compiler discards or never sees: macros, code that exists only in headers, templates nobody instantiated;\nwhat a signature does not say: which extra arguments a variadic function takes, who frees a returned string;\nyour own choices: what to leave out of the wrapper, what to call things.\n\nLeaving one of these out rarely fails the build. The usual result is a wrapper that loads and is missing something, so go through the list deliberately.\n\nThe first item matters most. RepliBuild can only build what it is fed, and how you feed it differs for files in your project, a folder on disk, a git repository and a tarball: Where the source comes from.","title":"What you state, and what you never state"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#The-checklist","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"Read the library's public headers and its build files (CMakeLists.txt, Makefile) with these questions.\n\nQuestion If yes Section\nWhere is the source: in the project, a folder on disk, git, a tarball? source_files, [dependencies] Where the source is\nDoes it hold tests, examples, tools, other platforms' code? exclude, or a shorter source_files Files that are not the library\nIs it more than one library? several sources, [graph], or link_dirs Several libraries\nDoes its build pass -D defines, a -std=, extra include folders? [compile] Flags, defines and include folders\nDoes its build write a config.h or other header? capture it, or use graph mode Headers the build system generates\nDoes it use sin, threads, dlopen, or another library? [link] Libraries it links\nIs part of the API #defined? [wrap.macros] Macros\nAre there static inline functions, or is it a single-header library? [wrap.macros], shim_defines Code that lives in headers\nAre there functions ending in ...? [wrap.varargs] Variadic functions\nDoes a function return a char* the caller must free? [wrap.cstring_owned] Strings the caller must free\nDo structs or classes come out empty? -fstandalone-debug Types declared only in headers\nC++: do you want std::vector, std::map, or a library template? [types] templates C++ templates and containers\nDoes the wrapper carry internal functions or types? visibility, exclude_symbols, surface_types_only Leaving things out\nIs a name unusable? [wrap.rename] Names\nMust one recipe work on Linux and Windows? [platform.*] Linux and Windows in one file\nIs it a CMake project of several libraries? [graph] CMake projects","title":"The checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#A-complete-recipe","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"Lua, from its git repository. Each block answers one of the questions above.\n\n[project]\nname = \"lua\"                       # → liblua.so, module Lua, julia/Lua.jl\n\n[dependencies.lua]                 # where the source is\ntype    = \"git\"\nurl     = \"https://github.com/lua/lua.git\"\ntag     = \"v5.4.7\"\ncommit  = \"1ab3208a1fceb12fca8f24ba57d6e13c5bff15e3\"\nexclude = [\"onelua.c\", \"lua.c\", \"luac.c\", \"ltests.c\"]   # programs and tests, not the library\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-DLUA_USE_LINUX\"]             # what Lua's Makefile passes\n\n[link]\nenable_lto         = false\noptimization_level = \"2\"\nlink_libraries     = [\"m\", \"dl\"]                        # -lm -ldl\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage     = \"c\"\nshim_headers = [\"lua.h\", \"lauxlib.h\"]                   # headers the macros come from\n\n[wrap.macros.lua_pop]                                   # part of Lua's API is macros\nret  = \"void\"\nargs = [\"lua_State*\", \"int\"]\n\n[wrap.varargs]                                          # lua_pushfstring(L, fmt, ...)\nproven_at       = \"1ab3208a1fceb12fca8f24ba57d6e13c5bff15e3\"\nlua_pushfstring = [[\"Cstring\"], [\"Cint\"], [\"Cstring\", \"Cint\"]]\n\n[platform.windows.compile]                              # what differs on Windows\nflags = [\"-O2\"]\n[platform.windows.link]\nlink_libraries = []\n\nLua's 33 source files, and every struct, enum and function in them, are not mentioned. Those come from the build.","title":"A complete recipe"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#The-four-lines-every-recipe-has","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"[project]\nname = \"mylib\"\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\"]\n\n[link]\nenable_lto         = false\noptimization_level = \"2\"\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage = \"c\"\n\n[project] name names everything: the library file (libmylib.so), the Julia module (Mylib, CamelCase of the name) and the name you use it by. [wrap] module_name overrides the module name.\n[wrap] language is \"c\" or \"cpp\". It chooses the compiler, so it must match the library: a C++ library built as \"c\" loses its classes and methods. discover sets it from the file extensions. Spell it in lower case; anything other than \"c\" is treated as C++.\n[compile] flags are passed to the compiler for every file. Always include -fPIC. discover writes no -O flag, which is an unoptimized build: add -O2, and set [link] optimization_level = \"2\" to match. For C++, add the standard the library needs (-std=c++17).\n[link] enable_lto = false. Keep this line in every recipe. discover writes it; a C recipe written by hand without it switches on an experimental calling mode.","title":"The four lines every recipe has"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Where-the-source-is","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"This is the first thing to state, and each way of stating it behaves differently. Where the source comes from is the full account; the short version:\n\n# 1. Files in the project folder: listed, one by one. build never searches for them.\n[compile]\nsource_files = [\"src/mylib.c\", \"extra/glue.c\"]\n\n# 2. A folder on disk: searched on every build. Its tests ARE compiled unless excluded.\n[dependencies.mylib]\ntype    = \"local\"\npath    = \"../mylib\"\nexclude = [\"tests/\", \"examples/\"]\n\n# 3. A git repository: cloned, checked against the commit, searched.\n[dependencies.cjson]\ntype   = \"git\"\nurl    = \"https://github.com/DaveGamble/cJSON.git\"\ntag    = \"v1.7.18\"\ncommit = \"acc76239bee01d8e9c858ae2cab296704e52d916\"   # the full 40-character commit\n\n# 4. A tarball: downloaded, checked against the hash, unpacked, searched.\n[dependencies.quickjs]\ntype   = \"archive\"\nurl    = \"https://bellard.org/quickjs/quickjs-2026-06-04.tar.xz\"\nsha256 = \"b376e839b322978313d929fd20663b11ba58b75df5a46c126dd19ea2fa70ad2a\"\n\nA recipe may use any number of these together. All of it is compiled into the one library.\n\ndiscover writes source_files for a project folder, listing every source file it finds, tests included. Read the list and delete what does not belong.\nAlways give commit with a git dependency. A tag can be moved to different code; with commit set, a moved tag stops the build instead of silently compiling something else.","title":"Where the source is"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Files-that-are-not-the-library","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. Look at the top level of the source. Folders such as test, examples, tools, bench, fuzz, cli, a bundled copy of another library, or one folder per platform or backend are not the library. Any .c or .cpp file with a main function is a program, not the library.\n\nRepliBuild compiles every source file it is given, so these have to be kept out. How depends on where the source is:\n\nSource Kept out by\nFiles in the project folder Deleting their lines from source_files\nA local folder exclude. Only build, .git and .cache are skipped by themselves\ngit or archive exclude. Folders named test, tests, example, examples, fuzzing, build, doc and docs are skipped by themselves\n\n[dependencies.box2d]\ntype    = \"git\"\nurl     = \"https://github.com/erincatto/box2d.git\"\ntag     = \"v2.4.1\"\ncommit  = \"9ebbbcd960ad424e03e5de6e66a40764c16f51bc\"\nexclude = [\"testbed/\", \"unit-test/\", \"extern/\"]   # a GUI demo, tests, bundled libraries\n\nAn entry matches when it is the file's name, or appears anywhere in the file's path inside the dependency. So \"tools/\" removes a folder and \"luac.c\" removes one file, and a short entry such as \"t\" removes far too much. Write folder entries with a trailing /.\n\nIf you leave it out. The build fails on a duplicate main or a missing header, or it succeeds and the wrapper is full of test helpers and another library's functions. A main among names(M) is the sign.","title":"Files that are not the library"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Flags,-defines-and-include-folders","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. Open the library's CMakeLists.txt or Makefile and look for -D…, add_definitions, target_compile_definitions, -std=, and include_directories. A README section called \"Building\" or \"Configuration\" often lists the defines that switch features on.\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\n    \"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-std=c++17\",\n    \"-DGGML_USE_CPU\",                      # a feature switch\n    '-DGGML_VERSION=\"0.18.1\"',             # a define with a string value\n]\ninclude_dirs = [\n    \"include\",                                         # in the project\n    \".replibuild_cache/deps/llamacpp/ggml/include\",    # inside a fetched dependency\n]\n\n[compile.defines]            # the same as -DSQLITE_THREADSAFE=1 in flags\nSQLITE_THREADSAFE = \"1\"\n\nRelative paths are relative to the folder holding replibuild.toml. Do not write absolute paths into a recipe you intend to share.\nA git or archive dependency lives in .replibuild_cache/deps/<name>/. Its top folder, its include/ and its src/ are searched for headers already. A local folder is searched at its top and in its include/ only. Name any other folder under include_dirs.\n[compile.defines] always writes -Dname=value. For a define with no value (-DFOO), use flags.\n\nIf you leave it out. A missing include folder is a compile error naming the header. A missing define is either a compile error in one file, or a feature of the library that is silently switched off.","title":"Flags, defines and include folders"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Headers-the-build-system-generates","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. The build stops on a header that is not in the repository: config.h: No such file or directory, or <mylib>_config.h. The repository has a config.h.in, config.h.cmake or a configure_file line in its CMakeLists.txt instead.\n\nDo not write the header by hand. For a CMake project there are two ways:\n\nLet CMake drive the build. Add a [graph] section, and RepliBuild runs the project's configure step itself: CMake projects.\nCapture the generated files once and keep them beside the recipe:\nusing RepliBuild\nconst S = RepliBuild.SysConfigGen\n\nprobe = S.cmake_probe(\"path/to/library/source\";\n                      args = [\"-DBUILD_TESTING=OFF\"])    # the library's own CMake options\nS.capture_config(probe, \"config\")        # copies the generated headers into ./config/\nprint(S.toml_fragment(probe))            # proposed [compile] flags, include dirs and excludes\ncmake_probe runs only CMake's configure step, with the same compiler the build will use. capture_config also writes a SYSCONFIG.md recording how the files were produced. toml_fragment prints a suggestion: read it, and paste what applies. The captured files describe one platform; capture again for each platform and after upgrading the library.\n\nA header written while the library builds, by a script or a helper program, is beyond the second way. Use [graph], or look in the repository for a ready-made copy (libpng ships pnglibconf.h.prebuilt).","title":"Headers the build system generates"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Libraries-it-links","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. The library includes <math.h> (m), <pthread.h> (pthread), <dlfcn.h> (dl), or the headers of another library. Its build files have target_link_libraries or -l….\n\n[link]\nlink_libraries = [\"m\", \"dl\"]              # -lm -ldl\nlink_dirs      = [\"/opt/thing/lib\"]       # where to find a library that is not a system one\n\nA library found through link_dirs is also the one loaded at run time, so the wrapper uses the copy you linked and not another that happens to be installed.\n\nm, dl, pthread and rt are Linux names. A recipe that should also build on Windows overrides the list there (Linux and Windows in one file).\n\nIf you leave it out. The build still succeeds, because a shared library may have unresolved names. The failure comes later, as could not load symbol at the first call that needs the missing library, or not at all on your machine and then on someone else's.","title":"Libraries it links"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Macros","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. Search the public headers for #define. Two kinds matter: constants the API expects you to pass (SQLITE_OK, LUA_REGISTRYINDEX), and function-like macros that the documentation presents as functions (lua_pop, deflateInit).\n\nA macro is gone once the library is compiled, so each one you want is declared with its types, and RepliBuild compiles a small real function for it:\n\n[wrap]\nshim_headers = [\"lua.h\", \"lauxlib.h\"]     # the headers that define the macros\n\n[wrap.macros.lua_pop]                     # #define lua_pop(L, n) …\nret  = \"void\"\nargs = [\"lua_State*\", \"int\"]\n\n[wrap.macros.LUA_REGISTRYINDEX]           # #define LUA_REGISTRYINDEX (…)\nret = \"int\"\n\nret and args are C types, written as you would in C.\nWith args, the entry is a function-like macro. Without args, it is a constant. args = [] is a function-like macro taking no arguments.\nIn Julia both are functions: lua_pop(L, 1) and LUA_REGISTRYINDEX().\nshim_headers are written as you would #include them. Each must be found inside the project or a dependency. If one resolves to a copy installed on the system instead, the build stops, because that copy may be a different version: give a longer path (\"c/blake3.h\") or add the folder to include_dirs.\n\nIf you leave it out. The macro is not in the wrapper, and nothing says so.","title":"Macros"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Code-that-lives-in-headers","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"Three cases, in rising order of work. Header-only libraries covers all of them in full.\n\nA single-header library in the stb style, whose implementation is compiled only where a macro is defined:\n\n[wrap]\nshim_headers = [\"stb_image.h\"]\nshim_defines = [\"STB_IMAGE_IMPLEMENTATION\"]\n\nThat is the whole recipe for it: no source file of your own. Do not put the define in [compile] flags instead. There it reaches every file, the implementation is compiled more than once, and the link fails.\n\nstatic inline functions in a C header are declared like macros, under [wrap.macros].\n\nC++ functions defined inside a class need nothing. RepliBuild compiles them in itself, provided the class is fully described (Types declared only in headers).","title":"Code that lives in headers"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Variadic-functions","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. A declaration ends in ...: int ml_log(const char *fmt, ...).\n\nThe declaration does not say what the extra arguments are, so list the combinations you want to call. Each becomes its own Julia function:\n\n[wrap.varargs]\nproven_at       = \"1ab3208a1fceb12fca8f24ba57d6e13c5bff15e3\"\nlua_pushfstring = [\n    [\"Cstring\"],              # lua_pushfstring(L, fmt, const char*)\n    [\"Cint\"],                 # lua_pushfstring(L, fmt, int)\n    [\"Cstring\", \"Cint\"],      # lua_pushfstring(L, fmt, const char*, int)\n]\n\nThree rules:\n\nList only the extra arguments. The fixed ones (L and fmt here) are already known.\nUse Julia's names for C types, not C's: Cint, Cuint, Clong, Culong, Clonglong, Csize_t, Cdouble, Cfloat, Cstring, Cchar, Bool, the sized types (Int32, UInt64, Float64, …), and Ptr{Name} for a pointer. This is the opposite of [wrap.macros], which takes C types.\nIt is a list of lists. One combination with one argument is [[\"Cint\"]], not [\"Cint\"].\n\nIn Julia each combination is named after its types: lua_pushfstring_Cstring(L, fmt, s), lua_pushfstring_Cstring_Cint(L, fmt, s, i). The plain lua_pushfstring(L, fmt) takes the fixed arguments only.\n\nproven_at is the commit of the library you checked these combinations against. Without it, wrap warns on every run. With it, upgrading the library to another commit stops the build until you have looked at the function again and updated the value. It is compared with the commit of the dependency, so a project with no git dependency cannot set it and keeps the warning.\n\nIf you leave it out. Only the fixed-arguments form exists, and wrap warns once per function.","title":"Variadic functions"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Strings-the-caller-must-free","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. A function returns char* (not const char*), and the documentation says the caller owns the result, or names a function to release it: cJSON_Print and cJSON_free, sqlite3_mprintf and sqlite3_free.\n\n[wrap.cstring_owned]\ncJSON_Print     = \"cJSON_free\"\nsqlite3_mprintf = \"sqlite3_free\"\nstrdup_like_fn  = \"free\"            # released with the C library's free\n\nThe Julia function then copies the text into a String, frees the C buffer with the function you named, and returns the String.\n\nWithout an entry, a char* result is still returned as a String (or nothing for a NULL pointer), and the C buffer is left alone. That is correct for strings the library keeps, such as sqlite3_errmsg, which is why it is the default.\n\nIf you leave it out. Every call leaks the buffer.","title":"Strings the caller must free"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Types-declared-only-in-headers","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. A struct or class you can see in a header appears in the wrapper with no fields, cannot be constructed, or functions that take it by value raise an ABI Safety Trap. Typical victims are the \"settings\" structs a caller fills in (b2BodyDef, llama_context_params) and C++ classes whose constructors are written inside the class.\n\nThe compiler records a type's full definition only where the library's own code needs it. One flag makes it record every type the headers define:\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-std=c++17\", \"-fstandalone-debug\"]\n\nAdd it to C++ recipes from the start. For a large C++ library where it makes the build too big, \"-Xclang\", \"-fno-use-ctor-homing\" (two entries in flags) covers the most common case, classes with header-defined constructors, at a fraction of the size.\n\nWith the definitions recorded, RepliBuild also does three things for C++ classes without being asked:\n\na constructor defined in the header becomes callable;\na member function defined in the class body becomes callable;\na call that leaves out default arguments becomes a shorter method of the same function.\n\nwrap prints a line for each, saying how many it added and what it skipped.\n\nA class that still does not appear is one that no source file of the library mentions at all. Name it:\n\n[types]\nrecords = [\"btDefaultMotionState\"]        # spelled as in C++, with its namespace","title":"Types declared only in headers"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#C-templates-and-containers","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"How to tell. You want to create a std::vector<double> in Julia and hand it to the library, or the API uses an instantiation of the library's own template (fmt::basic_memory_buffer<char>), and the type is missing from the wrapper.\n\nA template is compiled only for the type arguments somebody uses. List the ones you want:\n\n[types]\ntemplates        = [\"std::vector<double>\", \"std::string\", \"std::map<int, int>\"]\ntemplate_headers = [\"<fmt/format.h>\"]      # headers for templates that are not std::\n\nEntries are C++ type names, written as in C++.\nThe standard headers for std::vector, std::string, std::map, std::unordered_map, std::set, std::deque and std::list are found for you. Name any other header in template_headers: <…> for a system header, a plain name for one of the library's.\nstd::vector, std::string, std::map and std::unordered_map get a Julia constructor, create_std_vector_double(), and work like Julia collections (Call the wrapper). Any other template is compiled so its type exists in the wrapper, with no constructor.\nA function template cannot be listed here (Header-only libraries).\n\nA library whose API uses no templates needs none of this. Functions that take or return std::string work without an entry.\n\nIf you leave it out. The type is missing, or there is no way to create one from Julia. An entry that produces nothing is reported by build as a warning naming it.","title":"C++ templates and containers"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Leaving-things-out","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"By default the wrapper has a function for everything the library exports, and a type for everything the build recorded. For many libraries that includes internals. There are three tools, in the order to reach for them.","title":"Leaving things out"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#exclude_symbols:-name-what-should-go","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"[wrap]\nexclude_symbols = [\"_pcre2_*\", \"matmul_*\", \"vk::*\"]\n\nEach entry is a pattern for a function or global variable to leave out. * matches any text and ? one character; everything else is literal, including (, +, < and ::. A pattern must match a whole name, so use a trailing * for a prefix.\n\nA C function is matched by its name. A C++ function is matched by its full C++ signature, shapes::Rect::scale(double), so end the pattern with *: shapes::Rect::scale* for one function's overloads, vk::* for a namespace. Its symbol name in the library (_ZN6shapes4Rect5scaleEd) matches too.\n\nA pattern that matches nothing is an error. It means the library changed or the pattern has a typo, and either way the recipe is no longer doing what you wrote it to do.\n\nThis is applied by wrap: changing it needs no rebuild.","title":"exclude_symbols: name what should go"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#visibility:-use-the-library's-own-list-of-public-functions","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"Many libraries mark their public functions with an export macro (PCRE2_EXPORT, B2_API, CJSON_PUBLIC). For those, one key hides everything unmarked:\n\n[compile]\nvisibility = \"hidden\"\n\ndanger: Check before you set it\nOn a library that does not mark its public functions, this hides the entire API and the build is refused. Ask first:RepliBuild.VisibilityProbe.visibility_probe(\"replibuild.toml\")VisibilityReport: pcre2\n  external defs      : 105\n  library annotated  : 78  (74.3%)\n  verdict            : annotated\n  wrapped today      : 119\n    retained         : 92\n    DROPPED          : 27annotated means the key is safe. Read the dropped list anyway: those functions leave the wrapper, and the probe cannot tell an internal helper from a public function the authors forgot to mark.\n\nSome libraries only mark their functions when a define is set (cJSON needs -DCJSON_API_VISIBILITY, ggml needs -DGGML_SHARED). The probe uses your recipe's flags, so run it again after changing them, and after upgrading the library.","title":"visibility: use the library's own list of public functions"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#surface_types_only:-drop-types-the-API-never-uses","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"A C++ library that compiles a large internal codebase can record thousands of internal types. If the module fails to load because of them (a type that shadows a Julia name, duplicate fields), keep only the types the wrapped functions and globals reach:\n\n[wrap]\nsurface_types_only  = true\nsurface_types_extra = [\"MD_*_DETAIL\", \"Argon2_ErrorCodes\"]   # types to keep anyway\n\nsurface_types_extra is for types a caller needs that no function signature names: a struct passed to a callback as void*, or an enum whose values are returned as plain int. Patterns work as in exclude_symbols, and one that matches nothing is an error.\n\nLeave this off unless the module does not load. It can remove types you need.","title":"surface_types_only: drop types the API never uses"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Names","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"A C function keeps its name. A C++ function is named after its namespace, class and name, joined with _: tinyxml2::XMLDocument::Parse is tinyxml2_XMLDocument_Parse. Template arguments are part of the name, which can make it hundreds of characters long.\n\nGive a function a name of your own with [wrap.rename]:\n\n[wrap.rename]\n\"boost_math_pdf_*\"            = \"pdf\"          # every match becomes a method of `pdf`\nshapes_Rect_area              = \"rect_area\"    # by the name the wrapper gives it\n_ZN8stl_test6scaledIlEET_S1_i = \"scaled_long\"  # by its symbol name in the library\n\nThe key is the name the wrapper gives the function without the rename, or the function's symbol name in the library. Patterns work as in exclude_symbols.\nThe value must be a valid Julia name.\nSeveral functions may be given one name. They become methods of one Julia function, told apart by their argument types.\nThe old name no longer exists.\nA key that matches nothing, or a new name that is already taken, is an error.\n\nTypes cannot be renamed. In your own code, const Normal = M.normal_distribution_double_… gives one a short name.","title":"Names"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Linux-and-Windows-in-one-file","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"Write the recipe for Linux. Put what differs on Windows in [platform.windows.*] tables, which are laid over the rest on that system:\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-DLUA_USE_LINUX\"]\n\n[link]\nlink_libraries = [\"m\", \"dl\"]\n\n[platform.windows.compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\"]                       # replaces the list above\n\n[platform.windows.link]\nlink_libraries = []                   # an empty list means none\n\n[platform.windows.dependencies.glfw]\nexclude = [\"x11_\", \"glx_\", \"posix_\", \"linux_\"]   # the exclude list of [dependencies.glfw], on Windows\n\nA value in a platform table replaces the value outside it. Lists are replaced whole, not merged. The platform names are linux, windows and macos; any other name is an error.","title":"Linux and Windows in one file"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#When-an-edit-takes-effect","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"Needs build, then wrap Needs only wrap\n[compile]: flags, defines, include folders, visibility, source_files [wrap] exclude_symbols\n[link]: libraries, folders, optimization level [wrap.rename]\n[dependencies] [wrap.varargs]\n[types] templates, template_headers, records [wrap.cstring_owned]\n[wrap.macros], shim_headers, shim_defines [wrap] surface_types_only, surface_types_extra\n\nRunning build when nothing it reads has changed costs almost nothing, so when unsure, run both.","title":"When an edit takes effect"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Mistakes-the-file-does-not-catch","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"A misspelled key is ignored, silently. If a setting seems to do nothing, check its spelling and its section against Every key.\n[wrap.macros] takes C types; [wrap.varargs] takes Julia types.\nA dependency with a url and no type is not fetched. The default type is \"local\".\nA new file in the project folder is not compiled until it is in source_files.\nA local folder's tests/ are compiled unless exclude names them.\nextra_link_libs is not a [link] key. The key is link_libraries.","title":"Mistakes the file does not catch"},{"category":"section","location":"toml/#Recipes-to-copy-from","page":"What goes in the TOML","text":"RepliBuild-Hub holds working recipes for lua, sqlite, zlib, pcre2, cJSON, imgui, box2d, fmt, pugixml, llama.cpp, libwebp and others. Find one whose library is built like yours and start from it. RepliBuild.search(\"xml\") lists them by keyword.","title":"Recipes to copy from"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals-and-Dispatch","page":"Internals","text":"note: Developer documentation\nThis page is for contributors. The user manual starts at Home. Sister pages: JLCS / MLIR, Inheritance ABI, Developer index.\n\nRepliBuild compiles C/C++ with Clang, reads the DWARF debug metadata the compiler emits about its own output, and generates Julia bindings that are correct by construction — struct offsets, enum underlying types, vtable slots, base-subobject offsets, and calling conventions all come from the compiler's own record rather than being guessed. Where DWARF is incomplete (enums the optimizer folded away, function-pointer typedefs) the Clang.jl AST fills the gap (macros come only from [wrap.macros], which compiles a shim per macro); where Julia's computed struct alignment disagrees with the DWARF size, the struct is treated as packed and routed away from ccall.\n\nThis page is the technical reference for how that pipeline is assembled — the stages, the three dispatch tiers, and the modules behind each.\n\nTier 2 — the MLIR/JLCS marshalling layer — has its own page: ABI Marshalling as Compiler IR covers the dialect's types and ops, the SysV lowering, the thunk calling contract, source-level debugging, and the failure classes the design exists to make loud. This page describes where it sits; that one describes what it is.","title":"Internals & Dispatch"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#The-pipeline","page":"Internals","text":"Source becomes a loadable Julia module in seven stages, each owned by one module:\n\nDependencyResolver — resolve the [dependencies] table (git / local / system) and merge the external sources into the build graph.\nDiscovery — scan files and the #include graph; write or refresh replibuild.toml.\nCompiler — Clang compiles each translation unit to LLVM IR, cached on source mtime plus a compile fingerprint (flags, defines, include dirs, LLVM version, target triple).\nLinker — link, optimize, and assemble the IR into the .so (plus LTO bitcode when enable_lto is on).\nDWARFParser — llvm-dwarfdump + nm produce ClassInfo / VtableInfo and the compilation_metadata.json layout facts.\nWrapper generator — DWARF + symbols drive the C or C++ generator to emit the Julia module.\nJITManager — at module load, stand up a per-library MLIR thunk engine for any Tier-2 calls.\n\nFor C projects the link/optimize/assemble steps run in-process on Julia's resident libLLVM, version-matched to the JLL clang that emitted the IR (no external LLVM, no DWARF-dropping version skew); a failure is a hard error unless [link] fallback = true selects the external llvm-link/opt pipeline. C++ always uses the external pipeline plus the system MLIR dialect. Only the final .ll → .so codegen shells to clang in both buckets.","title":"The pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Three-dispatch-tiers","page":"Internals","text":"Each wrapped function is routed to exactly one calling tier, chosen from its DWARF signature:\n\nTier Mechanism Selected when\n1 Base.llvmcall against a per-function bitcode slice POD args, scalar/pointer return, [wrap.tier1] enable = true (C only)\n2 MLIR thunk via libJLCS.so (JIT at load, or AOT _thunks.so) packed structs, unions, large by-value struct returns, C++ classes, virtual dispatch, exceptions\n3 ccall into the .so the unconditional fallback\n\nThe routing decision lives in Wrapper/DispatchLogic.jl (is_ccall_safe, is_c_lto_safe); the Tier selection logic section below lists the exact checks.\n\nnote: Two Tier-1 payloads, one supported\nTier 1 can carry its IR two ways, and the knobs are independent.Per-function slices — [wrap.tier1] enable (C only, default off) is the supported path. _tier1_slice_prepass (Wrapper/C/Tier1C.jl) runs IRGen/Slicer.jl over every is_c_lto_safe non-varargs candidate, applies the hazard/size policy, and dlsym-pre-flights each slice's declarations against the real .so. A throw while slicing one function makes it an errored refusal, never cached, so that function alone stays on ccall; a failure on the whole promoted module turns Tier 1 off for the wrap. Acceptance only makes a function eligible: a call site additionally needs lto_shape_ok (no Cstring or struct crossing) and must survive the signature dedup, so _tier1_emit_slices! writes julia/slices/<mangled>.ll from the final wrapper text — only slices a call site actually reads reach disk, and TIER1_FUNCTIONS is derived in the same pass. Rests on static promotion (_promote_statics_libllvm) to guarantee every declared symbol reaches the dynamic symbol table. Decided at generation time — non-accepted functions emit plain ccall, and the wrapper exports TIER1_FUNCTIONS.Whole-module bitcode — [link] enable_lto embeds the entire linked module at each call site: scale-limited (can crash Julia's JIT on large libraries) and it duplicates file-local static state between the embedded bitcode and the .so. Production configurations set enable_lto = false; C++ defaults to LTO off. Treat it as an experimentation path for small stateless kernels.See Tier 1.","title":"Three dispatch tiers"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Wrapper","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Wrapper.jl, src/Wrapper/\n\nThe Wrapper package generates Julia FFI modules from DWARF metadata and binary symbol tables. It is structured as a two-track system: a C generator and a C++ generator, selected automatically via config.wrap.language.","title":"Wrapper"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Module-layout","page":"Internals","text":"Module Source Role\nWrapper.Generator src/Wrapper/Generator.jl Top-level wrap_library() entry point; dispatches to C or C++ generator\nWrapper.DispatchLogic src/Wrapper/DispatchLogic.jl Per-function tier routing decisions (is_ccall_safe, is_c_lto_safe)\nWrapper.TypeRegistry src/Wrapper/TypeRegistry.jl TypeRegistry and TypeStrictness — shared type-resolution context\nWrapper.Symbols src/Wrapper/Symbols.jl ParamInfo / SymbolInfo structs for structured symbol data\nWrapper.FunctionPointers src/Wrapper/FunctionPointers.jl DWARF function_ptr(...) signature to Julia @cfunction type string\nWrapper.Utils src/Wrapper/Utils.jl Keyword escaping, identifier sanitization shared between generators\nWrapper.C.GeneratorC src/Wrapper/C/GeneratorC.jl Full C wrapper generator (structs, enums, functions, LTO, thunks)\nWrapper.C.TypesC src/Wrapper/C/TypesC.jl C type heuristics and base type map\nWrapper.C.UtilsC src/Wrapper/C/UtilsC.jl C-specific identifier/format helpers\nWrapper.C.IdentifiersC src/Wrapper/C/IdentifiersC.jl C name sanitization\nWrapper.Cpp.GeneratorCpp src/Wrapper/Cpp/GeneratorCpp.jl Full C++ wrapper generator (classes, inheritance flattening with subobject-offset rebasing, as_<Base>/as_<VBase> upcasts, Managed handles, virtual dispatch)\nWrapper.Cpp.TypesCpp src/Wrapper/Cpp/TypesCpp.jl C++ type map including STL, templates, references\nWrapper.Cpp.IdentifiersCpp src/Wrapper/Cpp/IdentifiersCpp.jl Namespace stripping, operator sanitization\nWrapper.Cpp.UtilsCpp src/Wrapper/Cpp/UtilsCpp.jl C++ formatting helpers\nWrapper.Cpp.STLWrappers src/Wrapper/Cpp/STLWrappers.jl STL container type detection and accessor generation\nRustBuild src/Builder/RustBuild.jl build for a [rust] project: cargo and rustc compile and link the crate, and the result is read back into compilation_metadata.json\nRustABI src/Builder/RustABI.jl How each Rust function is called, read from the define lines of rustc's IR and matched to its DWARF parameters; a function that does not match is refused\nRustDWARF src/Builder/RustDWARF.jl rustc's DWARF: records, enums that carry data, pointer kinds, methods\nRustGlue src/Builder/RustGlue.jl The Rust the build writes per type so a value can be dropped: which types, how each is spelled in source, and what the built library says of each\nRustThunks src/IRGen/RustThunks.jl One thunk per Rust function, from its metadata row: JIT'd, or compiled by wrap into a library of their own that depends on nothing\nRustRuntime src/RustRuntime.jl What a Rust wrapper calls at run time: the library, a call through a thunk, RustPanic, RustError, drop!\nGeneratorRust src/Wrapper/Rust/GeneratorRust.jl wrap for a [rust] project: the Julia module, from the metadata alone","title":"Module layout"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Language-selection","page":"Internals","text":"wrap.language selects the generator: \"c\" or \"cpp\". A Rust project is selected by its [rust] section instead, and takes a path of its own from build on (RustBuild) and through wrap (GeneratorRust).\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage = \"c\"   # selects C generator + clang toolchain\nlanguage = \"cpp\" # selects C++ generator + clang++ toolchain (default)\n\ndiscover() sets this automatically based on the scanned source files. A new language is more than a generator: the build pipeline takes any language that is not C for C++, so a language with its own compiler branches off before it, as Rust does.","title":"Language selection"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Tier-selection-logic","page":"Internals","text":"The function is_ccall_safe() in src/Wrapper/DispatchLogic.jl is the core dispatch decision. It inspects each function's DWARF metadata and returns false when the signature needs an MLIR thunk (Tier 2), true when it can be called directly (Tier 1 or Tier 3).\n\nChoosing between the two direct tiers is a second, C-only step. is_c_lto_safe() marks a function Tier-1-eligible unless its return type is a packed struct or a by-value union — C has no templates, vtables, STL, or inheritance, so those are the only ABI hazards, and by-value parameters are fine either way. Eligibility is necessary but not sufficient: with [wrap.tier1] enable = true the function must also survive slicing, the hazard/size policy, and the dlsym pre-flight. Anything that does not, and everything when Tier 1 is off, emits ccall.\n\nChecks performed:\n\nSTL container types — Any STL type in parameters or return forces Tier 2\nReturn type safety:\nTemplate returns (contains <) → Tier 2 (unpredictable ABI)\nStruct return by value > 16 bytes → Tier 2 (too large for ccall sret)\nNon-POD class return → Tier 2\nPacked struct return (DWARF size != Julia aligned size) → Tier 2\nParameter type safety:\nUnion parameters → Tier 2\nPacked struct parameters → Tier 2\nException safety — Per-function is_noexcept flag: a noexcept in the sources and a nounwind definition in the linked IR. If absent (function may throw) and the module's may_throw setting is on, the function routes through jlcs.try_call rather than ccall. A function with C linkage in a C++ library is routed by the nounwind fact alone (metadata nounwind): extern \"C\" code may throw too.\n\nFunctions routed to Tier 2 are further divided between JIT dispatch (JITManager.invoke()) and AOT thunks (ccall to _thunks.so), controlled by the aot_thunks config flag.","title":"Tier selection logic"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Idiomatic-wrapper-generation","page":"Internals","text":"Beyond raw ccall bindings, the C++ generator emits Julia types that own a C++ object and free it from a GC finalizer:\n\nDeleters. A class's deleter is the library's deleter function for it (a free function or static member, one X* parameter, a name that says delete, destroy or free), else its deleting destructor (D0). A class with neither has no deleter: its other destructors end the object's lifetime without freeing its storage, and a method that takes this is never a deleter.\nManagedX, for every class with a deleter: a mutable struct holding a Ptr{X} whose finalizer calls the deleter by its mangled symbol, plus <f>_safe for each ccall-path function, and each function with C linkage, returning X*.\nFactory structs. A free function named create_, new_, make_, alloc_, init_ or build_ returning a pointer to a known class (the pointee, or a class its name names that derives from the pointee) makes that class a mutable struct with a Ptr{Cvoid} handle, constructors that call the factories, and method proxies. Its finalizer is a deleter function for the class or the returned type, or the class's D0 when no class in the library derives from it. The struct replaces the class's layout struct and its ManagedX.","title":"Idiomatic wrapper generation"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Compiler","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/Compiler.jl\n\nThe Compiler module handles the translation of C/C++ source code into LLVM IR and shared libraries. It oversees the entire build pipeline from dependency management down to IR optimization.","title":"Compiler"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Build-pipeline","page":"Internals","text":"Auto-discovery and dependency resolution: Scans the project directory, resolving file paths and external git/local dependencies to merge into the build graph.\nPre-processing (shims and templates): Dynamically generates C/C++ shim files for configured macros and explicitly instantiates templates based on replibuild.toml settings. This allows normally invisible constructs to manifest in the final binary and DWARF metadata. Macro shims are pinned to default symbol visibility, and a header-collision guard verifies each direct shim #include resolves inside the project/dependency tree — the shim TU lives under the build cache, so a bare include could otherwise fall through the -I path to a system-installed header at a different version and silently bake wrong macro values.\nCompilation to LLVM IR: Translates source code into .ll text format — .c via the JLL clang, .cpp via system clang++. The per-file cache is keyed on source mtime plus a compile fingerprint (flags, defines, include dirs, LLVM version, target triple); config changes can never silently reuse stale IR.\nIR transformation and sanitization: Strips attributes incompatible with Julia's internal LLVM JIT, removes va_start/va_end intrinsics from varargs function bodies (varargs are routed through true-variadic @ccall wrapper generation), and cleans mismatched debug metadata.\nLink / optimize / assemble: For C, these steps run in-process on Julia's resident libLLVM — LLVM.link! for linking, the new pass manager (default<O…>) for optimization, and in-process bitcode assembly — version-matched to the JLL clang that emitted the IR. A failure is a hard error; [link] fallback = true selects the external llvm-link/opt pipeline instead. C++ always uses the external pipeline.\nStatic promotion (_promote_statics_libllvm, C in-process bucket, [link] promote_statics): see below.\nCodegen: The final .ll → .so step shells to clang/clang++.","title":"Build pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Static-promotion","page":"Internals","text":"Post-optimization and pre-codegen, every function or global that a Tier-1 bitcode slice may bind by declare but that cannot reach the .so's dynamic symbol table is renamed to an exported __rb_<lib>_<name> with external linkage and default visibility. The test is \"not exportable\", not \"internal linkage\": default<O…> runs no internalize pass, so it covers both internal/private linkage (file-local statics) and external-linkage-but-hidden/protected symbols — Lua's LUAI_FUNC functions and LUAI_DDEF tables are exactly the latter shape. Only internal constants stay internal: no symbol exists for a slice to bind, and read-only data has no divergence class.\n\nThe rename happens on the one module (<name>_abi.ll) that becomes both the .so and the slice source, so the two are bit-identical by construction — which is what removes the whole-module path's duplicated-static failure class rather than papering over it. The old→new map lands in compilation_metadata.json under promoted_symbols, and extract_symbols_from_binary filters __rb_* so promoted statics never surface as wrappable API.","title":"Static promotion"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Metadata-extraction","page":"Internals","text":"At build time the compiler also extracts DWARF metadata into compilation_metadata.json (functions, struct definitions, enums, globals). DIE parsing is depth-aware: readelf DIE headers carry the tree depth, and member/enumerator/inheritance/template DIEs at depth d attribute to the type last seen at depth d−1 — so nested type definitions interleaved between members (routine clang output) cannot steal subsequent members from the enclosing class. The recorded emitter version is the compiler that actually produced the IR, not whichever llvm-config happens to be on the PATH.","title":"Metadata extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Configuration-Manager","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/ConfigurationManager.jl\n\nThe single source of truth for all build settings. Handles TOML parsing, validation, and merging into a typed RepliBuildConfig struct.","title":"Configuration Manager"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Discovery","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/Discovery.jl\n\nScans the filesystem to identify C/C++ source files, headers, and dependencies. Auto-detects project language (:c vs :cpp) from the scanned source extensions and sets wrap.language accordingly in the generated replibuild.toml.","title":"Discovery"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#DWARFParser","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/DWARFParser.jl\n\nParses llvm-dwarfdump output to extract structured type information from compiled binaries. This is the bridge between C++ debug metadata and Julia wrapper generation.","title":"DWARFParser"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Data-structures","page":"Internals","text":"Type Fields Role\nClassInfo name, vtable_ptr_offset, base_classes, base_offsets, virtual_bases, virtual_methods, members, size Complete class/struct description with byte-level layout, inheritance chain (subobject offsets), and virtual-base flags\nVtableInfo classes, vtable_addresses, method_addresses Aggregate metadata for all classes in a binary\nVirtualMethod name, mangled_name, slot, return_type, parameters Single virtual method with the slot index in its declaring class's primary vtable\nMemberInfo name, type_name, offset Struct field with byte offset from struct base","title":"Data structures"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Extraction-targets","page":"Internals","text":"DWARF Tag Extracted Data\nDW_TAG_class_type / DW_TAG_structure_type Class/struct name, byte size, members, virtual methods, inheritance\nDW_TAG_member Field name, type, DW_AT_data_member_location (byte offset)\nDW_TAG_subprogram (with virtual flag) Virtual method name, mangled name, vtable slot (DW_AT_vtable_elem_location)\nDW_TAG_inheritance Base class with subobject offset; for virtual bases, the vtable-relative offset expression parsed into vbase_vtable_offset\nDW_TAG_enumeration_type Enum definitions\nDW_TAG_union_type Union layout\nDW_TAG_variable Global variables\nDW_TAG_typedef Type aliases","title":"Extraction targets"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#JLCSIRGenerator","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/IRGen/JLCSIRGenerator.jl, src/IRGen/ir_gen/\n\nTransforms parsed DWARF metadata (VtableInfo) into MLIR source text in the JLCS dialect. The generated IR is then parsed and either JIT-compiled by MLIRNative (Tier 2 JIT) or written to disk and AOT-compiled by ThunkBuilder (Tier 2 AOT). Both paths share this module — there is no separate AOT IR generator.","title":"JLCSIRGenerator"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Submodules","page":"Internals","text":"Module Source Input Output\nTypeUtils src/IRGen/ir_gen/TypeUtils.jl C++ type string MLIR type string (f64, i32, !llvm.ptr, etc.)\nStructGen src/IRGen/ir_gen/StructGen.jl struct metadata Struct type aliases + registration IR; aligned-vs-packed LLVM struct type strings; members laid out at their DWARF offsets with explicit padding and verified against a Julia mirror of LLVM's abiSize/abiAlign, degrading to a correctly-sized opaque region when they cannot be; packed structs nested by value in other struct bodies are inlined as byte-identical LLVM literals\nFunctionGen src/IRGen/ir_gen/FunctionGen.jl function or virtual method metadata external func.func private @mangled decl + public func.func @mangled_thunk wrapper with llvm.emit_c_interface; scope-RAII temporaries for non-trivial by-value class params\nArrayViewGen src/IRGen/ir_gen/ArrayViewGen.jl fixed-size primitive array members Zero-copy get/set thunks through jlcs.load/store_array_element\nSTLContainerGen src/IRGen/ir_gen/STLContainerGen.jl STL method metadata Accessor thunks for size(), data(), etc.","title":"Submodules"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Generation-flow","page":"Internals","text":"generate_jlcs_ir(vtinfo, metadata; needed_symbols) produces a complete MLIR module:\n\nStruct aliases + registration: type aliases for all extracted structs (packed structs as !jlcs.c_struct, padded structs as !llvm.struct with packed members inlined as LLVM literals)\nType info operations: jlcs.type_info for each class with non-empty members, carrying the DWARF-resolved destructor and the base/virtual-base tables\nFunction thunks: func.func @mangled_thunk wrappers carrying llvm.emit_c_interface — filtered by needed_symbols (the wrapper's thunk manifest, i.e. dead-thunk elimination). Each body unpacks %args_ptr (ciface convention), emits jlcs.marshal_arg for packed-struct parameters, jlcs.scope copy-construct/destruct brackets for non-trivial by-value class parameters, jlcs.ffe_call / jlcs.try_call (per-function noexcept routing) or jlcs.vcall (virtual instance methods with scalar/pointer signatures), and jlcs.marshal_ret for packed-struct returns\nSTL container thunks: Accessor thunks for detected STL containers (size, data, push_back, etc.)\nArray-view thunks: rank-1 strided accessors for fixed-size primitive array members","title":"Generation flow"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Slicer","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/IRGen/Slicer.jl\n\nPer-function bitcode slicing for Tier 1, on Julia's resident libLLVM. slice_library(abi_ll; targets, cache_dir) parses the promoted module once and clones it per target (LLVMCloneModule), then strips the clone to declarations: LLVMFunctionDeleteBody for every reached function, LLVMSetInitializer2(gv, NULL) for reached mutable and external constant globals, internalize + globaldce for everything unreached. Internal constants are embedded rather than declared. Every slice is verified before it is returned, and results are cached content-addressed under <cache>/slices/.\n\nThe closure is one level deep by construction — a declared function contributes no edges of its own — so slice size tracks the target function, not the library. lua_gettop cuts 15.8 MB down to 2.8 KB; luaL_openlibs lands at 6 KB. This is why max_slice_kb is a tripwire rather than a tuning knob.\n\nAnything the Slicer cannot slice correctly comes back as a refusal with a reason, never as silently-wrong IR: a variadic target, a blockaddress into a body being deleted, alias/ifunc, or an unpromoted module (the fail-loud guard against slicing _opt.ll by mistake). Softer shapes come back as hazard flags for the generator's gate — :setjmp_family, :varargs_callee, :noinline, :weak, :inline_asm, :module_asm.\n\nEach SliceResult also records the symbols the slice declares, post-DCE and excluding intrinsics. _tier1_preflight! in the C generator dlopens the .so RTLD_GLOBAL and dlsyms each one — the exact lookup ORC will perform at first call — because an unresolved declare does not raise: ORC prints Symbols not found: [...] and then blocks forever. A miss demotes that function to ccall; a .so that will not dlopen disables Tier 1 for the whole wrap.","title":"Slicer"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#ThunkBuilder","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/ThunkBuilder.jl\n\nAOT compilation path for Tier 2 thunks. When aot_thunks = true in replibuild.toml, wrap runs this module as its last step (Wrapper.wrap_library), with the thunk set the C++ generator has just decided. It drives the same JLCSIRGenerator.generate_jlcs_ir() used by the JIT path, lowers the result with MLIRNative.lower_to_llvm(), emits an object file through MLIRNative.emit_object(), and links it against the user's compiled library (clang/clang++ -shared, rpath'd to the library directory) into a companion shared library named <libname>_thunks.so, holding exactly the thunks the wrapper binds. With [link] enable_lto on it additionally emits and assembles the thunks' own LTO bitcode. An AOT failure is a warning, not a wrap failure: the wrapper is generated again with JIT dispatch.\n\nThe Julia wrapper then calls the AOT thunks rather than JITManager.invoke. There is no MLIR JIT at run time, but libJLCS is still loaded: the thunks import its exception helpers, and on Windows every thunk call goes through its guard frame. A consumer of an AOT wrapper needs libJLCS as well (Build a package on it).","title":"ThunkBuilder"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#MLIRNative","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/IRGen/MLIRNative.jl\n\nLow-level ccall bindings to libJLCS.so, the compiled JLCS MLIR dialect shared library. Provides context management, module parsing, JIT engine creation, LLVM lowering, symbol lookup, and the object/IR emission used by the AOT path. Building the dialect (cd src/mlir && ./build.sh) is required only for the C++/Tier-2 bucket.\n\nTwo behaviours here are load-bearing beyond plain FFI plumbing. parse_module names the parse buffer after a content-hashed file it writes under the library's .debug/mlir/ — MLIR's parser stamps that name onto every op as a FileLineColLoc, and the lowering turns it into the emitted DWARF's DIFile, which is what makes a JIT'd thunk steppable in gdb. And jit_source_path falls back to a temp directory when .debug is unwritable (a read-only install), because losing co-location costs nothing while losing the source view costs the whole capability.\n\nThe dialect itself — its two types, fourteen ops, lowering pass, and calling contract — is documented in ABI Marshalling as Compiler IR.","title":"MLIRNative"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#JITManager","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/IRGen/JITManager.jl\n\nRuntime for Tier 2 dispatch: one MLIR execution engine per wrapped binary (LibraryEngine), held in a process-wide GLOBAL_JIT behind a shared, lock-free thunk cache.","title":"JITManager"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Key-design-points","page":"Internals","text":"Per-library engines. initialize_global_jit(binary_path) is called from each generated module's __init__ and creates (or reuses) the engine for its binary. Multiple wrappers coexist in one session — previously the first wrapper won and the second library's entire Tier 2 silently died, found while composing box2d with pugixml. A per-library initialization failure degrades only that library, and a missing-symbol error names every engine that was searched.\nManifest-driven initialization: initialize_global_jit() reads thunk_manifest.json — the thunks the wrapper actually dispatches to — so dead thunks are never generated. Any initialization failure (including the pre-flight rejection of untranslatable IR types in libJLCS) degrades the module to \"Tier 2 disabled\" with ccall wrappers intact, never a process crash.\nSymbol registration before lowering: the engine is given the library and libJLCS.so as shared libraries, and the C++ runtime EH symbols (__gxx_personality_v0, __cxa_begin_catch, __cxa_end_catch) plus the jlcs_* exception helpers are registered explicitly, since JIT'd landing pads reference them by name.\nLock-free hot path: _lookup_cached() reads from an @atomic snapshot of the symbol dictionary with no locking. The cache is published copy-on-write — a fresh dict is built with the new entry and atomically swapped in. Readers always see a stable, immutable snapshot.\nArity specialization: invoke is @generated, emitting arity-specialized code for any argument count — stack-allocated Refs and a fixed-size Ptr{Cvoid}[], allocation-free at every arity. A thunk slot holds a pointer to the argument's storage, so Ref(x) is the right shape for an isbits x; the two kinds that are already an indirection — an AbstractString, and a Base.Ref that is not a Ptr (what a caller passes for a C++ T const& parameter) — are flattened to a raw pointer first and GC-preserved across the call. Both invoke methods share one _arg_marshal_plan, because two copies is how a fix to one silently misses the other.\n@generated return dispatch: _invoke_call resolves at compile time whether the return type is a primitive (direct ccall return) or a struct (sret buffer allocation). An unresolved Any return fails loudly with the actual cause instead of corrupting memory.\nException propagation: After every Tier 2 call, _check_pending_exception() polls the thread-local exception buffer set by jlcs.try_call lowering. If a C++ exception was caught during the call, a CxxException is thrown with the original what() message.","title":"Key design points"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Calling-convention","page":"Internals","text":"All Tier 2 functions use a unified ciface calling convention:\n\nReturn Signature\nScalar T ciface(void** args_ptr)\nStruct void ciface(T* sret, void** args_ptr)\nVoid void ciface(void** args_ptr)","title":"Calling convention"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Debug","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Debug/Debug.jl\n\nStatic inspection of what the Tier-2 pipeline actually emitted, for a package this process never built. thunks(pkg) lists the thunk symbols; mlir_body(pkg, symbol) prints the generated dialect; disassemble(pkg; symbol=…) shells to objdump -dS so dialect ops and machine code interleave; dwarf(pkg; section=…) shows the address → MLIR-line table; walk(pkg, symbol) does the common combination in one call.\n\nThe object file it disassembles only exists when the JIT's object cache was enabled, and MLIR requires that at engine-creation time — so it is read from REPLIBUILD_JIT_OBJDUMP before the wrapper loads, not passed as an argument. Nothing here links gdb or LLVM: it shells to objdump and llvm-dwarfdump. See Debugging a thunk for the live-process counterpart.","title":"Debug"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#BuildBridge","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/BuildBridge.jl\n\nLow-level compiler driver that shells out to clang, clang++, llvm-link, opt, llvm-as, and nm. All subprocess invocations go through this module, providing a single point of control for toolchain interaction. It serves the C++ pipeline and the C bucket's [link] fallback = true escape hatch; the default C path links and optimizes in-process on Julia's libLLVM (see Compiler).","title":"BuildBridge"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#LLVMEnvironment","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/LLVMEnvironment.jl\n\nDetects the system LLVM/Clang toolchain by searching standard paths and version-suffixed binaries. Falls back to LLVM_full_jll when no system toolchain is found. Caches results in ~/.replibuild/toolchain.toml with a 24-hour TTL.","title":"LLVMEnvironment"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#EnvironmentDoctor","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/EnvironmentDoctor.jl\n\ncheck_environment() validates both toolchain buckets: the C bucket (JLL clang + Julia's resident libLLVM — no external install required) and the C++/Tier 2 bucket (system LLVM/MLIR 21+, Clang, mlir-tblgen, CMake 3.20+, and libJLCS.so). Returns a ToolchainStatus struct indicating which tiers are available, with OS-specific install instructions for missing components.","title":"EnvironmentDoctor"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#DependencyResolver","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/DependencyResolver.jl\n\nProcesses the [dependencies] table from replibuild.toml. Supports three dependency types:\n\nType Mechanism\ngit Shallow clone (--depth 1) into .replibuild_cache/deps/<name>/; re-fetches on tag change\nlocal Scanned in-place; no copying\nsystem pkg-config --cflags to inject include paths\n\nThe exclude list is applied after scanning. Resolved source files merge into the compilation graph before the compile step.","title":"DependencyResolver"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#PackageRegistry","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/PackageRegistry.jl\n\nLocal package registry at ~/.replibuild/registry/. Provides:\n\nregister() — Store a project's build configuration\nuse() — Build + wrap + load a locally registered name, with artifact caching in ~/.replibuild/builds/<hash>/. No Hub fallback — a missing name is an error\nsearch() — Query the Hub index by name, description, tags, or language (browse only; does not register)\nlist_registry() — Print all registered packages with hash, source, and build status\nunregister() — Remove a package and clean cached builds\n\nThe build-cache key (hash_config) covers the TOML, sources, headers, and project git HEAD plus the generator fingerprint — RepliBuild's own version and git revision — so upgrading RepliBuild invalidates wrappers produced by older codegen. Cached wrappers resolve their .so sibling-first via @__DIR__, with the baked absolute path as fallback.\n\nThe REPLIBUILD_HOME environment variable overrides the default registry location; REPLIBUILD_HUB_URL points Hub operations at a private mirror.","title":"PackageRegistry"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#STLWrappers","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Wrapper/Cpp/STLWrappers.jl\n\nDetects STL container types (std::vector, std::string, std::map, etc.) in DWARF metadata and generates accessor functions. These are used by the MLIR IR generator (src/IRGen/ir_gen/STLContainerGen.jl) to produce JIT thunks for STL container methods.","title":"STLWrappers"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#ASTWalker","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/ASTWalker.jl\n\nClang.jl-based AST walker for enum extraction. Handles enum class, hex values, namespaces, and other constructs that are difficult to extract reliably from DWARF alone. Replaces the earlier regex-based approach.","title":"ASTWalker"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#ClangJLBridge","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/ClangJLBridge.jl\n\nIntegration module for Clang.jl header parsing. Used by the wrapper generator when use_clang_jl = true to supplement DWARF metadata with AST-level information.","title":"ClangJLBridge"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Scaffold","page":"Internals","text":"Source: src/Builder/PackageRegistry.jl (scaffold_package function)\n\nGenerates a distributable Julia package from a registered RepliBuild project. The scaffolded package includes the compiled shared library, generated wrapper module, and a standard Julia Project.toml — ready for Pkg.add().","title":"Scaffold"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.extract_symbols-Tuple{String, RepliBuild.Wrapper.TypeRegistry}","page":"Internals","text":"extract_symbols(binary_path::String, registry::TypeRegistry; demangle::Bool=true, method::Symbol=:nm)\n\nExtract symbols from compiled binary using specified method.\n\nMethods\n\n:nm - Fast, basic symbol extraction\n:objdump - Detailed with debug info (TODO)\n:all - Try all methods and merge (TODO)\n\nReturns vector of SymbolInfo objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.extract_symbols"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.is_enum_like-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"is_enum_like(cpp_type::String)::Bool\n\nCheck if a C++ type looks like an enum based on naming conventions. Returns false — enum detection is unreliable by name alone (uppercase names are more likely structs/classes). Real enum identification uses DWARF metadata via _is_enum_type() in DispatchLogic.jl which checks __enum__ prefixed keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.is_enum_like"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.is_function_pointer_like-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"is_function_pointer_like(cpp_type::String)::Bool\n\nCheck if a C++ type contains function pointer syntax. Looks for patterns: (name), ()(args), (^name) (blocks)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.is_function_pointer_like"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.is_struct_like-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"is_struct_like(cpp_type::String)::Bool\n\nCheck if a C++ type looks like a struct/class based on naming conventions. Structs typically: start with uppercase, contain only alphanumeric + underscore.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.is_struct_like"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.wrap_basic-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, String}","page":"Internals","text":"wrap_basic(config::RepliBuildConfig, library_path::String; generate_docs::Bool=true)\n\nGenerate basic Julia wrapper from binary symbols only (no headers required).\n\nQuality: ~40% - Conservative types, placeholder signatures, requires manual refinement. Use when: Headers not available, quick prototyping, binary-only distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.wrap_basic"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.wrap_library-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, String}","page":"Internals","text":"wrap_library(config::RepliBuildConfig, library_path::String;\n             headers::Vector{String}=String[],\n             generate_tests::Bool=false,\n             generate_docs::Bool=true,\n             aot_compile=nothing)\n\nGenerate Julia wrapper for compiled library.\n\nWraps from the DWARF metadata (compilation_metadata.json) that build writes beside the library, and refuses when that file is missing.\n\nWith [compile] aot_thunks = true, a C++ wrap ends by compiling the thunks its call sites bind into lib<name>_thunks.<ext> (ThunkBuilder.build_aot_thunks), from the thunk set the generator has just decided. When that compile fails, the wrapper is generated again with JIT dispatch.\n\nArguments\n\nconfig: RepliBuildConfig with wrapper settings\nlibrary_path: Path to compiled library (.so, .dylib, .dll)\nheaders: Header files the Clang.jl walk starts from. NOT unused — this is what scopes that walk; empty means \"discover every header at the top level of every include dir\", which is the right default only when the include dir is the API. Overrides [wrap] headers, which is the same setting from a manifest. Inert when use_clang_jl = false.\ngenerate_tests: Generate test file (default: false, TODO)\ngenerate_docs: Include comprehensive documentation (default: true)\naot_compile: The AOT stage, called as aot_compile(config, library_path; needed_symbols) -> Bool. Defaults to ThunkBuilder.build_aot_thunks; tests pass a stand-in.\n\nReturns\n\nPath to generated Julia wrapper file\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.wrap_library"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.ParamInfo","page":"Internals","text":"ParamInfo\n\nInformation about a function parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.ParamInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.SymbolInfo","page":"Internals","text":"SymbolInfo\n\nComprehensive information about a symbol extracted from binary/headers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.SymbolInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.TypeRegistry","page":"Internals","text":"TypeRegistry\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.TypeRegistry"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Wrapper.TypeStrictness","page":"Internals","text":"TypeStrictness\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Wrapper.TypeStrictness"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.assemble_bitcode-Tuple{String, String}","page":"Internals","text":"Assemble LLVM IR text (.ll) to bitcode (.bc). Uses Julia's own libLLVM via the C API (LLVMParseIRInContext + LLVMWriteBitcodeToFile) to guarantee bitcode version compatibility with Base.llvmcall. Falls back to Clangunifiedjll, then system llvm-as.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.assemble_bitcode"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.build_type_registry-Tuple{Vector{Dict{String, Any}}}","page":"Internals","text":"Build type registry from functions. Collects all unique types used in the codebase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.build_type_registry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.compile_project-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Complete compilation workflow: discover sources, compile, link, create binary. This is the main entry point for building a project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.compile_project"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.compile_to_ir-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}}","page":"Internals","text":"Compile multiple C++ files to LLVM IR (with parallel support). Returns vector of IR file paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.compile_to_ir"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.compute_project_hash-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Compute a content hash for the entire project: replibuild.toml + source files + headers + git HEAD + the toolchain (_toolchain_identity). Returns a hex string that changes when anything relevant changes, and only then: it is SHA-256, so it is the same under every Julia release (see _key_feed!).\n\ntoolchain is a seam for tests; a build always takes _toolchain_identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.compute_project_hash"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.create_executable","page":"Internals","text":"Create executable from LLVM IR. Returns path to executable file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.create_executable"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.create_library","page":"Internals","text":"Create shared library from LLVM IR. Returns path to the library file.\n\nper_tu_ir is the UNMERGED per-translation-unit IR list, used only if clang refuses the (usually merged) ir_files for running out of source locations — see the fallback inside. Callers that already hold that list should pass it; without it the fallback cannot run, because splitting a merged module is not possible after the fact.\n\nextra_link_args go on the link line after [link] link_libraries, on every attempt. The graph build passes a target's own link inputs there (absolute library paths and linker flags the build system resolved, which -l cannot spell), its relative rpaths and, on Windows, its import library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.create_library"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.dwarf_type_to_julia-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Internals","text":"Comprehensive C/C++ type to Julia type mapping. Handles all standard C/C++ types including sized integers, pointers, and qualifiers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.dwarf_type_to_julia"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_class_name-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"Extract class name from method signature (only considers '::' in the function-name prefix). Example: \"Calculator::compute(int, int)\" -> \"Calculator\" Example: \"sumvector(std::vector<int> const&)\"  -> \"\"  (free function) Example: \"bool ggufreader::read<int>(...)\"     -> \"ggufreader\"  (NOT \"bool ggufreader\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_class_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_compilation_metadata-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}, String}","page":"Internals","text":"Extract compilation metadata from source files and binary. This is the core of automatic wrapper generation!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_compilation_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_dwarf_return_types-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"Extract return types and struct definitions from DWARF debug info. Returns: (returntypes, structdefs, globalvars, typedeftable, record_abi)\n\nreturntypes: Dict{mangledname => {ctype, juliatype, size}}\nstructdefs: Dict{structname => {members: [{name, type, offset}]}}\nrecordabi: Dict{recordname => {byte_size, pass, empty}} for by-value records\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_dwarf_return_types"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_function_name-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"Extract function name from demangled signature. Example: \"Calculator::compute(int, int, char)\" -> \"compute\" Example: \"sumvector(std::vector<int, std::allocator<int> > const&)\" -> \"sumvector\" Example: \"bool gguf_reader::read<std::vector<int> >(...)\" -> \"read<std::vector<int> >\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_function_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_mangled_name-Tuple{String, String}","page":"Internals","text":"Extract mangled symbol name from nm output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_mangled_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_stl_method_symbols-Tuple{String, Vector{String}}","page":"Internals","text":"Extract STL method symbols from the compiled binary. Uses nm to find mangled names of template-instantiated STL methods, keeping only the ones the loader can bind (see _stl_methods_from_nm). Returns Dict mapping normalized container type -> vector of method info dicts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_stl_method_symbols"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_symbols_from_binary-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"Extract symbol information from compiled binary using nm. Returns vector of symbol dictionaries with mangled/demangled names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.extract_symbols_from_binary"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.generate_macro_shims-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}}","page":"Internals","text":"Generate a C/C++ shim file to instantiate requested macros as typed functions so they appear in the DWARF metadata and can be wrapped.\n\n[wrap] shim_defines go at the top of the same file, before its #includes: with STB_IMAGE_IMPLEMENTATION defined there, the file compiles a single-header library's implementation section into the library. The file is written when either key has entries; with no macros it holds only the defines and includes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.generate_macro_shims"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.generate_template_instantiations-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}}","page":"Internals","text":"Generate a dummy C++ file to explicitly instantiate requested templates so they appear in the DWARF metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.generate_template_instantiations"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.get_type_size-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Internals","text":"Get type size in bytes from C/C++ type name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.get_type_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.infer_return_type-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"Infer return type from demangled function signature using pattern matching. This is a fallback when DWARF debug info is unavailable for a given function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.infer_return_type"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.ingest_library-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"ingest_library(config) -> String\n\nBYOB (\"bring your own build\") path: skip the whole Clang/LLVM compile pipeline and ingest a pre-built .so produced by upstream's own build system. Runs DWARF metadata extraction on the binary and produces compilation_metadata.json next to a copy of the library inside the project's output dir, so wrap() finds it where it expects.\n\nIngested libraries dispatch through Tier 3 (ccall) only — there's no LTO bitcode to enable Tier 1 llvmcall, and no per-file IR for Tier 2 thunks.\n\nRequires config.ingest !== nothing (set by a [ingest] section in replibuild.toml).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.ingest_library"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.is_project_cache_valid-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"is_project_cache_valid(config; hash = compute_project_hash(config)) -> Bool\n\nCheck if the project-level cache is valid. Returns true if all artifacts exist and the content hash matches, meaning the build can be skipped entirely.\n\nhash is the current project hash. compile_project passes the one it took before the build read anything, and saves that same value when the build succeeds (see there).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.is_project_cache_valid"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.link_optimize_ir-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}, String}","page":"Internals","text":"Link multiple LLVM IR files and optimize. Returns linked IR path (String) or original IR files (Vector{String}) on llvm-link failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.link_optimize_ir"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.needs_recompile","page":"Internals","text":"Check if a source file needs recompilation.\n\nCache hit requires BOTH the IR being newer than the source (mtime) AND the compile fingerprint matching the one that produced it (<ir>.key sidecar). A missing key (cache from before fingerprinting) forces a recompile so the key is established. Pass compile_fingerprint=\"\" to skip the fingerprint check (legacy mtime-only behavior).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.needs_recompile"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.parse_function_signatures","page":"Internals","text":"Parse function signatures from symbol information. Infers parameter types and return types from demangled names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.parse_function_signatures"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.parse_parameters","page":"Internals","text":"Parse parameter types from demangled signature. Example: \"add(int, int)\" -> [{\"type\": \"int\", \"julia_type\": \"Cint\"}, ...] Template-aware: angle brackets in nested types do not confuse the splitter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.parse_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.sanitize_ir_for_julia-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"sanitize_ir_for_julia(ir_text::String) -> String\n\nSanitize LLVM IR text for compatibility with Julia's internal LLVM (18). Strips LLVM 19–22 attributes/instructions, debug metadata, and converts varargs function bodies to extern declarations (vastart/vaend can't be JIT-compiled). Uses inlinehint (not alwaysinline) to avoid recursive inliner explosion on large modules.\n\nCoverage by LLVM version:   19: GEP nuw, #dbg* records, inrange(), captures(), deadonunwind,       initializes(), allocptr, icmp samesign, range(), trunc nuw/nsw,       zext/uitofp nneg   20: GEP nusw, or disjoint, fptrunc/fpext fast-math flags, ptrtoaddr→ptrtoint   21: (covered by 19 patterns — captures/deadon_unwind/allocptr landed here)   22: ptrtoaddr instruction\n\nThis is the single source of truth for IR compatibility — used by both the C source LTO pipeline and the MLIR AOT thunks pipeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.sanitize_ir_for_julia"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.save_compilation_metadata-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}, String}","page":"Internals","text":"Save compilation metadata to JSON file next to binary. This enables automatic wrapper generation!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.save_compilation_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.save_project_hash-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"save_project_hash(config; hash = compute_project_hash(config))\n\nSave the project hash after a successful build. compile_project passes the hash it took before the build began, never one taken now: the build compiled the files as they were then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.save_project_hash"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Compiler.verify_wrap_surface-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, String, Union{Nothing, String}}","page":"Internals","text":"verify_wrap_surface(config, binary_path, metadata_path)\n\nGuard: every function the wrapper is about to emit must be resolvable in the built library's dynamic symbol table, under the exact symbol name the wrapper will ccall.\n\nThe wrap surface and the runtime surface come from two different symbol tables and nothing has ever checked that they agree. extract_compilation_metadata correlates DWARF against extract_symbols_from_binary, which reads nm -g — the full .symtab. The generated wrapper resolves through dlsym, which sees only .dynsym (extract_symbols_nm uses nm -D for exactly this reason). A symbol present in one and absent from the other is wrapped, documented, exported — and fails at the first call. Today the two agree everywhere; the check is cheap and the disagreement is silent, which is the whole argument for asserting it.\n\npromote_statics is what makes them disagree. It treats hidden visibility as \"internal\" (_promote_statics_libllvm: needs_promotion tests linkage OR hidden/protected visibility) and renames such definitions to __rb_<lib>_<name>, which by design drops them from the wrap surface — DWARF still names the original, so the renamed symbol has no DWARF match. That is correct while hidden means what the library's author meant by it.\n\nCompile with -fvisibility=hidden and it stops meaning that. Every function the library did not annotate visibility(\"default\") goes hidden — including public API whose export macro is a bare extern, which is lua's LUA_API and is common. Then promotion renames the entire API to __rb_* and the wrapper generates nothing, from a build that succeeded and warned about nothing. Reproduced 2026-09-15 on a library-free three-function fixture through the JLL clang: annotated_public (carrying __attribute__((visibility(\"default\")))) survives under its own name; bare_extern_public and uses_helper both come back as __rb_fix_*. The first of those is API.\n\ntest_cache_invalidation.jl records the same interaction from the other side — \"internal_helper under that flag is external-linkage-and-hidden (the LUAIFUNC shape), so static promotion re-exports it as `__rbcachetestinternalhelper` … assert names, never export counts, on anything downstream of promotion.\" This guard asserts names. The one count it reads is the degenerate zero.\n\nTwo rules, neither carrying a threshold:\n\nR1 — collapse. The wrap surface is empty while promotion renamed symbols. That is the failure above, observed rather than inferred: something was compiled, and all of it left under a different name.\nR2 — reachability. Every wrappable function appears in the dynamic table under its mangled name. Measured across all 31 built Hub packages 2026-09-15 — 0 offenders, including every C++ package (constructor clones and weak template instantiations are all present in .dynsym), so a non-empty offender list is news rather than noise.\n\nRuns BEFORE save_project_hash: a build that fails this must not leave a cache marker claiming it succeeded, or the next run skips straight past the failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Compiler.verify_wrap_surface"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.create_default_config","page":"Internals","text":"Create a default configuration and save to file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.create_default_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_build_path-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Get full build path (project_root + paths.build)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_build_path"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_cache_path-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Get full cache path (project_root + cache.directory)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_cache_path"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_compile_flags-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"The flags every translation unit is compiled with — [compile] flags plus whatever the typed keys above them resolve to.\n\nvisibility = \"hidden\" is folded in HERE rather than at the compile call, because this is the list compute_compile_fingerprint hashes: fold it in later and flipping the key leaves the per-file IR cache valid, so the next build serves IR compiled under the other visibility and the wrapper's API silently belongs to the previous setting.\n\nIdempotent against the legacy spelling — a package carrying -fvisibility=hidden in flags directly (argon2, box2d3 and zstd all predate the key) gets one flag, not two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_compile_flags"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_include_dirs-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Get all include directories (from config)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_include_dirs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_library_name-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Get library output name (uses config or auto-generates)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_library_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_module_name-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Get wrapper module name (uses config or auto-generates)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_module_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_output_path-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Get full output path (project_root + paths.output)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_output_path"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_source_files-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Get all C++ source files (from config)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.get_source_files"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.is_cache_enabled-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Should use cache?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.is_cache_enabled"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.is_parallel_enabled-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Should run parallel compilation?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.is_parallel_enabled"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.is_stage_enabled-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"Check if a stage is in the workflow\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.is_stage_enabled"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.load_config","page":"Internals","text":"Load RepliBuildConfig from TOML file. This is the ONLY function that parses TOML - all other modules use this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.load_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.merge_compile_flags-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}}","page":"Internals","text":"Merge compile flags into config (creates new config). Used for runtime overrides like: compile(sources, flags=[\"-O3\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.merge_compile_flags"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.print_config-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Print configuration summary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.print_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.save_config-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Save configuration to TOML file. Only saves user-configurable settings (not runtime data).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.save_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.validate_config!-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Validate config and throw error if invalid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.validate_config!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.validate_config-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Validate configuration and return list of errors (empty if valid).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.validate_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.with_discovery_results-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig}","page":"Internals","text":"Update both source files and include dirs (creates new config). Used after discovery completes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.with_discovery_results"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.with_include_dirs-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}}","page":"Internals","text":"Update include directories in config (creates new config).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.with_include_dirs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.with_source_files-Tuple{RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig, Vector{String}}","page":"Internals","text":"Update source files in config (creates new config). Used after discovery finds C++ sources.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.with_source_files"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.BinaryConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [binary] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.BinaryConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.CacheConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [cache] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.CacheConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.CompileConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [compile] section.\n\nvisibility is :default (unchanged behaviour) or :hidden — the declared form of -fvisibility=hidden. It lives in the config rather than in flags because it is not an ordinary flag: it decides what the generated wrapper's API is. Under :hidden every definition the library did not annotate visibility(\"default\") stops reaching .dynsym, so the wrap surface narrows to the set upstream declared public — free and exact for a library that annotates, and total erasure for one that does not (Compiler.verify_wrap_surface refuses that build; VisibilityProbe.visibility_probe answers which kind a package is BEFORE anyone sets this).\n\nConsumed by get_compile_flags, deliberately: that is what compute_compile_fingerprint hashes, so flipping this key invalidates the per-file IR cache. Appending the flag further downstream — beside the -g in compile_single_to_ir, say — would leave the fingerprint blind to it and happily serve stale IR built under the other visibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.CompileConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.DependenciesConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [dependencies] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.DependenciesConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.DependencyItem","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for a single dependency.\n\ntag is a MUTABLE ref — upstream can force-push a tag to different content, and a plain git checkout <tag> would fetch it with no signal. commit is the optional expected 40-hex object name for that ref: when set, DependencyResolver hard-errors if the resolved HEAD differs, which is the only check that survives a cache wipe (the sidecar marker cannot, since clean() deletes it along with the clone). Leave commit empty to keep the old trust-the-tag behaviour.\n\nsubmodules (git only) names the superproject's submodule paths to fetch, sorted and deduplicated at load; empty means none, never all. The superproject's tree records each submodule's commit, so commit pins them too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.DependencyItem"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.DiscoveryConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [discovery] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.DiscoveryConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.GraphConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [graph] section.\n\nPresence of this section selects graph mode: the project is a CMake project of several libraries, whose build graph CMake's File API reports at configure time. build configures it (CMakeGraph.cmake_graph), runs the build-time steps that write its generated sources (GraphGenerators), compiles and links every library itself (GraphBuild) and extracts each library's metadata; wrap writes one wrapper per library and a graph module that includes them (GraphMode).\n\nsource: the CMake project directory (the one holding CMakeLists.txt). With no dependency, absolute and /-separated; relative in the TOML, against the project root. With a dependency, the directory inside that dependency's checkout, relative and /-separated (\".\": the checkout itself); GraphMode.source_dir joins it to the checkout.\ndependency: the [dependencies.<name>] entry whose checkout is the CMake project (a git, archive or local dependency), or \"\" when source is a directory of the project. build resolves it as a single library's build does, pinned by commit or sha256, before configuring it.\ntargets: the library targets to build and wrap, by name or File API id; the shared libraries they link come too. Empty: every library target.\noptions: extra configure arguments, after RepliBuild's own.\nbuild_type: CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE of the configure; empty omits it.\ncmake_dir, out_dir: where the configure runs (<[paths] build>/cmake) and where the libraries, their wrappers and the graph module land ([paths] output). Derived at load, absolute and /-separated, never written back to a TOML.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.GraphConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.IngestConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [ingest] section.\n\nPresence of this section flips RepliBuild from source-build mode (compile via Clang/LLVM) to ingest mode: the user supplies a pre-built .so (built by upstream's own build system), and RepliBuild only runs DWARF metadata extraction + wrapping. Ingested libraries dispatch through Tier 3 (ccall) only — no LTO bitcode, no Tier 1 llvmcall.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.IngestConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.LLVMConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [llvm] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.LLVMConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.LinkConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [link] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.LinkConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.PathsConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [paths] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.PathsConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.ProjectConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [project] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.ProjectConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Main immutable configuration structure. All modules receive this struct - it's the single source of truth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RepliBuildConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RustConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [rust] section.\n\nPresence of this section selects the Rust front end: the project is a Cargo crate, and cargo and rustc compile AND link it (RustBuild). RepliBuild sets the crate type, the debug info, the optimization level and the IR emission, and reads back what it reads from a clang build: the library's dynamic symbol table, its DWARF, and the define lines of the IR rustc emitted. The crate's source is never edited and needs no extern \"C\".\n\nmanifest: the crate's Cargo.toml. With no dependency, absolute and /-separated; relative in the TOML, against the project root. With a dependency, the file inside that dependency's checkout, relative and /-separated; RustBuild.manifest_path joins it to the checkout.\ndependency: the [dependencies.<name>] entry whose checkout holds the crate (a git, archive or local dependency), or \"\" when the crate is in the project. build resolves it, pinned by commit or sha256, before cargo is asked anything. This is the shape of a Hub recipe.\npackage: the package to wrap, by its Cargo name, when the manifest is a workspace or has several packages; \"\" is the manifest's own package.\nopt_level: rustc's opt-level for the wrapped crate, 0 to 3. Above 0, rustc gives a small pub fn no symbol of its own (it is compiled into its callers instead), so the build keeps incremental compilation on, which turns that off; RustBuild says how that was measured.\nfeatures, default_features: the Cargo features the crate is built with.\ntarget_dir: cargo's target directory, <[paths] build>/rust/target. Derived at load, absolute and /-separated, never written back to a TOML.\ninstantiate: generic functions to compile for particular types, each a Rust expression that names one function (geo::largest::<f64>, geo::Pair::<i32>::sum). A generic function exists only where something uses it with a type, and no compile of the crate says which types its user wants: this is the one thing about a Rust crate the compiler cannot be asked. An #[inline] function needs no entry; the build finds those.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.RustConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.TypesConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [types] section - Type validation settings\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.TypesConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.WorkflowConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [workflow] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.WorkflowConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.WrapConfig","page":"Internals","text":"Nested struct for [wrap] section\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ConfigurationManager.WrapConfig"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.Discovery.discover","page":"Internals","text":"discover(target_dir::String=pwd(); force::Bool=false, unsafe::Bool=false, build::Bool=false, wrap::Bool=false) -> String\n\nMain discovery pipeline - scans project and generates configuration.\n\nA directory that holds a Cargo.toml and no replibuild.toml is a Rust crate: cargo is asked what the crate is (RustBuild.discover_project) and the project file names it under [rust]; nothing is scanned. An existing [rust] project is kept as it is, with force too.\n\nProcess:\n\nCheck for existing replibuild.toml (project identified by presence of replibuild.toml)\nScan all files and categorize\nDetect and analyze binaries\nWalk AST dependencies using clang\nGenerate or update replibuild.toml with discovered data\nOptionally build, or build, wrap and register (vendor the build)\n\nArguments\n\ntarget_dir: Project directory (default: current directory)\nforce: Force rediscovery even if replibuild.toml exists\nunsafe: Bypass safety checks (use with extreme caution)\nbuild: Automatically run build() after discovery (default: false)\nwrap: With build, wrap too and then register the project, which vendors the build system-wide and adds it to the Julia environment (default: false)\n\nSafety Features\n\nDiscovery is scoped ONLY to target_dir and subdirectories\nWill not scan outside the project root\nSkips .git, build, node_modules, .cache directories\n\nReturns\n\nPath to generated replibuild.toml file\n\nExamples\n\n# Discover only\ntoml_path = RepliBuild.Discovery.discover()\n\n# Discover and build\ntoml_path = RepliBuild.Discovery.discover(build=true)\n\n# Full pipeline: discover → build → wrap\ntoml_path = RepliBuild.Discovery.discover(build=true, wrap=true)\n\n# Then use the TOML path:\nRepliBuild.build(toml_path)\nRepliBuild.wrap(toml_path)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.Discovery.discover"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.export_vtable_json-Tuple{RepliBuild.DWARFParser.VtableInfo, String}","page":"Internals","text":"export_vtable_json(vtinfo::VtableInfo, output_path::String)\n\nExport vtable information to JSON for inspection or use by other tools.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.export_vtable_json"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.parse_dwarf_output-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"parse_dwarf_output(dwarf_text::String) -> Dict{String, ClassInfo}\n\nParse llvm-dwarfdump output to extract class and vtable information.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.parse_dwarf_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.parse_symbol_table-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"parse_symbol_table(nm_output::String) -> Tuple{Dict{String, UInt64}, Dict{String, UInt64}}\n\nParse nm output to extract vtable and method addresses. Returns (vtableaddresses, methodaddresses).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.parse_symbol_table"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.parse_vtables-Tuple{String}","page":"Internals","text":"parse_vtables(binary_path::String) -> VtableInfo\n\nExtract complete vtable information from a binary using DWARF and symbol table.\n\nArguments\n\nbinary_path: Path to compiled binary with debug info\n\nReturns\n\nVtableInfo containing classes, vtable addresses, and method addresses\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.parse_vtables"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.read_vtable_data-Tuple{String, UInt64, Int64}","page":"Internals","text":"read_vtable_data(binary_path::String, vtable_addr::UInt64, num_entries::Int) -> Vector{UInt64}\n\nRead actual vtable function pointers from binary at given address.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.read_vtable_data"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.ClassInfo","page":"Internals","text":"Information about a C++ class with virtual methods\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.ClassInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.MemberInfo","page":"Internals","text":"Information about a data member (field)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.MemberInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.VirtualMethod","page":"Internals","text":"Information about a virtual method\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.VirtualMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#RepliBuild.DWARFParser.VtableInfo","page":"Internals","text":"Complete vtable information from binary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.DWARFParser.VtableInfo"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#Load-it-anywhere","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"While you are working on a wrapper you load it with include and a path. register makes a finished one loadable by name, from any Julia session:\n\nRepliBuild.register(\"path/to/mathlib/replibuild.toml\")\n\n# any later session\nusing RepliBuild\nM = RepliBuild.use(\"mathlib\")       # by the project's name\n\nusing Mathlib                       # or as a package\n\nNothing is registered until you register it. A new installation has no wrappers, and use does not download any.","title":"Load it anywhere"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#What-register-does","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"It runs build and wrap on the project, where the project is. If the build is up to date this takes no time.\nIt copies the result to ~/.replibuild/builds/, so the registered copy does not change while you keep working on the project.\nIt adds the wrapper to a Julia environment as a package, by default the environment every session loads from (@v1.x). That is what makes using Mathlib work.\n\ndiscover(path; build = true, wrap = true) ends by doing the same.","title":"What register does"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#Choose-the-Julia-environment","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"RepliBuild.register(toml)                          # the default environment\nRepliBuild.register(toml; env = :active)           # the active environment\nRepliBuild.register(toml; env = \"path/to/env\")     # a specific environment\nRepliBuild.register(toml; env = nothing)           # none: only `use` loads it\n\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JULIA_ENV\"] sets the default: default, active, none, or a path. Set it to none while experimenting, so trial wrappers stay out of your environment.","title":"Choose the Julia environment"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#What-use-does","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"use(\"name\") checks whether the project changed since it was registered.\n\nNot changed: it loads the registered copy. Nothing is compiled.\nChanged (the TOML, the sources, the compiler, or RepliBuild itself): it builds and wraps the project again, registers the new result and loads it. You do not call register again.\nThe project folder is gone: it loads the last registered copy and warns.\n\nRepliBuild.use(\"mathlib\"; force_rebuild = true)    # rebuild from nothing first\n\nforce_rebuild = true deletes the project's build/, julia/ and .replibuild_cache/ first, so fetched sources are downloaded again. Use it after a RepliBuild upgrade whose changelog says a fix needs a rebuild.\n\nusing Mathlib loads the copy registered last. After you edit the project, call use(\"mathlib\") once to register the new build, then start a new session.\n\nCall use at the top level, not inside a function that then calls into the module (Call the wrapper).","title":"What use does"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#Edit-and-reload-in-one-session","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"use is also the fastest way to iterate. Edit the TOML or the sources, call use again, and it rebuilds and returns a new module in a few seconds:\n\nM = RepliBuild.use(\"mathlib\")\n# edit replibuild.toml …\nM = RepliBuild.use(\"mathlib\")       # rebuilt; M is the new module\n\nKeep the module use returns. One you got earlier keeps working with the build it was loaded from.\n\nEach rebuild leaves a folder under ~/.replibuild/builds/. Nothing deletes the old ones, so clear that folder out now and then.","title":"Edit and reload in one session"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#List-and-remove","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"RepliBuild.list_registry()\nRepliBuild.unregister(\"mathlib\")\n\nunregister removes the registered copy and the package. It never touches your project. On Windows, a session that has the wrapper loaded keeps its library open: unregister then warns and leaves that folder, which you can delete once the session has ended.\n\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_HOME\"] moves ~/.replibuild somewhere else.\n\nA name that is not registered gives:\n\nPackage 'cjson' not in registry. Use RepliBuild.list_registry() to see available packages,\nor RepliBuild.register(\"path/to/replibuild.toml\") to add one.","title":"List and remove"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#Ready-made-recipes","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"RepliBuild-Hub is a collection of finished replibuild.toml files: lua, sqlite, cJSON, zlib, box2d and others.\n\nRepliBuild.search()              # list them\nRepliBuild.search(\"xml\")         # by keyword\n\nsearch only lists. To use one, clone the Hub and register the recipe:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/obsidianjulua/RepliBuild-Hub\n\nRepliBuild.register(\"RepliBuild-Hub/packages/cjson/replibuild.toml\")\nC = RepliBuild.use(\"cjson\")\n\nregister downloads the library's source at the commit the recipe names and compiles it, which can take minutes for a large library.","title":"Ready-made recipes"},{"category":"section","location":"use/#CMake-projects","page":"Load it anywhere","text":"A CMake project registers as one unit, and use returns its top module: RepliBuild.use(\"libwebp\").Webp.","title":"CMake projects"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"The functions you call. Each takes the path of a replibuild.toml, or of the folder that holds one, and does nothing when its inputs have not changed.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Check-the-machine","page":"API","text":"status.ready is true when C libraries can be wrapped, and status.tier2_ready when C++ libraries can. Set up explains the report.","title":"Check the machine"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Build-and-wrap","page":"API","text":"toml = RepliBuild.discover(\"path/to/project\")   # write replibuild.toml\nRepliBuild.build(toml)                           # compile the library\nRepliBuild.wrap(toml)                            # write julia/<Name>.jl\n\ndiscover(path; build = true, wrap = true) does all three and registers the result.\ndiscover keeps an existing replibuild.toml. discover(path; force = true) rewrites it, keeping only some keys.\nbuild(toml; clean = true) deletes everything built first.\nclean(toml; deps = false) keeps downloaded sources.","title":"Build and wrap"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Register-and-load","page":"API","text":"RepliBuild.register(\"replibuild.toml\")\nM = RepliBuild.use(\"myproject\")\n\nLoad it anywhere explains what each does.\n\nEnvironment variable Effect\nREPLIBUILD_HOME Where registered builds are kept. Default ~/.replibuild.\nREPLIBUILD_JULIA_ENV Which Julia environment register adds packages to: default, active, none, or a path.\nREPLIBUILD_HUB_URL Where search reads the list of recipes from.","title":"Register and load"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Helpers-for-writing-a-recipe","page":"API","text":"Function Use\nRepliBuild.VisibilityProbe.visibility_probe(toml) Says whether [compile] visibility = \"hidden\" is safe for this library, and which functions it would drop. Leaving things out\nRepliBuild.SysConfigGen.cmake_probe(source_dir; args) Runs a library's CMake configure step to find the headers it generates. Headers the build system generates\nRepliBuild.SysConfigGen.capture_config(probe, dir) Copies those headers into dir.\nRepliBuild.SysConfigGen.toml_fragment(probe) Prints suggested [compile] and exclude entries.","title":"Helpers for writing a recipe"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#In-every-generated-module","page":"API","text":"Not exported; call them with the module's name. Call the wrapper\n\nName Gives\nstruct_size(name) A struct's size in bytes\nmember_offset(name, member) A member's offset in bytes\nSTRUCT_SIZES, STRUCT_OFFSETS All of them, as Dicts\nlibc_free(ptr) Frees memory the library got from malloc\nLIBRARY_PATH The library file the module calls\nLIBRARY_SHA, BUILD_GENERATOR The library's SHA-256, and the RepliBuild version that wrote the module","title":"In every generated module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Ingest","page":"API","text":"Wraps a C library you compiled yourself with -g, without building it from source. It is experimental and does not support C++. Prefer discover, build and wrap whenever the source builds.","title":"Ingest"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.check_environment","page":"API","text":"check_environment(; verbose=true, throw_on_error=false) -> ToolchainStatus\n\nRun environment diagnostics to verify LLVM 21+, MLIR, CMake, and other toolchain requirements.\n\nPrints a colorful report showing which tools are found, their versions, and installation instructions for anything missing. Use throw_on_error=true to abort on missing requirements.\n\nExample\n\nstatus = RepliBuild.check_environment()\nstatus.ready          # true if Tier 3 (ccall) builds will work\nstatus.tier2_ready    # true if MLIR JIT tier is also available\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.check_environment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.discover","page":"API","text":"discover(target_dir=\".\"; force=false, build=false, wrap=false) -> String\n\nScan a C or C++ project and generate its replibuild.toml. In a directory that holds a Cargo.toml and no replibuild.toml yet, the project is that Rust crate: cargo is asked what it is, and the file names it under [rust].\n\nThis is the entry point for new projects. Run this first to set up RepliBuild.\n\nAn existing replibuild.toml is kept, not rescanned, unless force=true; build and wrap still run against it. With build=true, wrap=true it builds and wraps the project where it lives, then registers it (register): the build is vendored system-wide and added to the Julia environment.\n\nArguments\n\ntarget_dir: Project directory to scan (default: current directory)\nforce: Force rediscovery even if replibuild.toml exists (default: false)\nbuild: Automatically run build() after discovery (default: false)\nwrap: Automatically run wrap() after build (requires build=true, default: false)\n\nReturns\n\nPath to the replibuild.toml file\n\nWorkflow\n\nBasic workflow (step-by-step):\n\n# 1. Discover and create config\ntoml_path = RepliBuild.discover()\n\n# 2. Build the library\nRepliBuild.build(toml_path)\n\n# 3. Generate Julia wrappers\nRepliBuild.wrap(toml_path)\n\nChained workflow (automated):\n\n# Discover → Build → Wrap (all at once)\ntoml_path = RepliBuild.discover(build=true, wrap=true)\n\n# Or just discover and build\ntoml_path = RepliBuild.discover(build=true)\n\nExamples\n\n# Discover current directory\nRepliBuild.discover()\n\n# Discover another directory\nRepliBuild.discover(\"path/to/cpp/project\")\n\n# Force regenerate config\nRepliBuild.discover(force=true)\n\n# Full automated pipeline\nRepliBuild.discover(build=true, wrap=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.discover"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.build","page":"API","text":"build(toml_path=\"replibuild.toml\"; clean=false)\n\nCompile C++ project → library (.so/.dylib/.dll)\n\nWhat it does:\n\nCompiles your C++ code to LLVM IR\nLinks and optimizes IR\nGenerates library file\nExtracts metadata (DWARF + symbols) for wrapping\n\nWhat it does NOT do:\n\nDoes NOT generate Julia wrappers (use wrap() for that)\n\nArguments\n\ntoml_path: Path to replibuild.toml configuration file (default: \"replibuild.toml\")\nclean: Clean before building (default: false)\n\nReturns\n\nLibrary path (String). For a [graph] project, the output directory that holds every library of the graph (see GraphMode).\n\nExamples\n\n# Build using replibuild.toml in current directory\nRepliBuild.build()\n\n# Build with specific config file\nRepliBuild.build(\"path/to/replibuild.toml\")\n\n# Clean build\nRepliBuild.build(clean=true)\n\n# Then generate Julia wrappers:\nRepliBuild.wrap(\"replibuild.toml\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.build"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.wrap","page":"API","text":"wrap(toml_path=\"replibuild.toml\"; headers=String[])\n\nGenerate Julia wrapper from compiled library\n\nWhat it does:\n\nLoads metadata from build (DWARF + symbols)\nGenerates Julia module with ccall wrappers\nCreates type definitions from C++ structs\nWith [compile] aot_thunks = true (C++), compiles the thunks the wrapper binds into lib<name>_thunks.<ext> beside the library\nSaves to julia/ directory\n\nRequirements:\n\nMust run build() first\nMetadata must exist in julia/compilation_metadata.json\nAOT thunks need the JLCS dialect library and clang++, as build does for C++\n\nArguments\n\ntoml_path: Path to replibuild.toml configuration file (default: \"replibuild.toml\")\nheaders: C++ headers for advanced wrapping (optional)\n\nReturns\n\nPath to generated Julia wrapper file. For a [graph] project, the graph module <output>/<Module>.jl, which includes one wrapper per library in load order.\n\nExamples\n\n# Generate wrapper using replibuild.toml in current directory\nRepliBuild.wrap()\n\n# Generate wrapper with specific config file\nRepliBuild.wrap(\"path/to/replibuild.toml\")\n\n# With headers for better type info\nRepliBuild.wrap(\"replibuild.toml\", headers=[\"mylib.h\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.wrap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.clean","page":"API","text":"clean(toml_path=\"replibuild.toml\")\n\nRemove build artifacts (build/, julia/, caches)\n\nArguments\n\ntoml_path: Path to replibuild.toml configuration file (default: \"replibuild.toml\")\ndeps: also remove the vendored git dependencies under .replibuild_cache/deps/ (default true). deps=false keeps the clones, so the next build checks their pins instead of fetching them again; everything else in the cache still goes.\n\nExamples\n\n# Clean using replibuild.toml in current directory\nRepliBuild.clean()\n\n# Clean specific project\nRepliBuild.clean(\"path/to/replibuild.toml\")\n\n# Rebuild from cold without re-cloning the dependencies\nRepliBuild.clean(\"path/to/replibuild.toml\"; deps=false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.clean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.info","page":"API","text":"info(toml_path=\"replibuild.toml\")\n\nShow project status (config, library, wrapper)\n\nArguments\n\ntoml_path: Path to replibuild.toml configuration file (default: \"replibuild.toml\")\n\nExamples\n\n# Show info for current directory\nRepliBuild.info()\n\n# Show info for specific project\nRepliBuild.info(\"path/to/replibuild.toml\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.info"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.register","page":"API","text":"register(toml_path::String; name=\"\", build=true, env=:default) -> RegistryEntry\n\nRecord a project in the global registry and vendor its build, so it loads in any session by RepliBuild.use(name) and by using <Module>.\n\nThe project builds where it lives: register runs build and wrap on it (a cache hit when its build is current), copies the result to ~/.replibuild/builds/<hash>/, writes a Julia package around it in ~/.replibuild/packages/<Module>/, and adds that package to the Julia environment env:\n\n:default — the @v#.# environment, which every session can load from;\n:active — the active environment;\na path to an environment;\nnothing — no environment; use still loads it.\n\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JULIA_ENV\"] (default, active, none, or a path) changes the default. A package's own Project.toml is never changed. build=false records the project only, and use(name) builds it later. Name is inferred from [project].name if not provided. discover(path; build=true, wrap=true) ends by calling this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.register"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.use","page":"API","text":"use(name::String; force_rebuild=false, verbose=true) -> Module\n\nLoad a wrapper by local registry name. Resolves dependencies, checks environment, builds if needed, and returns the loaded Julia module.\n\nThe name must already be registered (register(toml), or discover(path; build=true, wrap=true)). There is no Hub fallback — a missing name errors.\n\nuse loads the build register vendored while the project's inputs still match it. After an edit it builds and wraps the project where it lives, vendors the new build, and loads that; a project that has moved still loads from its last vendored build. force_rebuild=true builds the project from nothing first (git dependencies are fetched again).\n\nCall it at top level: the module is defined while your code runs, so calling into it from the function that called use needs Base.invokelatest.\n\nExample\n\nRepliBuild.register(\"path/to/replibuild.toml\")   # builds in place, vendors, adds to the Julia env\nM = RepliBuild.use(\"myproject\")                  # [project].name\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.use"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.list_registry","page":"API","text":"list_registry()\n\nPrint all registered packages in the global RepliBuild registry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.list_registry"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.unregister","page":"API","text":"unregister(name::String)\n\nRemove a package from the global registry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.unregister"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.search","page":"API","text":"search(query::String=\"\")\n\nBrowse the RepliBuild Hub catalog by name, description, tags, or language. Does not register or install anything — use only sees the local registry.\n\nRepliBuild.search()           # list catalog names\nRepliBuild.search(\"json\")     # filter by keyword\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.search"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.scaffold_package","page":"API","text":"scaffold_package(name::String; path::String=\".\") -> String\n\nGenerate a standardized Julia package for distributing RepliBuild wrappers.\n\nCreates a complete package with Project.toml, replibuild.toml, source stub, deps/build.jl hook, and test skeleton. Edit the replibuild.toml to point at your C/C++ source, then Pkg.build() compiles and wraps automatically.\n\nExample\n\nRepliBuild.scaffold_package(\"MyEigenWrapper\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.scaffold_package"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#RepliBuild.ingest","page":"API","text":"ingest(library_path; headers=String[], extra_link_libs=String[],\n                     name=\"\", project_dir=\".\", language=:c, build=false, wrap=false,\n                     register=true) -> String\n\nEXPERIMENTAL. Scaffold a replibuild.toml for ingest mode: wrap a pre-built .so (built by upstream's own build system) without recompiling. RepliBuild only runs DWARF metadata extraction + wrapper generation; the library must be built with -g.\n\nSupport matrix — the maintained, flagship path is the source-build pipeline (discover/build/wrap), where RepliBuild's own version-matched compilation guarantees the DWARF it consumes:\n\nlanguage = :c — works for plain-C ABIs (Tier-3 ccall only, no bitcode/thunks), but is best-effort: upstream's compiler and debug-info settings are outside RepliBuild's control, so extraction quality varies. Prefer the source build when the sources compile under one flag set.\nlanguage = :cpp — NOT supported. The C++ ABI surface (classes, methods, templates, virtual dispatch) requires the MLIR dialect to marshal calls and generate thunks, which only the source-build pipeline produces. Ingesting a C++ library can at best expose its extern \"C\" surface; the generated wrapper for the C++ API proper is unusable. If the library ships a C API variant, ingest that with language = :c instead.\n\nThis is the fallback for C libraries with elaborate build systems (autotools, CMake with code generators, configure scripts) that RepliBuild's source-build pipeline can't reproduce.\n\nArguments\n\nlibrary_path: path to the pre-built .so / .dylib / .dll\nheaders: header search dirs for type extraction (recommended)\nextra_link_libs: additional -l libraries the wrapper needs at load time\nname: project name (default: derived from library basename)\nproject_dir: where to write replibuild.toml (default: cwd)\nlanguage: :c or :cpp — drives wrapper generator selection\nbuild: also run build() after scaffolding\nwrap: also run wrap() (requires build=true)\nregister: with build=true, wrap=true, register the project afterwards (register), as discover does; false builds and wraps only\n\nRegistration follows discover: a bare call writes the TOML and does nothing else. With build=true, wrap=true it builds and wraps the project in project_dir, then registers it, which vendors the build to ~/.replibuild/builds/ and adds a package to a Julia environment (@v#.# unless ENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JULIA_ENV\"] names another). A registration failure is thrown, not logged.\n\nReturns\n\nPath to the generated replibuild.toml.\n\nExample\n\ntoml = RepliBuild.ingest(\"/usr/lib/libsqlite3.so\",\n                         headers=[\"/usr/include\"],\n                         name=\"sqlite_ingest\",\n                         build=true, wrap=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RepliBuild.ingest"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Make-it-fast","page":"Make it fast","text":"A wrapper is generated code that RepliBuild checked against the library it calls. It is built to add nothing to the cost of a call that the call does not need. This page says what a call costs, and the three steps that take a wrapper from \"works\" to \"starts instantly and calls at full speed\":\n\nbuild the library optimized;\ncompile the C++ stubs ahead of time (aot_thunks = true);\nput the wrapper in a package, and the package in a system image.\n\nThe numbers below were measured on one machine, on the small example libraries this manual uses. They show the size of each effect. Measure your own library before deciding anything.","title":"Make it fast"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#What-a-call-costs","page":"Make it fast","text":"A C function is called directly. The generated function converts its arguments and makes the call; there is no layer in between. It costs what the same call would cost if you wrote it by hand:\n\n Per call\nml_dot(v, v) through the wrapper 4.4 ns\nThe same call written by hand with ccall 4.3 ns\n\nA C++ function is called through a stub. A C++ call needs more than a jump: the object pointer goes in the right place, a virtual method is looked up in the object's table, a class passed or returned by value is laid out the way the compiler lays it out, and a C++ exception is caught and turned into a Julia one. RepliBuild generates a stub per function that does exactly that, compiled to machine code. How the stub is compiled decides its cost, which is the next section.\n\nFunctions that need none of it, such as a noexcept function taking plain numbers and pointers, are called directly even in a C++ wrapper.","title":"What a call costs"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Build-the-library-optimized","page":"Make it fast","text":"discover writes an unoptimized build. For anything but debugging:\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\"]\n\n[link]\noptimization_level = \"2\"\n\nBoth lines matter: the first optimizes each file, the second the library as a whole. Add the flags the library's own build would use for speed (-march=…, -DNDEBUG); RepliBuild adds none by itself.","title":"Build the library optimized"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#C-stubs-compiled-ahead-of-time","page":"Make it fast","text":"By default a C++ wrapper compiles its stubs each time it is loaded, in the session that loads it. One line makes wrap compile them once, into a library that ships with the wrapper:\n\n[compile]\naot_thunks = true\n\nwrap then writes lib<name>_thunks.so into julia/, beside the library.\n\nIt is faster in both ways that matter. On the example C++ library:\n\n Stubs compiled at load (default) aot_thunks = true\nLoading the wrapper, fresh session 8.7 s 0.5 s\nA virtual method call 35 ns 7 ns\nA function taking and returning structs by value 40 ns 13 ns\n\nLoading is faster because nothing is compiled. Calls are faster because the wrapper calls each stub through a plain function pointer.\n\nUse it for anything you ship or run repeatedly. The default is right while you are still changing the recipe: wrap is quicker without it, and RepliBuild.Debug can show a stub's machine code only for stubs compiled at load.\n\nWhat it changes for the people who run the wrapper: Deployment.","title":"C++ stubs compiled ahead of time"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Put-the-wrapper-in-a-package","page":"Make it fast","text":"Loaded with include, a wrapper is compiled by Julia in every session that loads it. Inside a package it is precompiled once, like any Julia code.\n\nBuild a package on it shows the layout. The generated module is written to precompile: everything that depends on the running process (opening the library, finding function addresses) happens when the package is loaded, never when it is precompiled.\n\nOn the example library, a package holding the ahead-of-time wrapper goes from julia starting to a C++ method's result in about one second:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using MyShapes; println(MyShapes.area(2.0, 3.0))'    # 1.1 s in all\n\nRepliBuild.register gives you the same without writing a package: it wraps the module in one and adds it to your Julia environment, so using Mathlib loads a precompiled wrapper (Load it anywhere).","title":"Put the wrapper in a package"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Compile-it-into-a-system-image","page":"Make it fast","text":"A system image is Julia with your packages already loaded and compiled. A package that contains a wrapper can be built into one with PackageCompiler, and then the wrapper, your code on top of it, and the compiled form of the calls you make are all there when Julia starts.\n\n# build_image.jl\nusing PackageCompiler\n\ncreate_sysimage([:MyShapes];\n                sysimage_path = \"MyShapes.so\",\n                project       = @__DIR__,\n                script        = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"workload.jl\"))\n\n# workload.jl: runs while the image is being written. What it calls is compiled in.\nusing MyShapes\n\n# While an image is being written, a module's start-up code has not run yet,\n# so the wrapper has not opened its library. Run it before calling anything.\nBase.invokelatest(MyShapes.Shapes.__init__)\n\nBase.invokelatest() do\n    MyShapes.area(2.0, 3.0)          # call what you want compiled into the image\nend\n\njulia build_image.jl\njulia -J MyShapes.so --project=. -e 'using MyShapes; println(MyShapes.area(2.0, 3.0))'\n\nThe whole progression on the example library, as the time from starting julia to printing a C++ method's result:\n\nHow the wrapper is loaded Start to result\ninclude, stubs compiled at load about 9 s\nIn a package, aot_thunks = true, precompiled 1.1 s\nThe same package in a system image 0.5 s\nFor comparison: julia -e 'println(6.0)' 0.3 s\n\nWith the image, a program that calls into C++ starts in nearly the time Julia itself takes to start. The image is large (about 480 MB here, most of it Julia and RepliBuild) and takes a few minutes to build.\n\nFour rules, each learned from a real image:\n\nRun the wrapper's __init__ in the workload, as above. Without it the wrapper's function pointers are still empty, the workload's first call fails with AOT thunk pointer is null, and the image is not built. It is safe to run twice; the image runs it again at every start.\nBuild the wrapper with aot_thunks = true. Stubs compiled at load are compiled again in every session, image or not.\nDo not leave live state in the workload. Anything it stores in a global is frozen into the image: an \"already initialised\" flag, an open handle, a pointer. Keep the workload's variables inside a let block or a function, and do not load data you do not want baked in.\nAn image is a snapshot. Julia runs the package inside the image and ignores later edits to its source. Rebuild the image after changing the package or re-wrapping the library.\n\nA complete example, with a GUI, three wrappers and a workload that draws frames, is LlamaChat in RepliBuild-Hub (sysimage/build.jl and sysimage/workload.jl).","title":"Compile it into a system image"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#In-your-own-code","page":"Make it fast","text":"Reuse Refs in a loop. Ref(x) for a pointer argument allocates. Make one outside the loop and assign into it: r[] = x.\nPass the struct, not a copy of its fields. Structs go to C by value at no extra cost; there is nothing to gain from unpacking them.\nUse the _ptr variant of a string-returning function when you do not need a Julia String: it skips the copy (Strings).\nKeep type-stable code around the calls. Every wrapper function has concrete argument and return types. Time is lost in the Julia code around a call far more often than in the call.\nBatch across the boundary where the library lets you. One call that processes an array beats a million calls that each process an element, in any language.","title":"In your own code"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Measuring","page":"Make it fast","text":"using BenchmarkTools\n@btime ml_dot($v, $v)\n\nTo compare the two ways of compiling stubs, build the project twice, in two folders, with and without aot_thunks, and time the same script against each.","title":"Measuring"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Troubleshooting","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Find the message or the symptom in the left column.\n\nMost problems with a wrapper are something missing from replibuild.toml, not a bug: a build that succeeds is not a complete wrapper. What goes in the TOML is the checklist.","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Setup","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"What you see What to do\ncheck_environment() shows FAIL or WARN The lines printed under it are the fix. Set up has the whole procedure.\nRepliBuild supports Linux (x86-64, AArch64) and Windows (x86-64) You are on macOS or another system. It is not supported.\nsysroot — cannot compile a TU that includes <stdio.h> Linux: install the C library's development files (libc6-dev, glibc-devel). Windows: install the MSYS2 CLANG64 toolchain.\nLLVM Toolchain not found when building C++ No clang++ and LLVM 21 or newer on PATH. Install them, or set LLVM_CONFIG to the path of llvm-config.\nLLVM 18 — need 21+ The system's LLVM is too old. On Debian and Ubuntu install a newer one from apt.llvm.org and put its bin folder first on PATH.\nJLCS dialect library not found RepliBuild's C++ support library is missing. Linux: Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\") downloads it. Windows: Set up, step 6.\nlibJLCS — built against LLVM X, but llvm-config is Y The system's LLVM was upgraded. Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\") again.\nlibJLCS — built for another dialect ABI The support library was built by a different RepliBuild version. Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\") again; for one you built yourself, rebuild it (Set up).\nFailed to initialize JIT for lib….so when a C++ wrapper loads The wrapper's C++ functions will not work. The reason is printed under the message; usually the support library is missing or llvm-dwarfdump is not on PATH. Run check_environment().\nC works, C++ does not Run check_environment() and read the C++ section.\nWindows: every C file fails on its first #include The MSYS2 CLANG64 toolchain is missing or was not found. Install it, or set MSYS2_ROOT.\nWindows: libJLCS.dll will not load, The specified module could not be found C:\\msys64\\clang64\\bin is not on the Windows PATH. Add it and restart Julia. If it is there, MSYS2 upgraded LLVM: Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\").\nWindows: The specified module could not be found opening a …_thunks.dll The same: the file it cannot find is libJLCS.dll.\nWindows: C++ stopped working after upgrading RepliBuild The new version needs its support library built: Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\").\nWindows: An Application Control policy has blocked this file Smart App Control refused to load a library RepliBuild just built, because it is new and unsigned. Event Viewer → Applications and Services Logs → Microsoft → Windows → CodeIntegrity → Operational, event 3077, names the file. RepliBuild cannot sign what it builds; changing the policy is your decision.","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Building","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"What you see What to do\nConfiguration file not found Run discover first, or pass the path of the TOML.\nNo source files to compile, then Compilation produced no library No sources were found. If they come from a dependency, look further up for Failed to clone dependency. If the library is header-only: Header-only libraries.\nFailed to clone dependency … (a warning) The source was not fetched; the build goes on and fails later. Check the url, the tag and the network.\nA dependency with a url is never fetched It has no type. Add type = \"git\".\nThe dependency's commit does not match commit The tag was moved, or you changed tag without commit. Update commit to the full 40-character commit of the tag.\nA file fails on a missing header Add the header's folder to [compile] include_dirs. Check the spelling: a folder that does not exist is passed on without complaint.\nconfig.h: No such file or directory The library's build system generates that header: Headers the build system generates.\nA file fails on an undefined name that the library's own build defines Add the define: Flags, defines and include folders.\nDuplicate main, or errors in test and example files Exclude them: Files that are not the library.\nA cloned repository has almost no files (Boost) It is made of submodules: Git submodules.\nIn-process libLLVM link failed for C source Usually the same function defined in two files. A common cause is an …_IMPLEMENTATION define in [compile] flags; move it to [wrap] shim_defines.\nThe shim_headers include resolved outside the project A header of the same name is installed on the system and was found first. Give a longer path (\"c/blake3.h\") or add the right folder to include_dirs.\nwrappable surface … is EMPTY, or functions NOT reachable [compile] visibility = \"hidden\" on a library that does not mark its public functions. Remove it, or add the define that switches the marking on: Leaving things out.\nA pattern matched nothing An exclude_symbols, surface_types_extra or [wrap.rename] entry matches no name. Fix the pattern or delete it.\nwrap fails with The wrapper module would be named … The project's name, in CamelCase, is a name the wrapper uses itself (String, Ptr). Set [wrap] module_name to something else.\nLibrary not found: …/julia/lib….so Run build before wrap.\nNo compilation_metadata.json next to … The same: run build.\nAn edit to a source file is not picked up The file's timestamp is older than the cached result, which happens when the clock was set back. build(toml; clean = true).\nA setting has no effect The key is misspelled or in the wrong section. Misspelled keys are ignored silently: check Every key.\nWindows: failed to write output '….dll': Permission denied A Julia session has the library loaded. Close it and build again.\nWindows: clean fails because a file is in use The same.\nWrapper generation is refused with another message These name something RepliBuild cannot wrap yet. Leave the named function out with [wrap] exclude_symbols so the rest is written, and please report it.","title":"Building"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#The-wrapper-is-missing-something","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"What you see What to add\nA macro is not in the wrapper [wrap.macros]\nA macro has the wrong value shim_headers found a different copy of the header. Give a longer path.\nAn inline function or method is missing Header-only libraries\nA struct or class has no fields -fstandalone-debug\nA C++ class has no constructor The same flag. If the class is still missing, [types] records.\nA template type or a container is missing [types] templates\nA printf-style function takes only its fixed arguments [wrap.varargs]\nMemory grows with every call that returns a string [wrap.cstring_owned]\nA struct became raw bytes, with a warning Julia cannot reproduce its layout. Use the accessor functions the wrapper defines for it.\nUndefVarError for a type the library defines Its name is one Julia already uses, so it is not exported. Write MyModule.Number.\nA function or struct is missing under its own name The wrapper needed that name itself and gave the library's a different one. Read the names: lines wrap printed: String is String_, Integer is c_Integer (Find your way around).\nThe wrapper is full of internal functions Leaving things out\nThe module fails to load: a redefined constant, duplicate fields Internal types are colliding: surface_types_only\nA function's name is hundreds of characters long [wrap.rename]","title":"The wrapper is missing something"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Messages-from-the-TOML","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"What you see What it means\ninvalid type '…' in [wrap.varargs.f] [wrap.varargs] takes Julia's type names (Cint, Cstring), not C's.\nmust be a list of type lists Write f = [[\"Cint\"]], not f = [\"Cint\"].\n[wrap.varargs] … is unversioned, on every wrap Add proven_at with the dependency's commit (Variadic functions). A project without a git dependency cannot, and keeps the warning.\nVarargs function 'f' has no overloads configured f takes ... and has no [wrap.varargs] entry. Only its fixed arguments can be passed.","title":"Messages from the TOML"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Calling-the-wrapper","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"What you see What to do\nABI Safety Trap: cannot call 'f' RepliBuild found no safe way to call f, and defined a function that raises instead of one that might corrupt memory. The message gives the reason: often a long double, or a type passed by value that is only declared in a header (add -fstandalone-debug). The rest of the module works.\nFFI Safety Trap: Cannot call function 'f' A type in f's signature has no Julia equivalent. Give it one under [types.custom]: MyHandle = \"Ptr{Cvoid}\".\nno method matching unsafe_convert(::Type{Ptr{T}}, ::T) A pointer parameter was given a struct. Pass Ref(x).\nMethodError passing a Ref to a struct parameter A parameter declared as a struct takes the struct itself.\nMethodError passing 2 where the signature says Cdouble or Int32 That parameter takes exactly one type. Write 2.0 or Int32(2).\nMethodError calling a C++ overload The position where the overloads differ needs the exact type: a String for char*, the enum value for an enum, Ref or Ptr for a struct pointer (Overloads).\nA variadic function rejects its extra arguments Each combination is its own function: f_Cint(…), not f(…).\nno method matching unsafe_convert(::Type{Ptr{Nothing}}, ::Cstring) A <name>_ptr result was passed to a void* parameter. Wrap it: Ptr{Cvoid}(p).\nC++ exception: … The C++ code threw. Catch RepliBuild.JITManager.CxxException.\nA crash calling a C++ method on T() T() is a zeroed value, not a constructed object (Objects).\nA function returning std::string returns raw bytes The comment beside its call in the wrapper says why. If it mentions string_layout, rebuild: build(toml; clean = true), then wrap.\nthunk  not found but _inplace is The wrapper was written by an older RepliBuild. Run wrap(toml) again.\npackage MyPkg does not have Libdl in its dependencies A package that contains a wrapper must list Libdl as well as RepliBuild: Pkg.add(\"Libdl\") in the package's environment.\nAOT thunk pointer is null while building a system image The wrapper's start-up code has not run in the workload. Call its __init__ first (Make it fast).\nA wrong value, or a crash inside the library Debug it: the crash report names the library's source line, and gdb can stop in the library and in the C++ call stubs.\nmethod too new to be called from this world context A wrapper was loaded and called inside one function. Load it at the top level, or call through Base.@invokelatest M.f(x).\nA crash when freeing memory on Windows Memory the library allocated was freed with Libc.free. Use the library's own function, or M.libc_free.","title":"Calling the wrapper"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#Registering-and-loading","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"What you see What to do\nPackage '…' not in registry Nothing is registered under that name. register the recipe first (Load it anywhere).\nusing MyProject loads an old build It loads the build registered last. Call use(\"myproject\") once, then start a new session.\nA wrapper warns that an older RepliBuild generated it Run wrap(toml) again. If a changelog entry says the fix needs a rebuild, build(toml; clean = true) first.\nregister warns that it could not add the package to an environment The message says why. use still loads the wrapper. Pass env = … with another environment, or env = nothing.\nWindows: unregister could not delete a folder A session still has that build loaded. Delete the folder after the session ends.","title":"Registering and loading"},{"category":"section","location":"troubleshooting/#After-upgrading-RepliBuild","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"A project's build is reused as long as its TOML, sources and compiler have not changed, so an upgrade alone does not rebuild it. wrap(toml) regenerates the module with the new version. To also redo the build:\n\nRepliBuild.build(toml; clean = true)      # from nothing; fetches sources again\nRepliBuild.wrap(toml)\n\nFor a registered project, RepliBuild.use(name; force_rebuild = true) does both.","title":"After upgrading RepliBuild"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#Debug-it","page":"Debug it","text":"Sooner or later a call returns the wrong value, or the process crashes inside the library. With a well-known library that is rare. With your own code, or a library nobody has wrapped before, it is the normal course of work, and it is where RepliBuild gives you something other binding tools do not: every layer between your Julia call and the library's code can be read and stepped through.\n\nThere are three layers, and each has a source file a debugger can show:\n\nLayer Its source Why you can see it\nThe library Its own .c and .cpp files RepliBuild compiled it, with debug information, and kept it\nThe call stub (C++ only) A generated .mlir file in the project's .debug/ folder RepliBuild writes the stub's source to disk and tells the debugger about it\nThe wrapper julia/<Name>.jl It is ordinary generated Julia","title":"Debug it"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#Start-with-what-needs-no-debugger","page":"Debug it","text":"A crash names the line. When the library crashes, Julia's crash report includes the library's source file and line, because the library carries its debug information:\n\n[995976] signal 11 (1): Segmentation fault\nin expression starting at crash.jl:2\nml_normalize at /home/me/mathlib/src/mathlib.c:7\nml_normalize at /home/me/mathlib/julia/Mathlib.jl:431\n\nThe first line is where the C code died. The second is the wrapper function that called it.\n\nRead what the wrapper does. Each function's docstring gives its Julia signature, the C types of its arguments, and its symbol name in the library:\n\n?ml_normalize\n@less ml_normalize(Ref(ml_vec2(1.0, 2.0)))     # the generated function itself\n\nCompare sizes. If a struct's fields look shifted, compare what the wrapper believes with what the library's own compiler says:\n\nM.struct_size(:ml_vec2)              # 16\nM.member_offset(:ml_vec2, :y)        # 8\nsizeof(M.ml_vec2)                    # must equal struct_size\n\nSee what changed. wrap keeps the previous module as <Name>.jl.prev. After a change to the TOML or an upgrade, diff julia/Mathlib.jl.prev julia/Mathlib.jl shows exactly which functions and types moved.\n\nRead wrap's report. It lists what it could not do: structs wrapped as raw bytes, functions it refused to call (ABI Safety Trap), constructors and methods it added or skipped, names it withheld.","title":"Start with what needs no debugger"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#Step-through-the-library-with-gdb","page":"Debug it","text":"The library RepliBuild builds has full debug information. Run Julia under gdb, break on a library function by its name, and you are in its source, with its arguments and variables:\n\ngdb -ex 'handle SIGSEGV nostop noprint pass' \\\n    -ex 'set breakpoint pending on' \\\n    -ex 'break ml_normalize' \\\n    -ex run \\\n    --args julia --project=. script.jl\n\nThread 1 \"julia\" hit Breakpoint 1, ml_normalize (v=0x7fffe0479c50) at mathlib/src/mathlib.c:7\n7\tml_status ml_normalize(ml_vec2 *v) { double n = sqrt(ml_dot(*v, *v)); …\n(gdb) print *v\n$1 = {x = 3, y = 4}\n(gdb) finish\nValue returned is $2 = ML_OK\n\nprint *v shows what actually arrived from Julia, which settles most \"wrong value\" questions at once: either the argument is already wrong on arrival, or the library computed something you did not expect.\n\nThe two -ex settings at the front are required:\n\nhandle SIGSEGV nostop noprint pass: Julia uses that signal for its own purposes during normal running, and gdb would otherwise stop on each one.\nset breakpoint pending on: the library is not loaded when gdb starts, so the breakpoint has to wait for it.\n\nFor a C++ method, break on its C++ name: break shapes::Rect::area.\n\nBuild for debugging. An optimized build removes variables and merges lines. While you are chasing a bug, build without optimization:\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O0\", \"-fPIC\"]\n\n[link]\noptimization_level = \"0\"\n\nPut -O2 and \"2\" back afterwards.","title":"Step through the library with gdb"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#Step-through-a-C-call","page":"Debug it","text":"A C++ function is not called directly. RepliBuild generates a small stub for it that loads the arguments, finds the right method (through the object's virtual table when the method is virtual), makes the call, and catches a C++ exception. When a C++ call misbehaves, the question is usually whether the stub passed the arguments the way the method expects.\n\nYou can look. The stubs have a source file too, and gdb stops in it.\n\nEvery stub is named after its function's symbol, with _thunk at the end. The symbol is in the function's docstring (?shapes_Rect_area, \"Mangled symbol\"). Break on the stub and on the method:\n\ngdb -ex 'handle SIGSEGV nostop noprint pass' \\\n    -ex 'set breakpoint pending on' \\\n    -ex 'break _ZNK6shapes4Rect4areaEv_thunk' \\\n    -ex 'break shapes::Rect::area' \\\n    -ex run \\\n    --args julia --project=. script.jl\n\nThread 1 \"julia\" hit Breakpoint 1, _ZNK6shapes4Rect4areaEv_thunk () at jlcs_4eff28487aee4fc6.mlir:217\n217\t  %val_ptr_1 = \"jlcs.get_field\"(%args_ptr) {fieldOffset = 0 : i64} : (!llvm.ptr) -> !llvm.ptr\n(gdb) continue\n\nThread 1 \"julia\" hit Breakpoint 2, shapes::Rect::area (this=0x29e3af0) at shapes/src/shapes.cpp:8\n8\tdouble Rect::area() const { return w_ * h_; }\n(gdb) bt\n#0  shapes::Rect::area (this=0x29e3af0) at shapes/src/shapes.cpp:8\n#1  _ZNK6shapes4Rect4areaEv_thunk () at jlcs_4eff28487aee4fc6.mlir:219\n#2  _mlir_ciface__ZNK6shapes4Rect4areaEv_thunk () at jlcs_4eff28487aee4fc6.mlir:216\n(gdb) print *this\n$1 = {<shapes::Shape> = {_vptr$Shape = 0x7fffc0c25d50 <vtable for shapes::Rect+16>, name_ = \"rect\"}, w_ = 2, h_ = 3}\n\nThe backtrace runs from your C++ method, through the stub at the exact line that made the call, back toward Julia. print *this shows the object the method received: if this is wrong here, the pointer you passed from Julia was wrong; if it is right and the result is still wrong, the problem is in the C++.\n\nstep and next work in the stub. You do not need to understand every word of a stub's line to use it: each line does one thing, and they read top to bottom as get the argument, load it, call, return.","title":"Step through a C++ call"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#Read-a-stub-without-running-anything","page":"Debug it","text":"RepliBuild.Debug shows a stub's source next to the machine code it became, from files on disk. No debugger, and no process stopped at the right moment.\n\nSet one environment variable before the wrapper is loaded, and run anything that loads it:\n\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JIT_OBJDUMP\"] = \"1\"\nusing RepliBuild\ninclude(\"shapes/julia/Shapes.jl\")\n\nThen, in that session or any later one:\n\nD = RepliBuild.Debug\n\nD.thunks(\"shapes\")                 # every stub's name\nD.walk(\"shapes\", \"_ZNK6shapes4Rect4areaEv_thunk\")\n\n═══ MLIR ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\nfunc.func @_ZNK6shapes4Rect4areaEv_thunk(%args_ptr: !llvm.ptr) -> f64 … {\n  %val_ptr_1 = \"jlcs.get_field\"(%args_ptr) {fieldOffset = 0 : i64} : (!llvm.ptr) -> !llvm.ptr\n  %val_1 = llvm.load %val_ptr_1 : !llvm.ptr -> !llvm.ptr\n  %ret_val = \"jlcs.vcall\"(%val_1) { class_name = @Rect, vtable_offset = 0 : i64, slot = 2 : i64, … } : (!llvm.ptr) -> f64\n  return %ret_val : f64\n}\n\n═══ EMITTED ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n0000000000000740 <_ZNK6shapes4Rect4areaEv_thunk>:\n  %val_ptr_1 = \"jlcs.get_field\"(%args_ptr) {fieldOffset = 0 : i64} …\n     745:\t48 8b 07             \tmov    (%rdi),%rax\n  %val_1 = llvm.load %val_ptr_1 : !llvm.ptr -> !llvm.ptr\n     748:\t48 8b 38             \tmov    (%rax),%rdi\n  %ret_val = \"jlcs.vcall\"(%val_1) { class_name = @Rect, … slot = 2 … }\n     74b:\t48 8b 07             \tmov    (%rdi),%rax\n     74e:\t48 8b 40 10          \tmov    0x10(%rax),%rax\n     75a:\tff d0                \tcall   *%rax\n\nRead it as: the object pointer goes into the first argument register, its virtual table is loaded, entry 2 of the table (offset 0x10) is called. If a virtual call lands in the wrong method, this is where you see which entry was used. If a struct argument arrives scrambled, this is where you see which registers it was put in.\n\nFunction Gives\nD.thunks(path) The names of all the stubs\nD.walk(path, name) One stub: its source, then its machine code with the source interleaved\nD.mlir_body(path, name) Only the stub's source\nD.disassemble(path; symbol = name) Only the machine code. source = false for bare instructions\nD.mlir_sources(path) The generated source files, newest first\nD.has_object(path) Whether machine code was captured\n\npath is the project folder, or anything inside it: the library file, the .debug folder, \".\" when you are standing in the project.\n\nTwo things to know:\n\nThe stub source is always written, to .debug/mlir/ in the project, each time the wrapper prepares its stubs. The machine code is kept only when REPLIBUILD_JIT_OBJDUMP was set before the wrapper loaded. Setting it afterwards is too late for that session, and walk says so.\nA wrapper built with aot_thunks = true can be debugged the same way. Its stubs are in lib<name>_thunks.so, with the same names and the same source: the gdb session above works unchanged, and D.thunks and D.mlir_body work too. Only D.walk and D.disassemble need the machine code captured at load, which such a wrapper never does; for those, turn aot_thunks off, wrap, and load once with the variable set.","title":"Read a stub without running anything"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#Which-copy-of-a-library-was-called","page":"Debug it","text":"When a call seems to reach the wrong code entirely, check which library file each name came from. On Linux:\n\nLD_DEBUG=bindings julia --project=. script.jl 2>&1 | grep ml_normalize\n\nM.LIBRARY_PATH is the file the wrapper opens. For two wrappers that link each other, see Several libraries.","title":"Which copy of a library was called"},{"category":"section","location":"debugging/#When-it-is-RepliBuild's-mistake","page":"Debug it","text":"If the library receives arguments that differ from what you passed, with types the docstring says are right, that is a bug in RepliBuild and worth reporting. The most useful report contains what this page produces: the function's C or C++ declaration, the docstring of its wrapper function, and for C++ the output of D.walk for its stub.\n\nUntil it is fixed, leave the function out with [wrap] exclude_symbols so the rest of the wrapper is usable.","title":"When it is RepliBuild's mistake"},{"category":"section","location":"libraries/#Several-libraries","page":"Several libraries","text":"One recipe normally builds one library. Real projects are often more than one: a library that needs another, a project whose build produces four libraries, a wrapper that must share state with a second wrapper. RepliBuild handles each of these, and which one you have decides what you write.\n\nYou have You write You get\nSeveral pieces of source that belong in one library One recipe with several sources One library, one module\nA CMake project that builds several libraries One recipe with a [graph] section One module, with a submodule per library and shared types\nTwo libraries you wrap separately, one using the other Two recipes, the second linking the first Two modules that share one copy of the first library\nUnrelated libraries One recipe each Independent modules that load side by side","title":"Several libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"libraries/#Several-sources,-one-library","page":"Several libraries","text":"Everything a recipe is given is compiled into the same library: its own source_files and every [dependencies] entry. If library A cannot be built without library B's source, and you do not need B as a library of its own, put both in one recipe:\n\n[project]\nname = \"app\"\n\n[dependencies.core]\ntype   = \"git\"\nurl    = \"https://github.com/example/core.git\"\ntag    = \"v2.1\"\ncommit = \"…\"\n\n[dependencies.plugins]\ntype    = \"local\"\npath    = \"../plugins\"\nexclude = [\"tests/\"]\n\n[compile]\nsource_files = [\"glue/bridge.c\"]\n\nThe result is one libapp.so and one module, App, holding the functions of all three. All of it is compiled with the same flags, which is the limit of this approach: if the pieces need different flags, use one of the next two.\n\nWhere the source comes from covers each kind of source.","title":"Several sources, one library"},{"category":"section","location":"libraries/#A-CMake-project-of-several-libraries","page":"Several libraries","text":"When a project's own CMake build produces several libraries that link each other, do not flatten it. Add a [graph] section and name the libraries you want:\n\n[project]\nname = \"libwebp\"\n\n[dependencies.libwebp]\ntype   = \"git\"\nurl    = \"https://github.com/webmproject/libwebp.git\"\ntag    = \"v1.6.0\"\ncommit = \"4fa21912338357f89e4fd51cf2368325b59e9bd9\"\n\n[graph]\ndependency = \"libwebp\"\ntargets    = [\"webpdemux\", \"libwebpmux\"]\noptions    = [\"-DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON\",\n              \"-DWEBP_BUILD_CWEBP=OFF\", \"-DWEBP_BUILD_DWEBP=OFF\"]\n\nThat is all you state. From it RepliBuild works out the rest:\n\nWhich libraries are needed. You name two; they link webp, which links sharpyuv. All four are built.\nHow each file is compiled. Every file gets the flags, defines and include folders CMake gives it, including files that need their own (-mavx2 on some files only).\nWhat must be generated first. Headers and sources the project writes during configuration or the build are produced before compiling.\nWhat goes into which library. Static and object libraries inside the project are folded into the shared libraries that use them, once each.\nThe order to build and load them in, so that each library finds the ones it depends on, and finds these copies rather than others installed on the system.\nWhich types are the same type. A struct, class or enum used by several of the libraries is one Julia type, so a value one library returns goes straight into another's functions:\n\ninclude(\"julia/Libwebp.jl\")\n\nW, D = Libwebp.Webp, Libwebp.Webpdemux\n# each library is a submodule; a type they share is one type in both\n\nCMake projects has the keys, the rules for shared types, and the limits.","title":"A CMake project of several libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"libraries/#Two-recipes,-one-using-the-other","page":"Several libraries","text":"Sometimes two libraries should stay separate wrappers: each is useful alone, each has its own recipe, and one links the other. The example is Dear ImGui's GLFW backend, which calls GLFW.\n\nBuild the first library with its own recipe. In the second recipe, link it from the first one's output folder:\n\n# imgui/replibuild.toml\n[link]\nlink_libraries = [\"glfw\", \"GL\"]\nlink_dirs      = [\"../glfw/julia\"]        # the folder where the glfw recipe wrote libglfw.so\n\nTwo things follow from link_dirs, and both matter:\n\nThe second library loads the copy you linked, not another libglfw installed on the system. Without this, a session using both wrappers would hold two GLFWs: the GLFW wrapper would initialise one, the ImGui library would call the other, and a window created through one would be garbage to the other. With it, there is one copy and both modules use it.\nBuild order becomes yours to keep. The first recipe must be built before the second, or the link fails with cannot find -lglfw.\n\nIn Julia, load both modules and pass pointers from one to the other:\n\ninclude(\"glfw/julia/Glfw.jl\")\ninclude(\"imgui/julia/Imgui.jl\")\n\nwin = Glfw.glfwCreateWindow(1280, 720, \"demo\", C_NULL, C_NULL)\nImgui.ImGui_ImplGlfw_InitForOpenGL(win, true)\n\nWhat does not happen between two separate recipes is type sharing: each module defines its own Julia types, so Glfw.GLFWwindow and a GLFWwindow the ImGui module may define are different types. Pointers pass between them because a pointer parameter accepts any pointer. If the two libraries exchange structs by value, wrap them as one CMake project instead, where types are shared.\n\nWhen you ship them, put both wrappers' files in the same folder. Each library looks beside itself first for the libraries it links, so a folder holding Glfw.jl, libglfw.so, Imgui.jl and libimgui.so keeps working wherever it is installed (Build a package on it).\n\nOn Windows there is no such search path; the wrapper opens the linked libraries itself before its own, from link_dirs and from beside itself. The recipe is the same.","title":"Two recipes, one using the other"},{"category":"section","location":"libraries/#Unrelated-libraries-in-one-session","page":"Several libraries","text":"Any number of wrappers can be loaded together. Each is its own module with its own library, and they do not interfere:\n\nL = RepliBuild.use(\"lua\")\nS = RepliBuild.use(\"sqlite\")\nZ = RepliBuild.use(\"zlib\")\n\nOne guarantee is worth knowing, because it is what makes this safe. A library RepliBuild builds always calls its own functions, even when another library with the same function names is already loaded. Julia itself loads its own copies of zlib, pcre2, curl, git2, gmp and others at startup. A zlib you wrap does not get mixed up with Julia's: your module calls your build, at your version, with your flags.\n\nThe same holds for failures. If one C++ library cannot start, its functions raise when called, and every other loaded wrapper keeps working.","title":"Unrelated libraries in one session"},{"category":"section","location":"libraries/#Choosing","page":"Several libraries","text":"The pieces need one set of flags and you want one module: several sources in one recipe.\nThe project has a CMake build that produces the libraries: [graph]. This is the only shape that shares types between libraries.\nThe libraries are independent projects and one happens to link the other: two recipes and link_dirs.","title":"Choosing"},{"category":"section","location":"tier1/#Tier-1-(experimental)","page":"Tier 1 (experimental)","text":"note: Developer documentation\nThis page is conserved from the old user guide. Tier 1 is an experiment, off by default, and not a supported calling path. Production and Hub configs leave it off. The user-facing rule is: [link] enable_lto = false, do not set [wrap.tier1] enable unless you are working on this.\n\nTier 1 hands a C function's LLVM IR to Base.llvmcall, so Julia's JIT can merge the C body into the calling Julia function. Two payloads can carry that IR. They are configured independently.","title":"Tier 1 (experimental)"},{"category":"section","location":"tier1/#Per-function-slices-([wrap.tier1])-—-off-by-default","page":"Tier 1 (experimental)","text":"warning: Not a supported tier\nIt ships, it works on the libraries it has been driven over, and it is not something to reach for in production. enable defaults to false, no Hub package should depend on it, and its tests are unwired from devtests.jl — run test/test_static_promotion.jl, test/test_slicer.jl, and test/test_tier1_dispatch.jl by hand.llvmcall is a passenger path and never the driver — any doubt resolves to ccall. The machinery is mostly refusals, demotions, and guards.\n\nA slice is a declarations-only module: one function's body, and every callee and global it reaches left as a bare declare, resolved at JIT time through the process, where the .so the wrapper dlopen'd RTLD_GLOBAL is one library among those loaded before it (which is why the library's own symbols are declared by alias; guarantee 4 below). Size tracks the function, not the library — in Lua, lua_gettop is a 2.8 KB slice cut from a 15.8 MB module.\n\n[link]\npromote_statics = true    # default\n\n[wrap.tier1]\nenable = true\n\nEach accepted function gets a @generated kernel that decides ccall vs llvmcall once, at generation time, plus a public wrapper that routes through it:\n\nconst _SLICE_lua_gettop = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"slices\", \"lua_gettop.ll\")\nisfile(_SLICE_lua_gettop) && include_dependency(_SLICE_lua_gettop)\n\n@generated function _TIER1_lua_gettop(__ptr_L::Ptr{lua_State})\n    # llvmcall is opportunistic: any doubt resolves to the ccall body.\n    if ccall(:jl_generating_output, Cint, ()) == 1 || !isfile(_SLICE_lua_gettop)\n        return :(ccall((:lua_gettop, LIBRARY_PATH), Cint, (Ptr{lua_State},), __ptr_L))\n    end\n    ir = read(_SLICE_lua_gettop, String)\n    return :(Base.llvmcall(($ir, \"lua_gettop\"), Cint, Tuple{Ptr{lua_State}}, __ptr_L))\nend\n\nThe slice is read at generation time — the first call — and spliced into the returned expression as a literal, which satisfies Base.llvmcall's statically-evaluable IR requirement while keeping module load free of slice I/O. Inside a precompile worker the kernel splices the plain ccall body instead (emitting llvmcall there deadlocks the JIT engine lock whenever a declare binds a dlopened symbol); a runtime first call regenerates to the slice. The isfile guard means a wrapper shipped without its slices/ directory demotes to ccall instead of failing.\n\nA function is decided at generation time, not call time. Anything not accepted emits the same ccall it always did. The module exports TIER1_FUNCTIONS::Set{String} naming the functions that dispatch through a slice. Slicing successfully is necessary but not sufficient — the ABI shape gate still refuses Cstring and struct crossings.\n\nFour guarantees make this safe where whole-module embedding was not:\n\nOne copy of internal state. Static promotion ([link] promote_statics) renames anything a slice might bind by declare but that cannot reach the .so's dynamic symbol table — file-local statics, and external-linkage symbols marked hidden — to an exported __rb_<lib>_<name>, on the exact module that becomes both the .so and the slice source. Promoted names are filtered out of the wrappable API.\nSlices are refused, never guessed. Variadic targets, blockaddress, alias/ifunc, and an unpromoted module come back as refusals; :weak, :inline_asm and :module_asm demote through the hazard gate. Every slice is verified before it is written.\nSymbol pre-flight. An unresolved declare does not raise — ORC prints Symbols not found: […] and then blocks forever on the first call. Before any slice reaches disk, the generator dlopens the .so and dlsym-checks every name the slice declares. A miss demotes that one function to ccall with a warning naming the symbol; a .so that will not dlopen disables Tier 1 for the whole wrap.\nThe library's own symbols bind to the library. ORC resolves a declare process-wide, and the pre-flight only asks whether the library has the symbol, so a public name a slice declared went to whichever library defined it first: Julia loads its own libz.so.1 at startup, and a zlib wrapper's gzclose slice called Julia's gzclose_w. With Tier 1 on, promotion also gives every exported definition the exported alias __rb_<lib>_<name> (exported_aliases in the metadata), and the slice declares the alias. A module built without the aliases refuses such a slice. Symbols the library only declares (libc) keep their names. The library's own native calls to its exports are the same problem one layer down (the dynamic linker resolves its PLT through the process first), so every library is linked -Bsymbolic on ELF; the alias covers the slice, which is code outside the library.\n\nEach slice const is paired with an include_dependency on the same path, so the .ll files are real precompilation dependencies. On Julia 1.11+ that tracking is by content.","title":"Per-function slices ([wrap.tier1]) — off by default"},{"category":"section","location":"tier1/#Whole-module-bitcode-([link]-enable_lto)-—-leave-off","page":"Tier 1 (experimental)","text":"The older payload emits <name>_lto.bc and embeds the whole linked module at each call site. Two verified consequences at library scale:\n\nJIT scale limit — embedding a whole library's IR per call can crash Julia's JIT. Small benchmark modules work; whole libraries do not reliably.\nDuplicated internal state — file-local static definitions stay private to the embedded bitcode, so two calls into the same library can observe different copies of its internals (observed live on a JSON parser's error-reporting path).\n\nBoth are properties of the whole-module payload, not of llvmcall. Slices fix the first by construction and the second through static promotion. Treat enable_lto as an experimentation feature for small stateless kernels. The two knobs are independent; Hub configs set enable_lto = false.\n\nPipeline placement and the rest of the calling paths: Internals.","title":"Whole-module bitcode ([link] enable_lto) — leave off"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#ABI-Marshalling-as-Compiler-IR-—-the-JLCS-Dialect","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"note: Developer documentation\nThis page is for contributors. The user manual starts at Home. Sister pages: Internals, Inheritance ABI, Developer index.\n\nThis is the architecture page. It describes the mechanism that makes the hard half of RepliBuild possible, and argues for why it is built the way it is.\n\nThe short version: a foreign call is a compilation problem, so RepliBuild compiles it. Where a wrapper generator would emit a C shim or interpret a signature at runtime, RepliBuild emits a small program in a purpose-built MLIR dialect (JLCS), lowers it to LLVM IR, and executes the result. The marshalling code for a function is derived from the same DWARF that describes its types, type-checked by MLIR, lowered by a pass that models the platform ABI explicitly, and — because it is real code with real debug info — steppable in gdb.\n\n","title":"ABI Marshalling as Compiler IR — the JLCS Dialect"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#1.-The-problem","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"A foreign function call is not one operation. It is a small pile of decisions the compiler normally makes for you, invisibly, at the call site:\n\nWhich arguments go in registers, which on the stack, and in what order.\nWhether a struct is passed as bytes in registers, as a hidden caller-owned stack copy, or by reference.\nWhether the return value comes back in RAX/XMM0, or through a hidden pointer the caller supplies (sret).\nWhether the C++ object's this pointer needs adjusting before the callee sees it, and whether the callee is even known statically (virtual dispatch).\nWhat happens to the Julia stack frame if the callee throws.\n\nccall makes exactly one of those decisions available to you — the argument type tuple — and derives the rest from Julia's own view of the types. That view is correct whenever Julia's idea of a type matches the C compiler's. The whole category of cases RepliBuild exists for is the category where it does not:\n\nCase Why ccall cannot express it\nPacked / attribute-aligned structs Julia lays the struct out its own way; the offsets diverge silently\nMEMORY-class (>16 B) by-value struct arguments SysV wants a caller-owned stack copy; Julia has no byval spelling\nLarge by-value struct returns Needs an sret pointer with ABI-correct alignment for a layout Julia may not model\nUnions crossing by value Register class depends on all overlapping fields, not on the arm you picked\nC++ virtual methods The callee address is in the object's vtable, not in the symbol table\nC++ exceptions An escaping exception unwinds through a Julia frame that has no landing pad\nNon-trivial by-value class parameters Itanium requires a copy-constructed temporary, destructed after the call\n\nTwo conventional answers exist, and both were rejected on purpose.\n\nWrite a C shim. Generate extern \"C\" glue, compile it, ccall that. This works, and it is what most binding generators fall back to. What it costs is the thing RepliBuild is built to avoid: the shim is source text in a second language, so its correctness is only checkable by compiling it, its knowledge of layout comes from headers rather than from the binary, and every library-specific quirk grows a new hand-written shim that someone has to maintain. The failure mode is a shim that compiles cleanly and marshals wrongly.\n\nInterpret the signature at runtime. libffi builds the call frame from a description, per call. That is a genuinely general answer, but it is an interpreter in the hot path, it models the ABI in a closed C library you cannot extend for C++ specifics (vtables, RAII scopes, landing pads), and there is nothing to inspect afterwards — a wrong call is a wrong call, with no artifact between the description and the crash.","title":"1. The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#2.-The-claim","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"Marshalling should be compiled code generated from the compiler's own record of the library, expressed in an IR whose types can be checked and whose lowering can be read.\n\nMLIR's dialect ecosystem is almost entirely about compute lowering — tensors, loops, hardware targets. JLCS uses the same machinery for the interop boundary, which as far as this project is aware nobody else does. Four properties fall out of that choice, and they are the argument for it:\n\nOne source of truth. The struct offsets in !jlcs.c_struct and the offsets the wrapper generator uses for Julia field access are the same DWARF numbers, read once. A shim-based design has two derivations that can drift.\nType-checked marshalling. Ops carry verifiers, the type converter refuses what it cannot translate, and a malformed thunk fails at parse — before anything executes. Several ops gained verifiers specifically because a malformed hand-written body used to reach the lowering and segfault it.\nThe ABI is a pass, not a convention. classifySysVStruct is ~40 lines that state the x86-64 SysV rules explicitly. When the rule was wrong (see §10) the fix was one function, applied to every call site at once, with a fixture that pins it against a real clang++-compiled callee.\nIt is inspectable. The generated dialect is written to disk, the JIT registers DWARF pointing at it, and gdb stops inside the emitted MLIR by file and line. Every ABI claim on this page is checkable with a breakpoint rather than by reasoning (§9).\n\nThe cost is honest and worth stating: Tier 2 needs a system LLVM/MLIR install and a compiled dialect (libJLCS.so), which is the single largest dependency in the project. C-only projects never touch it.","title":"2. The claim"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#3.-Where-thunks-sit","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"compilation_metadata.json  +  DWARF vtables          (Builder/)\n                    │\n                    ▼\n        JLCSIRGenerator.generate_jlcs_ir            Julia emits MLIR *text*\n        ├─ StructGen      → type aliases, DWARF-offset struct bodies\n        ├─ FunctionGen    → one `func.func @<mangled>_thunk` per function\n        ├─ ArrayViewGen   → strided accessors for fixed-size array members\n        └─ STLContainerGen→ container accessor thunks\n                    │  MLIR source text\n                    ▼\n        jlcsModuleCreateParse(ctx, text, \"<pkg>/.debug/mlir/jlcs_<hash>.mlir\")\n                    │  every op stamped with a FileLineColLoc naming that buffer\n                    ▼\n        jlcs_lower_to_llvm  ── jlcs-lower-to-llvm\n                            ── convert-func-to-llvm\n                            ── convert-arith-to-llvm\n                            ── reconcile-unrealized-casts\n                            ── DIScopeForLLVMFuncOp   (line-table DWARF)\n                    │\n        ┌───────────┴────────────┐\n        ▼                        ▼\n  ORC JIT engine           emit_object → clang -shared\n  (one per library)        → lib<name>_thunks.so        [aot_thunks = true]\n        │                        │\n        ▼                        ▼\n  JITManager.invoke(       direct ccall into the\n   \"_mlir_ciface_…\")       companion library\n\nThe generator is Julia code that produces MLIR as text, which is then parsed. That is a deliberate choice with two consequences worth knowing: it is why no producer has ever needed the dialect's C++ build() methods (which is how ten of them shipped undefined for years — §10), and it is why the parse buffer can be a real file on disk, which is what makes the debugger work.","title":"3. Where thunks sit"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#4.-The-thunk-contract","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"Every Tier-2 function is reached through one C signature, regardless of what it actually takes:\n\nReturn Emitted _mlir_ciface_* signature\nScalar / pointer T ciface(void** args)\nAny aggregate void ciface(T* sret, void** args)\nVoid void ciface(void** args)\n\nThe two sides agree on which is which without negotiating: emit_c_interface routes every struct-typed result through a result pointer, and JITManager._invoke_call picks the sret shape for every non-isprimitivetype Julia return type. (Note this is the ciface boundary, not the SysV classification of §7 — the thunk's own call into the library is classified separately.)\n\nThat uniformity is forced by the Julia side. ccall needs its signature at macro expansion time, so a runtime-general dispatcher cannot build one; a single void** entry point can be called for any arity, and JITManager.invoke is @generated, so each concrete argument tuple still specializes into allocation-free code. llvm.emit_c_interface on the func.func is what makes MLIR emit the _mlir_ciface_ wrapper implementing exactly this.","title":"4. The thunk contract"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#The-arg-slot-convention","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"A slot holds a pointer to the argument's storage, not the argument. The thunk therefore double-loads:\n\n%arg_ptr_1 = llvm.getelementptr %args_ptr[%idx_1] : (!llvm.ptr, i64) -> !llvm.ptr, !llvm.ptr\n%val_ptr_1 = llvm.load %arg_ptr_1 : !llvm.ptr -> !llvm.ptr   // slot → storage address\n%val_1     = llvm.load %val_ptr_1 : !llvm.ptr -> !llvm.ptr   // storage → value\n\nRef(x) on the Julia side produces exactly that shape for any isbits x. Two argument kinds are already an indirection and must be flattened to a raw pointer first, or the double-load spends one level too many and the callee receives the payload's own bytes as an address:\n\nan AbstractString — Ref(::String) points at the String object, not at its bytes;\na Base.Ref that is not already a Ptr — the spelling a caller reaches for when a parameter is annotated ::Ref{T}, which is what a C++ T const& generates. Ptr{T} <: Ref{T}, so the annotation accepts a RefValue silently.\n\nBoth are handled by _arg_marshal_plan (one plan shared by both invoke methods, because two copies is how a fix to one silently misses the other), and the original is GC-preserved across the call since only a raw pointer into it reaches the slot.\n\nCallers packing a by-value struct argument put &struct in the slot directly — not &Ref — because the struct's storage is what the callee wants.","title":"The arg-slot convention"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#Worked-example","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"A virtual method on a secondary base, from test/mi_test/. Julia side, as emitted by the wrapper generator:\n\nfunction Base2_get_b(this::Any)\n    # [Tier 2] Dispatch to MLIR JIT (Complex ABI / Packed / Union)\n    return RepliBuild.JITManager.invoke(\"_mlir_ciface__ZNK5Base25get_bEv_thunk\", Int32, this)\nend\n\nThe thunk that name resolves to, as generated and as written to test/mi_test/.debug/mlir/jlcs_<hash>.mlir:\n\nfunc.func private @_ZNK5Base25get_bEv(!llvm.ptr) -> i32\nfunc.func @_ZNK5Base25get_bEv_thunk(%args_ptr: !llvm.ptr) -> i32\n    attributes { llvm.emit_c_interface } {\n  %idx_1     = arith.constant 0 : i64\n  %arg_ptr_1 = llvm.getelementptr %args_ptr[%idx_1] : (!llvm.ptr, i64) -> !llvm.ptr, !llvm.ptr\n  %val_ptr_1 = llvm.load %arg_ptr_1 : !llvm.ptr -> !llvm.ptr\n  %val_1     = llvm.load %val_ptr_1 : !llvm.ptr -> !llvm.ptr\n  %ret_val   = \"jlcs.vcall\"(%val_1)\n      { class_name = @Base2, vtable_offset = 0 : i64, slot = 2 : i64,\n        this_offset = 0 : i64, may_throw } : (!llvm.ptr) -> i32\n  return %ret_val : i32\n}\n\nand what that lowers to, read out of gdb's disassemble /s at the breakpoint:\n\n%val_ptr_1 = llvm.load %arg_ptr_1     →  mov (%rdi),%rax     slot → storage address\n%val_1     = llvm.load %val_ptr_1     →  mov (%rax),%rdi     storage → `this`\njlcs.vcall … { slot = 2 }             →  mov 0x10(%rax),%rax 2 × 8 = 0x10\n\nThe slot arithmetic is checkable by eye. That is the point of §9.","title":"Worked example"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#5.-Types","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"The dialect defines two types. Both exist because LLVM's type system cannot say the thing the ABI needs said.","title":"5. Types"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#!jlcs.c_struct","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"!jlcs.c_struct<\"Vec3\", [f32, f32, f32], [[0 : i64, 4 : i64, 8 : i64]], packed = false>\n\nA C-ABI struct carrying its Julia type name, its flattened field types, the explicit byte offset of every field, and a packed flag. An !llvm.struct implies offsets from its element list; !jlcs.c_struct states them, which is what lets the marshalling ops move a field from where Julia put it to where C expects it without either side guessing. It converts to LLVM::LLVMStructType::getLiteral(fields, isPacked) during lowering.\n\nwarning: A `c_struct` must never appear inside an `!llvm.struct` body\nThe type converter treats !llvm.struct as legal and never rewrites its body — signature positions get rebuilt, body-op types do not. A nested c_struct alias therefore survives lowering and segfaults translateModuleToLLVMIR inside PtrLikeTypeInterface::getMemorySpace. StructGen inlines the byte-identical !llvm.struct<packed (…)> literal for c_struct-classified members instead, and jlcs_create_jit pre-flights every type in the module with mlir::LLVM::isCompatibleType, returning null (catchable in Julia → \"Tier 2 disabled for this library\") rather than crashing the process on anything foreign that still slips through.","title":"!jlcs.c_struct"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#!jlcs.array_view","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"!jlcs.array_view<f64, 3>\n\nA strided array descriptor — element type and rank — whose runtime layout is\n\nstruct ArrayView { T* data; int64_t* dims; int64_t* strides; int64_t rank; };\n//                 @0       @8             @16               @24\n\nchosen to be simultaneously expressible as a Julia Array{T,N} view, a NumPy ndarray, a C++ strided span, and a Rust slice. The producer (ir_gen/ArrayViewGen.jl) materializes one in place over a fixed-size array member — the data pointer aims into the caller's struct, so element access is zero-copy. Rank 1 today; the descriptor already carries dims and rank for the generalization, and the lowering reads data and strides only.","title":"!jlcs.array_view"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#6.-The-ops","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"Fifteen ops, all fifteen with a producer (the fourteen C++ ones as of 2026-08-13; native_call since 2026-11-19). Liveness is tracked deliberately, because \"defined in TableGen\" and \"something emits it\" are different claims — and it is checked mechanically rather than asserted here, by test_jlcs_invariants.jl §E, which reads the mnemonics out of JLCSOps.td, greps src/ for emission, and fails naming any op whose tier moved. That guard exists because this table was wrong for months (see below).\n\nOp Producer Verifier Role\njlcs.type_info JLCSIRGenerator ✅ class metadata: layout, primary base, destructor, MI/VI base tables\njlcs.ffe_call FunctionGen — direct call to a named symbol, SysV-coerced\njlcs.try_call FunctionGen — as ffe_call, plus invoke + landing pad\njlcs.native_call RustThunks ✅ a call with the callee's own signature and parameter attributes, passed through unchanged: no record rule is applied. By name, or through the function's address\njlcs.vcall FunctionGen ✅ vtable dispatch: read vptr, index slot, adjust this, indirect call\njlcs.marshal_arg FunctionGen ✅ Julia-aligned struct pointer → C-packed struct value\njlcs.marshal_ret FunctionGen — C-packed struct value → Julia-aligned struct value\njlcs.scope FunctionGen ✅ RAII region; destructors fire in reverse at exit\njlcs.ctor_call FunctionGen — constructor on an object pointer\njlcs.dtor_call FunctionGen ✅ destructor on an object pointer (exactly one operand)\njlcs.yield FunctionGen — jlcs.scope terminator\njlcs.load_array_element ArrayViewGen — strided read through an !jlcs.array_view\njlcs.store_array_element ArrayViewGen — strided write\njlcs.get_field FunctionGen, ArrayViewGen ✅ field read by byte offset\njlcs.set_field ArrayViewGen ✅ field write by byte offset","title":"6. The ops"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#How-the-last-three-got-producers","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"Until 2026-08-13 the bottom three rows read none, and one of them read none without saying so. jlcs.dtor_call was credited to FunctionGen here and in the internal devlog for months while nothing emitted it: the scope-RAII producer builds jlcs.scope(...) dtors([@sym, ...]), and it is the scope's lowering that emits those calls (as LLVM::CallOp, directly), so DestructorCallOp had a registered and unreachable conversion pattern. The pairing with ctor_call — which does have a producer — is what made the gap read as symmetry.\n\ndtor_call — a destructor thunk is exactly this op's shape: one object pointer in, void out. It used to go out as a generic ffe_call/try_call with the full SysV coercion machinery attached to a signature that needs none. The op gained a may_throw unit attribute (mirroring vcall's) with an invoke + landing-pad lowering, so a thunk moving onto it keeps the exact EH semantics it had — DWARF marks no destructor noexcept, so in practice C++ destructor thunks take the EH path.\nThe arity gate is load-bearing: under Itanium a base-object destructor (D2) of a class with virtual bases takes a second VTT argument, and this op has no operand to carry it. vi_test has three (_ZN4LeftD2Ev(this, vtt)). Those keep the ffe_call/try_call path; converting them would drop the VTT silently. The gate reads the final argument list, after this synthesis, not the DWARF parameter list.\nget_field / set_field — the thunk argument array is a record with a fixed layout: the ciface convention hands the thunk a void**, so reading argument slot i is a field read at byte offset 8i. That is what both producers now say, in one op, instead of a constant plus a pointer-scaled GEP plus a load. ArrayViewGen additionally builds the !jlcs.array_view descriptor with one set_field per field (data@0, dims@8, strides@16, rank@24) — the same terms the array-op lowering reads it back in, which previously was a GEP chain on the producer side and byte offsets on the consumer side with nothing tying them together.\n\nThis changes what the IR says, not what it does, and that is checked rather than asserted: every function of mi_test, vi_test and stl_test (188 symbols) compiles to byte-identical machine code across the switch, and test_jlcs_producers.jl §G pins the per-shape equivalence directly. The motivation is that the .mlir is the debugger's source file (see Debugging below) — jlcs.get_field ... {fieldOffset = 8} is what gdb shows at the breakpoint, and the argument-slot convention it encodes is the one this project's notes record as having cost a debugging session.","title":"How the last three got producers"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#Metadata:-type_info","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"jlcs.type_info \"Derived\",\n  !jlcs.c_struct<\"Derived\", [i32], [[28 : i64]], packed = false>,\n  \"Base1\", \"_ZN7DerivedD0Ev\"\n  {baseNames = [\"Base1\", \"Base2\"], baseOffsets = [0 : i64, 16 : i64]}\n\nErased at lowering — it exists so the layout facts travel with the IR that uses them. superType is the primary base (single-inheritance consumers). baseNames/baseOffsets pair 1:1 and carry each non-virtual base subobject's static offset. vbaseNames/vbaseVtableOffsets carry virtual bases, keyed on the negative byte position of the vbase-offset entry relative to the vtable address point — a virtual base has no static offset, so it can only be named by where to read the offset. destructorName is the DWARF-resolved complete-object destructor, which the RAII scope calls. The verifier enforces the 1:1 pairing, because consumers index one array by the other's position.","title":"Metadata: type_info"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#Calls:-ffe_call,-try_call,-vcall","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"ffe_call is the direct call. try_call is the same call marshalled identically and terminated differently — llvm.invoke with a landing pad, where a caught C++ exception is recorded through jlcs_catch_current_exception() and the call yields a zero sentinel rather than unwinding. The Julia side polls jlcs_has_pending_exception() after every Tier-2 call and raises CxxException with the original what() string. Sentinel-continue rather than unwind-through-Julia is the whole reason a C++ exception is survivable here.\n\nWhich one a function gets is per-function: the module-level may_throw (set for C++ builds) is overridden by the function's own DWARF noexcept flag.\n\nvcall is dispatch through the object's vtable, so overrides are honored — a direct symbol call is p->Class::method() static semantics, which is the wrong answer for a base-class wrapper invoked on a derived object. The op takes class-local coordinates (vtable_offset, slot, this_offset) and its producer always emits vtable_offset = this_offset = 0; see The inheritance ABI for why class-local coordinates are universally correct under Itanium. Two deliberate exclusions: destructors stay direct calls (exact-class semantics for finalizers and RAII), and struct-shaped signatures stay direct because the vcall lowering does no sret/packed coercion.\n\nnative_call is the call for a function whose signature is already the lowered one, because its own compiler wrote it. ffe_call and try_call hand every struct operand and result to the host's C classifier, which is right for a function clang compiled and wrong for one compiled under another convention: rustc returns an Option<i32> as a first-class { i32, i32 } in two registers, where the C rule packs it into one. native_call classifies nothing. Its argument and result types are the define line's, and arg_attrs holds one dictionary per argument with the parameter attributes that change how the argument is passed, under the LLVM dialect's own names.\n\nThe callee is named (callee = @f), or, with no callee, is the first operand: the function's address, called through. The second is the form RustThunks emits. A thunk is handed the address of the function it is to call, so a thunk module names no function, has nothing for a loader or a JIT to resolve, and can be compiled into a library that depends on nothing:\n\n%r = jlcs.native_call %fn, %x : (!llvm.ptr, i32) -> !llvm.struct<(i32, i32)>\n\njlcs.native_call %fn, %ret, %a\n  { arg_attrs = [{llvm.sret = !llvm.array<16 x i8>, llvm.align = 8 : i64}, {}] }\n  : (!llvm.ptr, !llvm.ptr, i8) -> ()\n\n%r = jlcs.native_call %x { callee = @_RNv…5maybe } : (i32) -> !llvm.struct<(i32, i32)>\n\nFor a named callee the lowering retypes the declaration to exactly that signature and stamps the attributes on it and on the call site; through an address there is no declaration, and the attributes go on the call site. One llvm.call either way. It is a plain call: a callee that unwinds unwinds through the thunk, and whatever must end that unwind is a frame outside it (a Rust panic may only be ended by Rust). Its producer is RustThunks, and only that: the C and C++ generators never emit it (test_jlcs_invariants.jl §E).","title":"Calls: ffe_call, try_call, vcall"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#RAII:-scope,-ctor_call,-dtor_call,-yield","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"jlcs.scope(%tmp : !llvm.ptr) dtors([@_ZN4BaseD1Ev]) {\n  jlcs.ctor_call @_ZN4BaseC1ERKS_(%tmp, %src) : (!llvm.ptr, !llvm.ptr) -> ()\n  %r = jlcs.ffe_call %tmp { callee = @_Z5takesB } : (!llvm.ptr) -> i32\n  llvm.store %r, %retslot : i32, !llvm.ptr\n  jlcs.yield\n}\n\nUnder Itanium, a by-value parameter of a class with a non-trivial destructor is passed as a pointer to a caller-owned temporary, destructed by the caller after the call — not as raw bits in registers. Passing the bits miscompiles those calls. The producer detects the case by symbol presence (a truly trivial destructor is never emitted as a symbol, so a class with one in the metadata is non-trivial for the purposes of calls), builds the temporary the way the class's declared constructors allow (FunctionGen.value_temporary: the copy constructor; the move constructor when the copy is deleted or not public; the bytes only when the copy is trivial), and brackets the call in a jlcs.scope. Destructors fire in reverse order at scope exit. A class no temporary can be made for gets no thunk and an ABI trap in the wrapper. The module declares every constructor and destructor a scope names, whether or not the wrapper binds it.\n\njlcs.scope has no results, so a return value leaves through a stack slot — that is why the RAII path allocates %raii_retslot. Because try_call converts an exception into sentinel-and-continue, the normal path is the only path, so destructor coverage is total.\n\nThe verifier exists because an arity mismatch between managed_ptrs and destructors used to SIGSEGV inside the lowering, which indexes one by the other's position.","title":"RAII: scope, ctor_call, dtor_call, yield"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#Marshalling:-marshal_arg,-marshal_ret","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"%packed = jlcs.marshal_arg %ptr\n  { memberTypes = [i32, f64], juliaOffsets = [0 : i64, 8 : i64] }\n  : (!llvm.ptr) -> !llvm.struct<packed (i32, f64)>\n\nThe layout-mismatch pair. marshal_arg reads each member from the Julia offset and assembles the C-packed value; marshal_ret does the inverse for returns. memberTypes and juliaOffsets pair 1:1 and the verifier says so — same reason as type_info.","title":"Marshalling: marshal_arg, marshal_ret"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#7.-The-lowering","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"One pass, jlcs-lower-to-llvm, run as a partial conversion with all fourteen ops marked illegal and the LLVM + arith dialects legal. func.func is dynamically legal — a signature containing !jlcs.c_struct cannot be handled by the stock ConvertFuncToLLVM type converter, so those functions are claimed by this pass with a converter that knows the type. The rest of the pipeline is stock (convert-func-to-llvm, convert-arith-to-llvm, reconcile-unrealized-casts), plus DIScopeForLLVMFuncOp last — see §9.\n\nInter-pass verification is deliberately off for the pipeline: the JLCS pass emits llvm.call ops referencing func.func symbols that do not become llvm.func until the next pass. The final module is fully verified.","title":"7. The lowering"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#The-SysV-classifier","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"Every by-value record crossing in RepliBuild is classified twice over, and it is worth reading the division as prose:\n\nThe facts are read on the Julia side. FunctionGen.sysv_record_abi (src/IRGen/ir_gen/SysVRecordABI.jl) walks the record's DWARF metadata: members at their byte offsets, bitfields by bit range, nested records at their real offsets, a union's members at its start, the scalar kinds (float/double SSE, integers, pointers and enums INTEGER, long double X87, _Complex as two halves, __int128 two INTEGER eightbytes), and the record's alignment (DW_AT_alignment, else the members' lowered to what the layout shows). It applies the ABI's rules as clang does: padding never classifies, a misaligned field makes MEMORY except a bitfield, classes merge per eightbyte, over 16 bytes is MEMORY, an X87UP not under X87 is MEMORY.\nThe verdict travels on the call. The thunk generator states it as sysv_args (one entry per operand) and sysv_ret (aapcs64_args and aapcs64_ret when generating for an AArch64 host) on each jlcs.ffe_call / jlcs.try_call: mem:<align>, regs:int,sse, regs:none,sse (a low eightbyte of padding, passed nowhere), or ignore.\nbuildCallShape acts on it. Under the SysV target a stated operand or result takes its class from the statement; one with no statement (hand-written IR, test_struct_abi.jl) is still classified from its LLVM body by classifySysVStruct. The Win64 rules never read the statement: they cross a record by size alone.\n\nWhy not classify the emitted body, as the dialect did until 2026-10-24? Because the body cannot say what the convention asks. StructGen's explicit pad arrays are bytes like a field's, so an eightbyte of a float and padding went in a general register ({float; double} crossed in RDI and XMM0 where C uses XMM0 and XMM1). A tiled body spells a nested record or a union as bytes, which hid a misaligned field inside, so a packed {char; {short; float}} went in registers where C uses memory (two abifuzz units segfaulted on that shape). A bitfield's storage unit sat at its byte offset with its whole type, which read as misaligned where C checks only the bits. long double and `Complexwere!llvm.ptr. And no body saysaligned(16).testsysvabioracle.jldiffs the result, as the call shape, against LLVM 23's own SysV classifier (llvm/ABI) on any host, andtestsysv_live.jl` calls one record of each kind.\n\nFrom that classification the shared buildSysVCallShape derives the entire call:\n\nShape Lowering\nMEMORY-class return alloca at the record's alignment (memorySlotAlign), pass as first argument, call returns void, load the result back\nRegister-class return call returns the coerced scalar(s); store to a slot (at offset 8 when the low eightbyte is padding), reload as the original struct type\nRegister-class argument store to a slot, load one scalar per eightbyte, pass them as separate arguments — exactly clang's coercion\nMEMORY-class argument alloca + store + pass the pointer with llvm.byval(T) and llvm.align at the record's alignment\nignore argument / return no operand; a void call and the record's zero\nEverything else passed through unchanged\n\nTwo details are load-bearing and were each a bug first:\n\nbyval must be stamped on the call site and the declaration. The call site is what the backend lowers; the declaration is what the verifier checks it against. retargetCallee rewrites the external declaration to the coerced signature and copies the attributes onto it.\nSlot alignment is explicit (memorySlotAlign = max(8, abiAlign), raised to the record's DWARF alignment when the call states one). Clang never emits an sret/byval slot below 8 on x86-64, and a struct RepliBuild failed to model field-by-field degrades to !llvm.array<N x i8>, whose natural alignment is 1 — leaving alignment implicit would hand native code an underaligned buffer for exactly the types least understood. A 16-aligned record (aligned(16), __int128, long double) needs the statement: no body of ours is aligned above 8.\n\nffe_call and try_call share one derivation of all of this. They used to carry independent copies, which is precisely how a fix to one silently missed the other; that shape recurs throughout §10.","title":"The SysV classifier"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#Exception-lowering","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"try_call (and vcall with may_throw) emit llvm.invoke with a landing pad that calls __cxa_begin_catch / jlcs_catch_current_exception / __cxa_end_catch, records the message in a thread-local buffer, and continues with a zero-valued result. A post-pipeline walk stamps __gxx_personality_v0 onto any llvm.func containing an llvm.invoke — it must run after ConvertFuncToLLVM, since only LLVM::LLVMFuncOp carries a personality attribute. The C++ runtime symbols themselves are registered into the JIT by JITManager at engine setup.","title":"Exception lowering"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#8.-Struct-bodies-come-from-DWARF-offsets","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"A struct body emitted for a thunk must have the size and layout of the C type, not of anything Julia or LLVM would pick. StructGen lays members out at their DWARF offsets with explicit !llvm.array<N x i8> padding between them, and verifies the result against a Julia mirror of LLVM's own abiSize/abiAlign (_mlir_layout). One shared derivation (_apply_dwarf_layout) feeds all three body builders.\n\nA struct that cannot be laid out consistently — overlapping members, a member whose emitted type will not sit at its offset, an unmeasurable member, or members summing past byte_size — degrades to a byte_size-byte opaque region and warns. Opaque, but never the wrong size.\n\nThat rule replaced the original \"close the struct with one trailing filler of byte_size - Σ member sizes\", which double-counted: LLVM inserts interior alignment padding itself, and DWARF reports enum members with size = 0. Every non-packed struct with interior padding came out larger than the C type, and since llvm.emit_c_interface stores a MEMORY-class result straight into the caller's buffer while JITManager sizes that buffer from the Julia struct, every such call wrote past a live Julia object. Measured on llama.cpp: llama_context_params emitted 200 bytes against a native 160, overrunning a 160-byte Ref by 34. Every member offset was correct, which is why it presented as intermittent corruption rather than as a marshalling bug. Pinned by test/test_struct_layout.jl, which needs no toolchain and is negative-checked against the old rule.","title":"8. Struct bodies come from DWARF offsets"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#9.-Debugging-a-thunk","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"This is the default way to debug Tier 2. Reach for it before reading IR and reasoning about it.\n\nThree pieces, each added for its own reason, compose into a source-level debugger for generated marshalling code:\n\noptions.enableGDBNotificationListener = true — ORC registers every JIT'd object with gdb through __jit_debug_register_code.\nMLIR's parser stamps every op with a FileLineColLoc naming its buffer, and DIScopeForLLVMFuncOpPass turns that into the emitted DWARF's DIFile.\nThe .debug/mlir/jlcs_<hash>.mlir dump is the file those locations point at — so it is not diagnostic output, it is the debugger's source file.\n\nNothing needs to be enabled and nothing links gdb: RepliBuild is the publisher of LLVM's GDB JIT interface, and gdb reads the descriptor out of the inferior. clean() removes .debug, and it regenerates on the next JIT init.\n\ntimeout -s KILL 175 gdb -batch -nx \\\n  -ex 'set pagination off' -ex 'set confirm off' -ex 'set listsize 4' \\\n  -ex 'handle SIGSEGV nostop noprint pass' \\\n  -ex 'set breakpoint pending on' \\\n  -ex 'break _ZNK5Base15get_aEv_thunk' -ex 'run' \\\n  -ex 'bt 1' -ex 'info source' -ex 'disassemble /s' \\\n  --args julia --project=. test/mi_test/verify.jl\n\nThread 1 \"julia\" hit Breakpoint 1, _ZNK5Base15get_aEv_thunk () at jlcs_83a242c27fb37885.mlir:126\n126\t  %val_ptr_1 = llvm.load %arg_ptr_1 : !llvm.ptr -> !llvm.ptr\nProducer is MLIR.   Compiled with DWARF 4 debugging format.\n\nTwo flags are mandatory and fail silently without explanation: set breakpoint pending on (the thunk does not exist until the JIT emits it, so the breakpoint cannot resolve at load) and handle SIGSEGV nostop noprint pass (Julia's GC uses SIGSEGV for write barriers). disassemble /s is the payoff — dialect ops interleaved with the machine code they became.","title":"9. Debugging a thunk"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#The-static-path-—-RepliBuild.Debug","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"gdb needs a live process stopped at the right moment. RepliBuild.Debug needs a file, carries the same information, cannot wedge, and works on a package this process never built. Prefer it.\n\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JIT_OBJDUMP\"] = \"1\"   # BEFORE the wrapper loads — see below\nD = RepliBuild.Debug\n\nD.thunks(\"test/vi_test\")                            # what you can ask about\nD.walk(\"test/vi_test\", \"_ZNK5VBase3tagEv_thunk\")    # MLIR + emitted asm, one call\nD.disassemble(pkg; symbol = s)                      # objdump -dS, ops ↔ machine code\nD.mlir_body(pkg, symbol)                            # just the dialect\nD.dwarf(pkg; section = \"line\")                      # the address → MLIR-line table\n\nThe env var is read, not passed, and that is forced: MLIR must have the object cache when the engine is created, and engines are cached per library per process, so there is no later moment at which an argument could arrive. Setting it after the first Tier-2 call is too late, and says so.\n\n.debug_info holds only a compile unit — no DW_TAG_subprogram, no variable DIEs. That is what LineTablesOnly means here, and it is why file and line are perfect in gdb while info locals is empty. It is pinned negatively in test_debug_inspection.jl, so raising the emission kind shows up as a failing test rather than as a surprise.\n\nOptional profiling: REPLIBUILD_JIT_PROFILE turns on LLVM's perf listener and routes the per-process jitdump to a session directory. Off by default — the listener writes to $HOME/.debug/jit with nothing rotating or expiring it.","title":"The static path — RepliBuild.Debug"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#10.-Failure-classes-this-architecture-has-hit","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"Recorded because the shapes recur, and because a reader deciding how much to trust the marshalling layer deserves the real list rather than a claim of correctness.\n\nTwo generators, one guard. The Julia wrapper generator and the MLIR thunk generator derive the same decisions independently, so a gate added to one silently misses the other. Dear ImGui exposed it: is_method is a string heuristic (:: in the demangled prefix) and Itanium mangles a namespace-scoped free function identically to a member function, so the thunk generator synthesized a this for ImGui::GetVersion(). The thunk loaded args_ptr[0] and dereferenced it while Julia passed an empty array — 174 zero-arg functions segfaulting on first call, 614 more with every argument shifted one slot. Both generators now consult the aggregate table (a namespace is never a key in it), matching on every :: suffix, angle-bracket-depth aware. The same shape produced the independent ffe_call/try_call SysV copies (§7).\n\nEmitted size ≠ DWARF byte_size. §8. A live heap smasher on every MEMORY-class struct return, invisible because every member offset was right.\n\nMEMORY-class by-value arguments had two wrong shapes. Packed structs went as a bare pointer (by reference — the callee reads an address where it expects bytes); non-packed ones went as an LLVM first-class aggregate, which the backend splits per element, shifting every later argument. One llvm.byval path now. The discriminating fixture is gap_probe in test/struct_abi/, driven against a real clang++ callee — a self-JIT'd callee cannot catch a convention mismatch, because both sides share it.\n\nEnum returns must be bare integers. A struct result — even !llvm.struct<(i32)> — makes emit_c_interface use the sret convention, while the Julia side calls an enum back as a scalar: the args pointer lands in the sret slot and the call dereferences garbage. Found on tinyxml2's XMLDocument::Parse → XMLError.\n\nA tier decided a presentation policy. The C++ generator's Tier-2 branch continues past the ccall emission path, and the char* return policy (Union{String,Nothing}, the _ptr sibling, the [wrap.cstring_owned] free) lived only on that path — so 75 functions across five Hub packages handed back a raw Cstring and silently discarded any declared deallocator. Not a marshalling bug: the pointer was correct, it just arrived undressed. The rule the fix encodes is the general one — a dispatch tier decides how a function is called, never how its result is presented — and it is enforced by _assert_cstring_policy on the wrapper write path rather than by convention.\n\nTen undefined symbols shipped in libJLCS.so for its entire life. dlopen(…, RTLD_NOW) refused the library. Eight op build() bodies and two ArrayViewType accessors were declared by TableGen (skipDefaultBuilders, genStorageClass = 0) and never defined in C++. Nothing caught it because ccall((:sym, path), …) binds lazily and every producer builds IR as text, so no call site ever existed: the library loaded, the dialect worked, and the whole Tier-2 suite was green on a binary the loader would reject if asked to resolve it eagerly. The guard is a fresh-subprocess RTLD_NOW dlopen plus an nm -D --undefined-only sweep (test_jlcs_invariants.jl §D).\n\nNested c_struct in an llvm.struct body. §5 — a whole-process load crash on pugixml, now prevented at emission and caught by the create_jit pre-flight.\n\nUnbounded arity and malformed operand segments. llvm.call's AttrSizedOperandSegments has two groups, not three; a hand-rolled OperationState for the indirect vcall wrote {1, nArgs, 0} where {1 + nArgs, 0} was required, and translation walked off the end. Use the dedicated indirect-call builder.\n\nThe through-line: every one of these was silent. That is why the invariants suite, the layout mirror, the RTLD_NOW probe, the op verifiers, and the create_jit type pre-flight all exist — the architecture's real requirement is not that marshalling be right, it is that wrong marshalling be loud.","title":"10. Failure classes this architecture has hit"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#11.-Boundaries","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"Deliberately unbuilt, so they are not chased as bugs:\n\nArray views are rank 1. The producer's regex skips T[N][M]; the descriptor's dims/rank fields are populated but unread by the lowering, and the user-facing Julia accessors that would call these thunks are not emitted yet.\nvcall gates on scalar/pointer signatures. Virtual methods returning a struct by value or taking a packed struct by value keep the direct-call path; closing this means giving the vcall lowering try_call's coercion.\nSeven ops have no verifier (ffe_call, try_call, load/store_array_element, ctor_call, yield, marshal_ret). No known crash paths, but hand-written IR gets no schema help. get_field, set_field and dtor_call gained theirs on 2026-08-13 with their producers — all three lowerings take a pointer operand on faith, which ODS's AnyType accepts and LLVM translation then rejects far from the mistake.\nThe classifier is x86-64 SysV only. The 16-byte MEMORY threshold, 64-bit pointers, and i64/f64 eightbytes are hardcoded. Win64 crosses a record by size alone (classifyWin64Struct); AAPCS is not modeled.\nA long double record returned by value is a trap on SysV. C returns it in st0, which the thunk does not read; FunctionGen refuses the return (sysv_trap_reason) and the wrapper's stub says so. As an argument it goes in memory, byval 16, and works. A record of 16 bytes or less holding a member whose kind DWARF does not settle (a typedef the metadata cannot resolve, a record with no definition) is refused the same way: its class is unknowable.\nVectors and __float128 are not classified. SSEUP never arises: a record holding one is refused as unknown rather than passed in a scalar register.\nA 16-aligned record gets slots aligned to 8.\nNo in-IR virtual-base upcast op. Virtual inheritance works through a dynamic upcast on the Julia side (The inheritance ABI), which needed zero dialect changes. An op would only be required for IR that must do the adjustment inside a thunk, and no producer needs that today.","title":"11. Boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#12.-AOT-thunks","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"[compile] aot_thunks = true runs the identical generator and lowering as the last step of wrap, for the thunk set the wrapper binds, emits an object, and links it against the library into a companion lib<name>_thunks.so. The wrapper then calls that library instead of JITManager.invoke: no JIT at load. libJLCS.so is still required, to compile the thunks and when they run: they import its exception helpers, and on Windows its guard frame catches for them.","title":"12. AOT thunks"},{"category":"section","location":"mlir/#13.-Building-the-dialect","page":"JLCS / MLIR","text":"cd src/mlir && ./build.sh\n\nNeeds system LLVM/MLIR 21+ (22.1.x here), CMake 3.20+, and mlir-tblgen. The artifact is src/mlir/build/libJLCS.so, gitignored, and pinned to the installed MLIR's minor SONAME (libMLIR.so.22.1).\n\nA patch bump inside a minor needs nothing — SONAMEs are unchanged and the existing .so keeps resolving. Rebuilding anyway is cheap and was verified clean.\nA minor bump requires rm -rf build && ./build.sh and a Tier-2 re-run. Check the release notes for MLIR entries first; that decides whether the bump is a formality or real work.\nA worktree or fresh clone has no libJLCS.so (it is a build artifact), so Tier 2 fails there in a way that reads exactly like a code regression. Build it, or symlink src/mlir/build from a tree that has one.\n\n\n\nWhere the executable truth lives: test_mlir_templates.jl (dialect semantics: CStructs, RAII, vcall, sret, MI and the VI diamond), test_jlcs_invariants.jl (op arity/liveness probes, the RTLD_NOW symbol guard), test_jlcs_producers.jl (scope-RAII and array-view producers executing through the real JIT), test_struct_abi.jl (SysV small-struct and byval ABI against a real clang++ callee), test_sysv_abi_oracle.jl (the SysV classification against LLVM's ABI library), test_struct_layout.jl (DWARF-offset layout, no toolchain), test_multilib_jit.jl (two wrappers, one session), test_debug_inspection.jl (the debug surfaces above), and the fixtures test/mi_test/, test/vi_test/, test/stl_test/.","title":"13. Building the dialect"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#Header-only-libraries","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"RepliBuild wraps what ends up in the compiled library. Code in a header ends up there only when some source file uses it. A library that is only headers therefore starts with nothing to wrap, and a normal library can still have part of its API missing because that part is inline.\n\nThe fix is always the same: get the header's code compiled into the library. How depends on how the header delivers its code.\n\nThe header has Add Section\nAn implementation behind a macro (the stb style) [wrap] shim_defines An implementation section\nstatic inline C functions [wrap.macros] entries static inline functions\nC inline functions that are not static One declaration in a .c file C inline functions\nC++ class templates [types] templates C++ templates\nC++ constructors and methods written inside a class -fstandalone-debug C++ classes\nC++ free functions, operators, function templates A keep file C++: a keep file","title":"Header-only libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#Starting-a-header-only-project","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"On a folder that holds only headers, discover reports found: 0 sources and build stops with Compilation produced no library. With no source files to look at, discover also guesses language = \"c\", even for C++ headers.\n\nSo set the language and flags yourself:\n\n[project]\nname = \"hol\"\n\n[compile]\nflags        = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-std=c++17\"]     # drop -std for C\ninclude_dirs = [\".\"]\n\n[link]\nenable_lto         = false\noptimization_level = \"2\"\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage = \"cpp\"                                   # or \"c\"\n\nThen add one of the recipes below. If the recipe is a file of your own, add the file first and discover gets the language right by itself.","title":"Starting a header-only project"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#An-implementation-section","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"A single-header library in the stb style compiles its implementation only in a file that defines a macro before including it. Name the header and the macro:\n\n[wrap]\nshim_headers = [\"stb_image.h\"]\nshim_defines = [\"STB_IMAGE_IMPLEMENTATION\"]\n\nRepliBuild writes a small source file that defines the macro and includes the header, and compiles it into the library. No file of your own is needed.\n\nEach shim_defines entry is NAME, or NAME=VALUE.\n\nDo not put the macro in [compile] flags instead. A flag reaches every file in the build. As soon as two files include the header, the implementation is compiled twice and the link fails; in a C build the error is In-process libLLVM link failed for C source, which does not say why.\n\nA file of your own does the same job, if you prefer one:\n\n/* hol_impl.c */\n#define HOL_IMPLEMENTATION\n#include \"hol.h\"\n\nThis brings in the functions of the implementation section. The header's static inline functions and macros are still missing, and nothing reports them: see the next section.","title":"An implementation section"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#static-inline-functions","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"A static inline function never becomes part of the library. Declare it under [wrap.macros], exactly as you would a function-like macro (Macros):\n\n[wrap]\nshim_headers = [\"hol.h\"]\n\n[wrap.macros.hol_dot]                 # static inline float hol_dot(HolVec a, HolVec b)\nret  = \"float\"\nargs = [\"HolVec\", \"HolVec\"]\n\nhol_dot(a, b) then exists in Julia. Structs passed by value work. A library that is nothing but static inline functions needs no source file at all: list them and build.\n\nThis is for C. A C++ name with a namespace cannot be an entry.","title":"static inline functions"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#C-inline-functions","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"A C function declared inline without static is compiled only when one source file asks for it, by declaring it again without inline:\n\n/* holi_emit.c */\n#include \"holi.h\"\nint holi_triple(int v);     /* this file now provides holi_triple */\n\nPut that file in the project and build.","title":"C inline functions"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#C-templates","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"A class template is compiled only for the type arguments someone uses. List the ones you want:\n\n[types]\ntemplates        = [\"hol::Box<double>\", \"std::vector<int>\"]\ntemplate_headers = [\"hol.hpp\"]\n\nEvery member of each listed instantiation is compiled, constructors included. For a header-only template library this can be the whole recipe. C++ templates and containers has the rules. A function template cannot be listed here; use a keep file.","title":"C++ templates"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#C-classes","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"Constructors and member functions written inside a class body are handled for you. The build compiles in every public one that the library does not already contain, and also the calls that leave default arguments out. It needs the class to be fully described in the build, which one flag ensures:\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-std=c++17\", \"-fstandalone-debug\"]\n\nwrap reports what it added and what it skipped: private, deleted and template members, and abstract classes, are skipped.\n\nTwo things are not covered:\n\nA class that no source file in the build mentions. Name it in [types] records.\nFree functions and operators outside any class. Use a keep file.","title":"C++ classes"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#C:-a-keep-file","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"A keep file is a source file of your own whose only job is to use things from the headers so that they are compiled. Nothing calls it.\n\n// hol_keep.cpp\n#include \"hol.hpp\"\n\n// A free function or operator: taking its address compiles it.\n[[gnu::used]] static auto keep_twice = &hol::twice;\n\n// A function template: instantiate it explicitly.\ntemplate double hol::biggest<double>(double, double);\n\n[[gnu::used]] stops the optimizer from removing the reference.\n\nThe functions appear in the wrapper under their own names, like any other C++ function: hol_twice, hol_biggest_double.\n\nWhen the library comes from git, the keep file is yours and lives beside the TOML. List it in source_files; it is compiled along with the dependency:\n\n[dependencies.hol]\ntype    = \"git\"\nurl     = \"https://github.com/example/hol.git\"\ntag     = \"v1.0\"\ncommit  = \"…\"                                  # the full commit\nexclude = [\"tests/\", \"examples/\"]\n\n[compile]\nflags        = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-std=c++17\", \"-fstandalone-debug\"]\nsource_files = [\"shim/hol_keep.cpp\"]\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage = \"cpp\"\n\nA header-only repository's tests and examples are often its only .cpp files, and they would all be compiled, so exclude them. The keep file includes the library as a user would (#include \"hol/hol.hpp\"): the dependency's top folder, include/ and src/ are already searched.","title":"C++: a keep file"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#Boost-and-other-large-template-libraries","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"Wrap one Boost library at a time. boostorg/boost is a repository of about 170 submodules, and a plain clone of it is nearly empty. List the submodules your library needs (Git submodules): its own, plus the header-only ones it includes.\n\nThen two things from the library's own CMakeLists.txt:\n\nIts private defines (BOOST_FILESYSTEM_SOURCE, and options such as BOOST_FILESYSTEM_NO_CXX20_ATOMIC_REF for a -std=c++17 build) go in [compile] flags.\nThe submodules contain .cpp files that are not library code: configuration checks (config/, checks/), generators (tools/), benchmarks. exclude them by their libs/<module>/… path.\n\nA Boost library that can stand alone is simpler as its own dependency: boostorg/math with -DBOOST_MATH_STANDALONE.\n\nMost of a Boost library's public API is inline, so it needs a keep file. Under the library's own *_SOURCE define the headers hide that inline API; #undef the define in the keep file before the first include.\n\nTemplate names get long. A template instantiation's Julia name includes its arguments, defaults too. Boost.Math's pdf comes out over 500 characters. Give such functions names of your own, and let one pattern cover a family:\n\n[wrap.rename]\n\"boost_math_pdf_*\" = \"pdf\"\n\nFind the candidates with filter(n -> occursin(\"pdf\", string(n)), names(M; all = true)).\n\nA large template library also brings thousands of internal types with it. If the module does not load because of them, use surface_types_only.","title":"Boost and other large template libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#Growing-a-wrapper-from-the-REPL","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"The recipes that need no file of your own can be tried in one Julia session, one entry at a time:\n\nusing RepliBuild\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JULIA_ENV\"] = \"none\"        # keep it out of your Julia environment\ntoml = RepliBuild.discover(\"hol\")           # a folder holding hol.h\n# edit replibuild.toml: shim_headers, shim_defines …\nRepliBuild.register(toml)\nH = RepliBuild.use(\"hol\")\n# add a [wrap.macros] entry, then in the same session:\nH = RepliBuild.use(\"hol\")                   # rebuilt and reloaded, as a new module\n\nEach use after an edit rebuilds in a few seconds and returns a fresh module (Load it anywhere).","title":"Growing a wrapper from the REPL"},{"category":"section","location":"header-only/#Checking-what-was-compiled","page":"Header-only libraries","text":"The source files RepliBuild writes for these recipes are in .replibuild_cache/ inside the project: replibuild_shims.c (or .cpp) for [wrap.macros] and shim_defines, and replibuild_templates.cpp for [types] templates. Open them to see exactly what was compiled.","title":"Checking what was compiled"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#Every-key","page":"Every key","text":"The lookup table for replibuild.toml. To decide which keys your library needs, read What goes in the TOML first.\n\n\"Default\" is the value used when the key is absent. Paths are relative to the folder holding replibuild.toml unless they are absolute.\n\nwarning: A misspelled key is ignored without a warning\nThe file is read for the keys below and everything else is dropped. When a setting seems to do nothing, check its spelling and its section here.","title":"Every key"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[project]","page":"Every key","text":"Key Default Meaning\nname the folder's name Names the library file (lib<name>.so), the Julia module (CamelCase of the name) and the name you use it by.\nroot the TOML's folder The project folder.\n\nversion and uuid may be present (the Hub's recipes carry a version, and discover writes a uuid). Neither is used.","title":"[project]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[compile]","page":"Every key","text":"Key Default Meaning\nflags [\"-std=c++17\", \"-fPIC\"] Compiler flags for every file. discover writes [\"-fPIC\"] for C. Add -O2; neither default optimizes.\ndefines none A table: NAME = \"value\" becomes -DNAME=value. For a define with no value, put -DNAME in flags.\ninclude_dirs written by discover Folders to search for headers. Added automatically: a git or archive dependency's top folder, include/ and src/; a local dependency's folder and include/.\nsource_files written by discover The project's own files to compile, one by one. build does not search the project folder: a file not listed is not compiled. A dependency's files are added to these. Each listed file must exist.\nvisibility \"default\" \"hidden\" wraps only the functions the library marks as public. Check with the probe first: Leaving things out. Any other value is an error.\nparallel true Compile files in parallel.\naot_thunks false C++: have wrap compile the wrapper's C++ call stubs into a second library, lib<name>_thunks.so, instead of compiling them each time the wrapper loads. Faster loading; one more file to ship.","title":"[compile]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[link]","page":"Every key","text":"Key Default Meaning\noptimization_level \"0\" \"0\", \"1\", \"2\", \"3\", \"s\" or \"z\". Use \"2\".\nlink_libraries none Libraries to link: [\"m\", \"dl\"] is -lm -ldl.\nlink_dirs none Folders to search for those libraries. The library found there is also the one loaded at run time.\nenable_lto true for C, false for C++ Set to false. true is an experimental calling mode (Experimental keys).","title":"[link]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[wrap]","page":"Every key","text":"Key Default Meaning\nlanguage \"cpp\" \"c\" or \"cpp\", in lower case. Chooses the compiler. Anything other than \"c\" is treated as C++. A project with a [rust] section is \"rust\" and needs no language.\nmodule_name CamelCase of the project name The Julia module's name, and the wrapper file's (julia/<module_name>.jl).\nshim_headers none Headers that define the macros in [wrap.macros], written as in an #include. Used only together with [wrap.macros] or shim_defines.\nshim_defines none #defines placed before those headers, in that one generated file: \"NAME\" or \"NAME=VALUE\". Compiles a single-header library's implementation. Needs shim_headers.\nexclude_symbols none Patterns for functions and globals to leave out of the wrapper. One that matches nothing is an error.\nsurface_types_only false Emit only the types that wrapped functions and globals use.\nsurface_types_extra none Patterns for types to keep when surface_types_only is on. One that matches nothing is an error.\nheaders every header at the top of each include folder The headers read for enums and callback types that the compiled library does not record. An entry that is not found is an error.\nuse_clang_jl true false skips that header reading.\n\nPatterns use * for any text and ? for one character, and must match the whole name.","title":"[wrap]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[wrap.macros.NAME]","page":"Every key","text":"One table per macro, or per static inline function.\n\nKey Meaning\nret The result's C type: \"int\", \"const char*\", \"lua_State*\".\nargs The arguments' C types. Present: a function-like macro, called as NAME(a, b). Absent: a constant, called as NAME(). args = [] is a function-like macro with no arguments.\n\n[wrap.macros.deflateInit]\nret  = \"int\"\nargs = [\"z_stream*\", \"int\"]\n\n[wrap.macros.ZLIB_VERSION]\nret = \"const char*\"","title":"[wrap.macros.<NAME>]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[wrap.varargs]","page":"Every key","text":"function = [[types…], [types…]]: one inner list per combination of extra arguments, in Julia's names for C types. Fixed arguments are not listed.\n\nKey Meaning\n<function name> The list of combinations. Not a list of lists: error.\nproven_at The dependency commit the combinations were checked against. Absent: wrap warns. Different from the dependency's commit: error.\n\nAllowed type names: Cstring, Cwstring, Cchar, Cuchar, Cshort, Cushort, Cint, Cuint, Clong, Culong, Clonglong, Culonglong, Cintmax_t, Cuintmax_t, Csize_t, Cssize_t, Cptrdiff_t, Cwchar_t, Cfloat, Cdouble, Cintptr_t, Cuintptr_t, Bool, Any, the sized Int8…Int64, UInt8…UInt64, Float32, Float64, and Ptr{Name}. Anything else is an error naming the entry.\n\n[wrap.varargs]\nproven_at       = \"1ab3208a1fceb12fca8f24ba57d6e13c5bff15e3\"\nlua_pushfstring = [[\"Cstring\"], [\"Cint\"], [\"Cstring\", \"Cint\"]]","title":"[wrap.varargs]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[wrap.cstring_owned]","page":"Every key","text":"function = \"release function\". The wrapper copies the returned char* into a String, then frees the buffer with the named function. \"free\" means the C library's free.","title":"[wrap.cstring_owned]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[wrap.rename]","page":"Every key","text":"\"pattern\" = \"julia_name\". The pattern names a function by the name the wrapper would give it, or by its symbol name in the library. The value must be a valid Julia name. A pattern that matches nothing, or a name already in use, is an error.\n\nA function that wrap reports under a changed name (String as String_) is keyed by the name the library gave it (String). The name you choose must not be a Julia type.","title":"[wrap.rename]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[types]","page":"Every key","text":"Key Default Meaning\ntemplates none C++ template instantiations to compile in: \"std::vector<double>\".\ntemplate_headers none Headers those need, beyond the standard ones found automatically. \"<fmt/format.h>\" for a system-style include, \"mylib.h\" for a quoted one.\nrecords none C++ classes that no source file of the library uses, to make them appear in the wrapper. Spelled as in C++ (ns::Class). Not for templates.\ncustom none A table mapping a C type name to a Julia type, for a type the wrapper cannot map: MyHandle = \"Ptr{Cvoid}\".\nstrictness \"warn\" What to do with a type that has no exact Julia equivalent: \"strict\" fails, \"warn\" reports and continues, \"permissive\" continues silently.\nallow_unknown_structs true Treat an unknown struct as an opaque pointer instead of failing.\nallow_unknown_enums false Treat an unknown enum as a 32-bit integer.\nallow_function_pointers true Wrap function-pointer arguments as Ptr{Cvoid}.","title":"[types]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[dependencies.name]","page":"Every key","text":"Source that is compiled into the library. Where the source comes from explains each type.\n\nKey Default Meaning\ntype \"local\" \"git\", \"archive\", \"local\" or \"system\". Not \"git\" by default: an entry with a url and no type fetches nothing.\nurl  git: the repository. archive: the tarball's URL, or a path to one on disk.\ntag the default branch git: the tag or branch to check out.\ncommit  git: the full 40-character commit the tag must be at. A shorter value is an error; a checkout at another commit stops the build.\nsha256  archive: the tarball's SHA-256. Required.\nsubdir  archive: the folder inside the tarball that holds the source. Not needed when the tarball has one top folder.\npath  local: the folder. Used in place, never copied, searched on every build. A .a or .so found inside it is linked.\npkg_config  system: a package name. Its include and link flags are taken from pkg-config. Nothing is compiled.\nexclude none Files and folders not to compile: an entry matches a file's name or any part of its path inside the dependency. Skipped without an entry: for git and archive, folders named test, tests, testes, example, examples, fuzzing, build, doc, docs; for local, only build, .git, .cache.\nsubmodules none git: the submodule paths to fetch. Only these, at the commits the repository records.","title":"[dependencies.<name>]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[platform.system]","page":"Every key","text":"<system> is linux, windows or macos. Any section of the file may appear under it, and is laid over the rest on that system: [platform.windows.link], [platform.windows.dependencies.glfw]. A value replaces the one outside the platform table; a list is replaced whole. Any other system name is an error.","title":"[platform.<system>]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[graph]","page":"Every key","text":"Present: the project is a CMake project and the build is taken from CMake.\n\nKey Default Meaning\nsource \".\" The folder holding CMakeLists.txt. With dependency, a folder inside that dependency.\ndependency  The [dependencies] entry whose source is the CMake project.\ntargets every library The CMake library targets to build and wrap. The libraries they link are included. Naming a program is an error.\noptions none Arguments for CMake's configure step: [\"-DBUILD_TESTING=OFF\"].\nbuild_type \"Release\" CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE.\n\nAn unknown key or a wrong type in [graph] is an error. In a graph project [compile], [types] and the [wrap] keys other than module_name do not apply: flags come from CMake.","title":"[graph]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[rust]","page":"Every key","text":"Present: the project is a Rust crate, and cargo builds it. Rust crates is the guide; discover writes this section for a folder that holds a Cargo.toml.\n\nbuild, wrap and use work on a Rust crate's public functions, #[inline] ones included, on Linux (x86-64 and AArch64; Windows is not run yet):\n\nnumbers, bool, char, strings and slices in; records, tuples, enums and Option out;\na String is a Julia String and a Vec a Julia Vector, both ways;\nanything else that owns something (a Box, a record holding a String) is a Julia object that frees it when Julia collects the object. Passing it to a Rust function by value moves it: the object is empty afterwards. The module's OWNED lists those types;\na Result returned is its value, or a thrown RepliBuild.RustRuntime.RustError holding the error;\na Rust panic arrives as a RepliBuild.RustRuntime.RustPanic;\nwhere a function takes &dyn Trait, pass a value of any type that implements the trait, or a Box of the trait object; a trait's methods are functions too (Area_area(x));\nwhere a function takes a function (fn(i32) -> i32), pass a Julia function;\na static NAME is NAME() for its value and NAME_ptr() for where it is.\n\nA generic function is wrapped for the types instantiate names. A function that is not wrapped is in the module as a stub that says why, and is listed in the module's UNSUPPORTED.\n\nKey Default Meaning\nmanifest \"Cargo.toml\" The crate's Cargo.toml, relative to the project root. With dependency, relative to that dependency's source.\ndependency  The [dependencies] entry whose source holds the crate.\npackage the manifest's own package The package to wrap, by its Cargo name. Needed when the manifest is a workspace.\nopt_level 0 The optimization level the crate is compiled at, 0 to 3.\nfeatures none Cargo features to turn on: [\"simd\"].\ndefault_features true false turns the crate's default features off.\ninstantiate none Generic functions to compile for particular types, each written as Rust names it: [\"geo::largest::<f64>\", \"geo::Pair::<i32>::sum\"].\n\nA generic function such as fn largest<T>(xs: &[T]) -> T is not in a library until something uses it with a type, so instantiate says which types you want. Each entry becomes a function of the module named after it (largest_f64, Pair_i32_sum). An entry that is not a function of the crate is a build error that shows the compiler's message. A function that takes impl Trait is named by the type it should have: \"(geo::scale as fn(f32) -> f64)\". Nothing else about a crate needs an entry: its #[inline] functions are found by the build.\n\nThe crate is built as it is: it needs no extern \"C\", no #[repr(C)] and no other change. cargo and rustc must be installed; REPLIBUILD_TOOL_CARGO and REPLIBUILD_TOOL_RUSTC name particular ones.\n\nwrap compiles the calls a plain Julia call cannot make into lib<name>_thunks.so beside the library, whenever it can; there is no key for it. Without that file the module compiles them at first use.\n\nAn unknown key or a wrong type in [rust] is an error. In a Rust project [compile], [link] and [types] do not apply, and [rust] cannot be combined with [graph] or [ingest].","title":"[rust]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#[paths],-[binary],-[cache]","page":"Every key","text":"Key Default Meaning\n[paths] output \"julia\" Where the library and the wrapper are written.\n[paths] build \"build\" Where intermediate files are written.\n[binary] type \"shared\" Leave as is. A wrapper needs a shared library.\n[binary] output_name lib<name>.so The library's file name.\n[cache] enabled true Reuse compiled files between builds.\n[cache] directory \".replibuild_cache\" Where they, and fetched dependencies, are kept.","title":"[paths], [binary], [cache]"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#Keys-that-do-nothing","page":"Every key","text":"discover writes these and nothing reads them. Leave them or delete them:\n\n[discovery] (the whole section), [workflow] stages, [wrap] enabled, [wrap] style, [binary] strip_symbols, [paths] source, [paths] include, [paths] cache, [project] uuid, [llvm].\n\nIn particular [discovery] ignore_patterns does not keep files out of a build. Use exclude on the dependency.","title":"Keys that do nothing"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#Experimental-keys","page":"Every key","text":"These belong to features that are not part of the supported workflow. A recipe does not need them.\n\nKey Leave it at What it is\n[link] enable_lto false Calls C functions through the library's compiled code embedded in the wrapper, instead of through the library file. Fails on large libraries.\n[wrap.tier1] absent A per-function version of the same idea. Described under Tier 1.\n[link] promote_statics, [link] fallback absent Support for the two keys above.\n[ingest] absent Wraps a C library you compiled yourself, without its source. Written by RepliBuild.ingest.","title":"Experimental keys"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#What-a-forced-discover-keeps","page":"Every key","text":"discover on a project that already has a replibuild.toml leaves the file alone. discover(path; force = true) scans again and rewrites it, keeping these keys from the old file:\n\n[dependencies], [graph], [rust] and [platform.*], whole;\n[types] templates, template_headers, records;\n[wrap] macros, varargs, cstring_owned, rename, shim_headers, shim_defines, exclude_symbols, surface_types_only, surface_types_extra, headers, tier1;\n[compile] visibility, [link] promote_statics.\n\nEverything else is rewritten from the scan, including [compile] flags, include_dirs and [link] link_libraries. Do not use force = true on a recipe you have tuned by hand; keep the file in version control.","title":"What a forced discover keeps"},{"category":"section","location":"config/#What-stops,-what-warns","page":"Every key","text":"Errors when the file is read\n\ncommit that is not 40 hexadecimal characters.\n[wrap.varargs] entry that is not a list of lists.\n[compile] visibility other than \"default\" or \"hidden\", or \"hidden\" together with a different -fvisibility= in flags.\nshim_defines without shim_headers, or an entry that is not NAME or NAME=VALUE.\nsubmodules on a dependency that is not git, or an entry that is not a plain relative path.\nAn unknown [platform.<system>] name.\nAn empty [project] name, a root that does not exist, a source_files entry that does not exist, an optimization_level outside the list.\n\nErrors during build or wrap\n\nThe checked-out dependency is not at commit, or a tarball does not match sha256.\nA shim_headers entry resolved to a file outside the project and its dependencies.\nA [wrap.varargs] type name outside the allowed list, or a proven_at that differs from the dependency's commit.\nAn exclude_symbols, surface_types_extra or [wrap.rename] pattern that matches nothing.\nA [wrap] headers entry that is not found.\nThe built library exports none of its functions: visibility = \"hidden\" on a library that does not mark its public functions.\n\nWarnings\n\nA variadic function with no [wrap.varargs] entry, and a [wrap.varargs] table with no proven_at.\nA [types] templates entry that produced nothing usable.\nA struct that could not be given named fields and is wrapped as raw bytes with accessor functions.\nA git dependency that could not be fetched. The build continues and then fails on the missing files, so look for Failed to clone dependency above the first compile error.","title":"What stops, what warns"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Developer-documentation","page":"Overview","text":"These pages are for people changing RepliBuild, not for people wrapping a library. The user manual is the rest of this site, starting at Home.\n\nThe architecture essays below are conserved from the previous manual. They are deliberately not on the user path.","title":"Developer documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Architecture","page":"Overview","text":"Page What it is\nABI marshalling as compiler IR The JLCS dialect: why marshalling is compiled, the thunk contract, the op reference, SysV lowering, gdb inside generated MLIR\nThe inheritance ABI Mental model for MI and virtual inheritance: upcast helpers, class-local vcall\nInternals & dispatch Pipeline stages, the three calling paths, module-by-module map\nTier 1 (experimental) Per-function llvmcall slices, and why whole-module enable_lto stays off","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#In-the-repository,-not-on-this-site","page":"Overview","text":"Path What it is\ndocs/updates/ Dated engineering notes (MI/vcall, virtual inheritance, audits, release write-ups)\nCHANGELOG.md Full version history\nCLAUDE.md Contributor operating notes: API, ingest doctrine, Hub rules, known-unbuilt ledger","title":"In the repository, not on this site"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Tests","page":"Overview","text":"Three suites over one list, test/suites.jl, run by test/run.jl:\n\njulia --project=. test/runtests.jl              # pkg: what Pkg.test runs — loads, version, config, structural guards\njulia --project=. test/unit.jl                  # unit: every toolchain-free regression test, one process\njulia --project=. test/devtests.jl              # dev: everything that compiles or JITs\njulia --project=. test/devtests.jl --changed    # only what the diff against origin/main touches\njulia --project=. test/devtests.jl --area=cpp   # one area: build, dwarf, c, cpp, dialect, graph\njulia --project=. test/run.jl dev --files=test_c_sret_abi.jl --processes --tail\n\nJobs run in one process by default; --processes runs a child per job with its output in test/.logs/<job>.log. To add a test, add its line to test/suites.jl: the suite, the areas of src/ it covers, the fixture directories it locks and the fixture jobs it runs after.\n\nDialect and ABI truth lives in test/test_mlir_templates.jl, test/test_jlcs_invariants.jl, test/test_jlcs_producers.jl, test/test_struct_abi.jl, test/mi_test/, test/vi_test/.","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Debugging-a-generated-thunk","page":"Overview","text":"D = RepliBuild.Debug\nD.thunks(\"path/to/project\")\nD.walk(\"path/to/project\", \"some_symbol_thunk\")    # dialect + asm\n\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JIT_OBJDUMP\"] = \"1\" must be set before the wrapper loads for the disassembly path. gdb inside the emitted MLIR: Debugging a thunk.","title":"Debugging a generated thunk"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Wrap-your-first-library","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"This page takes a small C library from source to a Julia module you can call, start to finish. Every later page is detail on one of its steps.\n\nThe work has a fixed shape:\n\ndiscover scans the source folder and writes replibuild.toml.\nbuild compiles the library. wrap writes the Julia module.\nYou look at what is missing and add it to replibuild.toml.\nbuild and wrap again.\n\nStep 3 is the only part that needs you. RepliBuild works out every function, struct and enum from the compiled library. What it cannot work out is what the compiler threw away or never saw: macros, the arguments you mean to pass to a printf-style function, who frees a returned string, which files in the repository are not the library. Those go in the TOML.","title":"Wrap your first library"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#The-library","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"A folder with a header and a source file:\n\nmathlib/\n├── include/mathlib.h\n└── src/mathlib.c\n\n/* include/mathlib.h */\n#define ML_VERSION 3\n#define ML_SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))\n\ntypedef struct { double x, y; } ml_vec2;\ntypedef enum { ML_OK = 0, ML_EDOM = 1 } ml_status;\n\ndouble    ml_dot(ml_vec2 a, ml_vec2 b);\nml_status ml_normalize(ml_vec2 *v);\nchar     *ml_describe(ml_vec2 v);      /* allocated; free it with ml_free */\nvoid      ml_free(void *p);\nint       ml_log(const char *fmt, ...);\n\nsrc/mathlib.c implements them, and calls sqrt from the math library.","title":"The library"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#1.-Discover","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"using RepliBuild\n\ntoml = RepliBuild.discover(\"mathlib\")\n\nRepliBuild | discover mathlib\n  found: 1 sources, 3 include dirs\n\nThat writes mathlib/replibuild.toml and returns its path. The parts of the file that matter:\n\n[project]\nname = \"mathlib\"\nroot = \".\"\n\n[compile]\nflags        = [\"-fPIC\"]\nsource_files = [\"src/mathlib.c\"]\ninclude_dirs = [\".\", \"include\", \"src\"]\n\n[link]\noptimization_level = \"0\"\nenable_lto         = false\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage = \"c\"\n\ndiscover found the source file and the include folders, and saw that the library is C. It writes several more sections with default values; you can leave them or delete them.\n\nsource_files is the list of what will be compiled, and from now on it is yours: build compiles exactly the files on it and never searches the folder again. discover lists every source file it finds, tests and examples included, so on a real project read the list and delete what is not the library.\n\nIf a replibuild.toml already exists, discover keeps it. Once you have edited the file, it is yours: keep it in version control and do not run discover on it again.","title":"1. Discover"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#2.-Build-and-wrap","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"RepliBuild.build(toml)\nRepliBuild.wrap(toml)\n\nRepliBuild | mathlib\n  sources: 1  includes: 3\n  compile: 1 files\n  link: libmathlib.so (0.01 MB)\n  dwarf: 5 functions, 2 types\n  done: 1.98s\n┌ Warning: Varargs function 'ml_log' has no overloads configured in [wrap.varargs]. Generating base wrapper only.\n  wrap: Mathlib.jl\n\nbuild compiled the library into mathlib/julia/libmathlib.so. wrap wrote the Julia module beside it, mathlib/julia/Mathlib.jl. The module is named after the project, in CamelCase.\n\nThe first build and wrap in a Julia session take several seconds longer than later ones.","title":"2. Build and wrap"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#3.-Load-it-and-look","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"include(\"mathlib/julia/Mathlib.jl\")\nusing .Mathlib\n\nv = ml_vec2(3.0, 4.0)\nml_dot(v, v)                 # 25.0\n\nr = Ref(v)\nml_normalize(r)              # ML_OK\nr[]                          # ml_vec2(0.6, 0.8)\n\nIt works, and it is not finished. names(Mathlib) lists what the module exports, and comparing that with the header shows what is missing:\n\nIn the header In the first wrapper Why\nML_VERSION, ML_SQUARE absent, with no warning Macros do not exist in a compiled library.\nml_log(fmt, ...) ml_log(fmt) only Nothing says which extra arguments you want to pass. The warning above named it.\nml_describe returns a String, and leaks the C buffer on every call Nothing says the caller must free it.\nsqrt from the math library works here, by luck The library was linked without -lm.\n\nA build that succeeds is not a finished wrapper. Reading the library's header against this kind of list is the job, and What goes in the TOML is the full list.","title":"3. Load it and look"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#4.-Edit-the-TOML","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"Open mathlib/replibuild.toml and add what the library needs. This is the whole file after the edit, with the inert sections removed:\n\n[project]\nname = \"mathlib\"\n\n[compile]\nflags        = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\"]          # an optimized build\nsource_files = [\"src/mathlib.c\"]\ninclude_dirs = [\"include\"]\n\n[link]\nenable_lto         = false\noptimization_level = \"2\"\nlink_libraries     = [\"m\"]               # -lm\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage     = \"c\"\nshim_headers = [\"mathlib.h\"]             # the header the macros below come from\n\n[wrap.macros.ML_VERSION]                 # a constant macro\nret = \"int\"\n\n[wrap.macros.ML_SQUARE]                  # a function-like macro, used on doubles\nret  = \"double\"\nargs = [\"double\"]\n\n[wrap.varargs]                           # the calls to ml_log you want to make\nml_log = [[\"Cint\"], [\"Cstring\", \"Cdouble\"]]\n\n[wrap.cstring_owned]                     # ml_describe's result is freed with ml_free\nml_describe = \"ml_free\"","title":"4. Edit the TOML"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#5.-Build-and-wrap-again","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"Start a new Julia session, so the old library is not loaded, then:\n\nusing RepliBuild\ntoml = \"mathlib/replibuild.toml\"\n\nRepliBuild.build(toml)\nRepliBuild.wrap(toml)\n\ninclude(\"mathlib/julia/Mathlib.jl\")\nusing .Mathlib\n\nML_VERSION()                                    # 3\nML_SQUARE(1.5)                                  # 2.25\nml_describe(ml_vec2(3.0, 4.0))                  # \"(3, 4)\", and the C buffer is freed\nml_log_Cint(\"n=%d\\n\", 7)                        # prints n=7\nml_log_Cstring_Cdouble(\"%s=%g\\n\", \"pi\", 3.14)   # prints pi=3.14\n\nOnly what changed is recompiled. wrap keeps the previous module as Mathlib.jl.prev, so you can compare the two to see what an edit did.\n\nwrap now warns that [wrap.varargs] is \"unversioned\". That warning is for libraries fetched from git, where the overloads should name the commit they were checked against (What goes in the TOML). A library in your own folder has no commit and keeps the warning.","title":"5. Build and wrap again"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#6.-Use-it-from-anywhere","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"So far the wrapper is loaded with include and a path. To load it by name in any session:\n\nRepliBuild.register(toml)\n\n# any later session\nusing RepliBuild\nM = RepliBuild.use(\"mathlib\")\nM.ml_dot(M.ml_vec2(1.0, 2.0), M.ml_vec2(3.0, 4.0))\n\nregister also adds the wrapper to your default Julia environment as a package, so using Mathlib works too. Load it anywhere has the details, and says how to keep it out of your environment.","title":"6. Use it from anywhere"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#The-same-thing-in-one-call","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"RepliBuild.discover(\"mathlib\", build = true, wrap = true)\n\nThat discovers, builds, wraps and registers. It is a quick way to see whether a library builds at all. The result still needs step 3.","title":"The same thing in one call"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#C","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"The steps are the same. discover sets language = \"cpp\" when it finds C++ sources, and the build then uses the system's clang++ (Set up). What differs is what you add to the TOML:\n\n[compile]\nflags = [\"-O2\", \"-fPIC\", \"-std=c++17\", \"-fstandalone-debug\"]\n\n[link]\nenable_lto         = false\noptimization_level = \"2\"\n\n[wrap]\nlanguage = \"cpp\"\n\n[types]\ntemplates = [\"std::vector<double>\"]      # containers you want to create from Julia\n\n-fstandalone-debug is worth adding to every C++ recipe from the start: without it, classes and structs that are only declared in headers can come out with no fields and no constructors (What goes in the TOML).\n\nA C++ wrapper names every function after its namespace and class, and methods take the object first:\n\nr = shapes_make_rect(2.0, 3.0)      # shapes::make_rect\nshapes_Rect_area(r)                 # shapes::Rect::area\nshapes_Shape_rename(r, \"door\")      # takes a std::string: pass a Julia string\n\nCall the wrapper covers objects, strings, containers and exceptions.","title":"C++"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Day-to-day","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"To Call\nRebuild after editing the TOML or the sources build(toml), then wrap(toml)\nRegenerate the module after a TOML edit that does not change the library wrap(toml) alone (which keys)\nSee whether the library and module exist info(toml)\nStart over clean(toml), or build(toml; clean = true)\nStart over, keeping the downloaded sources clean(toml; deps = false)\n\nThree habits:\n\nLoad a rebuilt wrapper in a new Julia session. A session keeps the library it loaded. On Windows a build cannot even replace a library that a running session has open: it fails with Permission denied.\nNever edit the generated module. wrap overwrites it. Change the TOML, or put your own code in your own module (Build a package on it).\nKeep replibuild.toml in version control. It is the only file you write.","title":"Day to day"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Where-things-go","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"Everything stays inside the project folder:\n\nPath Holds\nreplibuild.toml The recipe. The only file you edit.\njulia/ The result: Mathlib.jl, libmathlib.so (.dll on Windows) and the data files the module reads. These files belong together.\nbuild/ Intermediate files.\n.replibuild_cache/ Compiled files reused between builds, and sources fetched from git.\n\nbuild/ and .replibuild_cache/ can be deleted at any time and should not be committed.","title":"Where things go"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Next","page":"Wrap your first library","text":"Your library is somewhere else (another folder, a git repository, a tarball), or is fed from several places: Where the source comes from.\nIt is more than one library: Several libraries.\nGo through your library's needs one by one: What goes in the TOML.\nIt is header-only, or keeps its API in inline functions: Header-only libraries.\nIt is a CMake project that builds several libraries: CMake projects.","title":"Next"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Where-the-source-comes-from","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"RepliBuild builds the library itself, so the first thing a recipe must state is what to build it from. There are five ways to give it source, and a recipe can use several at once. Everything they supply is added together and compiled, with one set of flags, into one library.\n\nThe source is You write RepliBuild does\nFiles in the project folder [compile] source_files, a list of files Compiles exactly those files\nA folder of source on disk [dependencies.x] with type = \"local\" Finds and compiles the source files in it, on every build\nA git repository type = \"git\" Clones it, checks the commit, finds and compiles its source files\nA release tarball type = \"archive\" Downloads it, checks its hash, unpacks it, finds and compiles its source files\nAn installed system package type = \"system\" Compiles nothing: takes include and link flags from pkg-config\n\nThe three that find files for you do not behave the same way. Read the section for the one you use.\n\nTo link against a library without compiling it, see Libraries it links. To build several libraries that stay separate, see Several libraries.","title":"Where the source comes from"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Files-in-the-project-folder","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"The project folder is the one holding replibuild.toml. Its files are compiled only when they are listed:\n\n[compile]\nsource_files = [\"src/core.c\", \"src/io.c\", \"vendor/util/util.c\"]\ninclude_dirs = [\"src\", \"include\"]\n\nbuild never searches the project folder. The list is the whole truth: a file not on it is not compiled, and a file on it that does not exist is an error.\n\ndiscover writes the list for you, once. It walks the whole project folder and lists every .c, .cpp, .cc and .cxx file it finds, skipping only folders named build, .git, .cache, node_modules, .replibuild_cache, .svn, .hg, __pycache__, venv and .venv. It also writes include_dirs: the project folder, its include/ and src/, and every folder that contains a header.\n\nSo after discover, read the list:\n\nDelete what is not the library. discover does not skip tests/ or examples/ here. A project with a tests/t_core.c gets that file in the list, and its main in the library.\nAdd a file later by adding a line. New files are not picked up by build. Running discover(path; force = true) rewrites the list, and also resets [compile] flags (what it keeps).\n\nPaths are relative to the project folder. Keep them relative, so the recipe works on another machine.","title":"Files in the project folder"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#A-folder-of-source-on-disk","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"When the source is a folder you would rather point at than list file by file (a library checked out beside your project, a folder of generated files, a vendored copy), make it a local dependency:\n\n[dependencies.mylib]\ntype    = \"local\"\npath    = \"../mylib\"              # relative to the TOML, or absolute\nexclude = [\"tests/\", \"examples/\", \"tools/\"]\n\nOn every build RepliBuild walks that folder and compiles every .c, .cpp, .cc and .cxx file in it. Nothing is copied: the folder is used where it is, a new file in it is compiled by the next build, and an edit reaches the next build.\n\nThree things are particular to a local folder:\n\nTests and examples are compiled unless you exclude them. Only folders named build, .git and .cache are skipped. A git or archive dependency skips tests/ and examples/ by itself; a local folder does not.\nHeaders are searched in the folder and in its include/. Not in its src/. If the library's sources include headers from src/ or anywhere else, add that folder to [compile] include_dirs.\nCompiled libraries found inside it are linked. A libfoo.a or libfoo.so anywhere in the folder adds its folder to the link search path and foo to the linked libraries. That is how you feed in a part that is already built. If the folder holds stale build output you do not want linked, exclude it or delete it.\n\nA local folder is not checked against anything. What is in it is what gets built.\n\nDo not point a local dependency at the project folder itself (path = \".\"): the walk would enter RepliBuild's own cache and output folders. List the project's own files in source_files.","title":"A folder of source on disk"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#A-git-repository","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"[dependencies.cjson]\ntype    = \"git\"\nurl     = \"https://github.com/DaveGamble/cJSON.git\"\ntag     = \"v1.7.18\"\ncommit  = \"acc76239bee01d8e9c858ae2cab296704e52d916\"\nexclude = [\"fuzzing/\"]\n\nRepliBuild clones the repository into .replibuild_cache/deps/cjson/ inside the project, checks out tag (a tag or a branch), and compiles its source files.\n\nAlways set commit: the full 40-character commit that tag must be at. A tag is a name its owner can move to different code. With commit set, a moved tag stops the build instead of compiling something you did not review. Get it with git rev-parse v1.7.18^{commit} in a clone, or from the tag's page on the hosting site. To upgrade the library, change tag and commit together.\nHeaders are searched in the clone's top folder, its include/ and its src/.\nFolders named test, tests, testes, example, examples, fuzzing, build, doc and docs are skipped. Name anything else that is not the library in exclude (what to look for).\nA folder inside the clone that is itself a git repository is not entered.\nSubmodules are not fetched unless you list them (below).\n\nThe clone is reused by later builds. It is fetched again when url, tag, commit or submodules change, or after clean(toml).","title":"A git repository"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#A-release-tarball","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"[dependencies.quickjs]\ntype    = \"archive\"\nurl     = \"https://bellard.org/quickjs/quickjs-2026-06-04.tar.xz\"\nsha256  = \"b376e839b322978313d929fd20663b11ba58b75df5a46c126dd19ea2fa70ad2a\"\nexclude = [\"qjs.c\", \"qjsc.c\", \"run-test262.c\", \"unicode_gen.c\"]   # programs, not the library\n\nsha256 is required. The download is checked against it before anything is unpacked, and a mismatch stops the build. Get it with sha256sum file.tar.gz.\nurl may also be the path of a tarball already on disk, relative to the project.\nsubdir names the folder inside the archive where the library's source starts, when it is not at the top: subdir = \"cdf39_1-dist/src\". An archive whose top level is a single folder needs no subdir.\nOnce unpacked it is treated exactly like a git clone: same header folders, same skipped folders, same exclude.","title":"A release tarball"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#An-installed-system-package","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"[dependencies.zlib]\ntype       = \"system\"\npkg_config = \"zlib\"\n\nNothing is compiled. pkg-config --cflags --libs zlib supplies the include folders, the link folders and the libraries, so that your own sources can include and link the installed package. It needs pkg-config and the package's development files on the machine, and the recipe then only builds where they are installed.","title":"An installed system package"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Side-by-side","page":"Where the source comes from","text":" Project files local git and archive\nWhich files are compiled Those in source_files All found in the folder All found in the clone or archive\nFound again on each build No Yes Yes\nFolders skipped without being asked (none; the list is explicit) build, .git, .cache test, tests, testes, example, examples, fuzzing, build, doc, docs, .git\nexclude applies No: delete the line from source_files Yes Yes\nHeaders searched in include_dirs only The folder, include/ The top folder, include/, src/\nCompiled libraries inside are linked No Yes No\nChecked against Nothing Nothing commit / sha256\n\nSource files are those ending in .c, .cpp, .cc or .cxx.\n\nexclude entries match a file's name, or any part of its path inside the dependency: \"tools/\" removes a folder, \"qjs.c\" one file.","title":"Side by side"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Using-several-at-once","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"Everything adds up. A recipe's library is built from its source_files, plus every dependency's files, with the include folders of all of them. Real recipes combine sources routinely:\n\nA fetched library plus files of your own. The library comes from git; a file beside the recipe adds to it.\n\n[dependencies.hol]\ntype   = \"git\"\nurl    = \"https://github.com/example/hol.git\"\ntag    = \"v1.0\"\ncommit = \"…\"\n\n[compile]\nsource_files = [\"shim/hol_keep.cpp\"]      # yours, in the project folder\n\nA fetched library plus a folder of generated files. llama.cpp's Vulkan shaders are produced by a generator the build cannot run. They are generated once, kept in a folder beside the recipe, and fed in as a second dependency:\n\n[dependencies.llamacpp]\ntype    = \"git\"\nurl     = \"https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp.git\"\ntag     = \"b10286\"\ncommit  = \"cd0fa6051abd04fd7f82ccb42ad378ec43885455\"\nexclude = [\"common/\", \"tools/\"]           # and about thirty more\n\n[dependencies.vkshaders]\ntype = \"local\"\npath = \"vulkan\"                           # generated .cpp files, kept in the recipe's folder\n\nA fetched library plus headers its build system would generate. The captured config.h lives in a folder beside the recipe and is found through include_dirs (Headers the build system generates):\n\n[dependencies.pcre2]\ntype   = \"git\"\nurl    = \"https://github.com/PCRE2Project/pcre2.git\"\ntag    = \"pcre2-10.45\"\ncommit = \"2dce7761b1831fd3f82a9c2bd5476259d945da4d\"\n\n[compile]\ninclude_dirs = [\"config\"]                 # the captured config.h and pcre2.h\nsource_files = [\"config/pcre2_chartables.c\"]\n\nThe order of dependencies does not matter. When two of them supply a file with the same function, the link fails on the duplicate: exclude one.","title":"Using several at once"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Git-submodules","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"A clone does not fetch submodules. For most libraries that is right. For a repository that is mostly submodules, such as boostorg/boost, list the ones you need:\n\n[dependencies.boost]\ntype       = \"git\"\nurl        = \"https://github.com/boostorg/boost.git\"\ntag        = \"boost-1.86.0\"\ncommit     = \"<the 40-character commit of that tag>\"\nsubmodules = [\"libs/filesystem\", \"libs/config\", \"libs/core\", \"libs/assert\"]\nexclude    = [\"libs/config/checks/\", \"libs/config/tools/\"]\n\nOnly the listed submodules are fetched, each at the commit the main repository records for it. The main repository's commit therefore fixes every submodule too.\nA submodule's own submodules are not fetched and cannot be listed.\nEach fetched submodule is treated like the clone itself: its top folder, include/ and src/ are searched for headers, and its sources are compiled.\nexclude paths start from the main repository's top: libs/config/checks/.","title":"Git submodules"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Which-submodules-to-list","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"List every submodule whose headers your library includes, directly or through another header. A list that is too short fails clearly, with a missing header at compile time. A list that is too long compiles code that is not your library.\n\nBuild and add. Start with the library's own submodule. Build. Add the submodule that owns each header the compiler reports missing. Repeat.\nUse the repository's own tool, when it has one. In a checkout of Boost:\npython tools/boostdep/depinst/depinst.py -X test -X example -X doc filesystem\ngit submodule status | grep -v '^-'      # what was fetched; leave out libs/headers and tools/\n\nA fetched header-only submodule often still contains .cpp files that are not library code: configuration checks, generators, benchmarks. Exclude them. Header-only libraries has more on Boost.","title":"Which submodules to list"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Seeing-what-was-fed-in","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"build prints the totals, after one line per dependency:\n\n  dependency: resolving ext (local)\nRepliBuild | feed\n  sources: 3  includes: 3\n  compile: 3 files\n\nIf sources is not the number you expect, something was found that you did not intend, or something was not found. A main function in the wrapper (names(M)) means a program or a test was compiled in.","title":"Seeing what was fed in"},{"category":"section","location":"dependencies/#Fetching-again","page":"Where the source comes from","text":"RepliBuild.clean(toml)                 # deletes fetched sources too; the next build downloads again\nRepliBuild.clean(toml; deps = false)   # keeps them; the next build checks them against the recipe","title":"Fetching again"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#Changelog","page":"Changelog","text":"The full, dated history is CHANGELOG.md in the repository. This page is what a user upgrading should know.\n\nThis manual is built from main. Pkg.add(\"RepliBuild\") installs the newest registered release, which can be older than what is described here; pkgversion(RepliBuild) says which version you have.","title":"Changelog"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#After-any-upgrade","page":"Changelog","text":"Generator fixes reach a wrapper when it is generated again. Run wrap(toml) for a wrapper you include; a wrapper written by an older RepliBuild warns when it loads. Packages loaded through use() are re-wrapped by themselves, because its cache key includes RepliBuild's version.\nFixes in what build extracts (the metadata) need a rebuild as well, and a build whose inputs did not change is skipped — by use() too. Run build(toml; clean=true) before wrap, or delete .replibuild_cache/project_hash and run build(toml), which keeps the compiled IR. For a registered package, call use(name; force_rebuild=true) once. The notes below say when a release has such fixes.\nOn Windows, build the JLCS dialect again: the new version lives in a new folder (Set up).","title":"After any upgrade"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.5.0-(2026-11-14)","page":"Changelog","text":"Every C++ wrapper must be generated again, and the project is rebuilt for the new decoders. The Tier 2 thunk for a function returning a record C++ passes by reference (std::string, std::map, any class with a non-trivial destructor) changed its convention and its name: a wrapper written by 4.4.0 or earlier fails to load with a message saying to re-wrap, instead of calling the old thunk. Linux AArch64 is supported, a std::string returned by value is a Julia String, and overloads that were one method are separate methods. Julia 1.12 is the oldest release supported (Project.toml admitted 1.10 and 1.11, which nothing tested).\n\nLinux AArch64. The AAPCS64 struct rules (a homogeneous floating aggregate in floating registers, 16 bytes or less in general registers, larger by reference) on both tiers, and the JIT fixes the first arm64 run needed. CI runs devtests on an arm64 runner. macOS is still refused, and Windows on ARM is not built.\nstd::string returned by value is a String. The wrapper reads the characters where C++ built the object and ends it there; libstdc++'s and libc++'s layouts, char strings only. A string the wrapper cannot decode keeps its bytes, with the reason in a comment beside the call. Needs a rebuild: the layout is recorded at build time. A std::string parameter still takes the byte stand-in (on the Not Yet Built list).\nstd::tuple, std::optional and std::complex by value are callable, laid out from their bases; std::complex<long double> on both conventions.\nPass-by-reference returns were built in a dead frame. Every Tier 2 function returning such a record moved the object's bytes out of the thunk's own stack; it held while the bytes meant the same at any address, and libstdc++'s short string does not. The object is now built in the caller's buffer. This is the thunk convention change above.\nC++ overloads dispatch on a pointer's target and on an enum. GetID(const char*) and GetID(const void*), or f(Kind) and f(int), were one Julia signature, and the one kept was whichever came last. The positions an overload set disagrees on now take a Union of what callers pass (String, Ptr, Ref, a Managed object, an AbstractVector), or the enum itself; every other pointer position stays ::Any. An argument outside that Union is a MethodError where it used to pick an overload. An rvalue overload beside its const& twin is not emitted (a move where the caller meant a copy).\n[wrap.rename]. A table of key = \"julia_name\", keyed by the mangled symbol or the derived Julia name (anchored globs), applied inside the one name derivation so every emitter binds the chosen name. For Boost.Math's 600-character template names (TOML reference).\nWrapper names and types that change — re-wrap, and update callers:\nC enums are open types (primitive type E <: RepliBuild.OpenEnums.OpenEnum) instead of @enum: any value of the underlying type is a value (VTERM_KEY_FUNCTION(n)), declared values print by name, undeclared ones as E(v). Integer conversion and ccall passing are unchanged.\nA pointer or reference return is Ptr{T} when the wrapper emits T (opaque handles included); void*, function pointers and mutable records stay Ptr{Cvoid}. Typing is not ownership: no finalizer is attached.\n[types] templates entries written through a typedef (std::vector<Point64>) yield the container's table and a factory named as written; an entry that yields nothing is a warning, not an empty table.\nGraph mode (a CMake project as one package): one Julia type per C++ type across the graph's libraries, and an imported enum brings its values (World.Happy === Hello.Happy); the project can be a pinned [dependencies] checkout ([graph] dependency); object-library sources, versioned sonames and file-local types (libwebp builds).\nCalls that crossed wrong:\nA C record whose eightbyte holds a float beside integers or bytes (a union of int and double, a float next to a bitfield) was an ABI trap; it crosses as C does. The last 31 Hub C stubs are calls.\nA C++ record over-aligned only through a member's alignas was read from the wrong stack slot by ccall; it goes to Tier 2.\nA derived class whose trivial destructor aliases its base's (tinyxml2's ~XMLComment) carried the base's destructor symbol, so its finalizer ran the base destructor.\nA method proxy's owner could be collected during a Tier 2 call; it is kept alive across the call.\nA thunk's callee in the C++ runtime library (a basic_string member the library calls but does not instantiate) binds through the library's own handle; one the handle cannot resolve is dropped with a warning instead of blocking the JIT.\nBuilds and tooling:\nA git dependency prints its clone and fetch progress at a terminal.\nThe Tier 1 slice cache is per libLLVM major, so two Julia releases on one checkout no longer re-slice each other on every wrap.\ntest/suites.jl is the one list of test files; test/devtests.jl --changed runs what a diff touches. test/tools/cpp_thunk_coverage.jl measures C++ coverage on the Hub as c_ccall_coverage.jl does for C.\nFixes in what build extracts — rebuild, then re-wrap: the string layout; toolchain records laid out from bases; destructor attribution for aliased destructors.","title":"v4.5.0 (2026-11-14)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.4.0-(2026-11-08)","page":"Changelog","text":"A function that returns or takes std::pair, std::array or std::string_view by value is callable, and a dependency can be a tarball. Generated wrappers change some names and parameter types (below), so this is a minor release.\n\nStandard-library records by value. A record the library passes, returns or holds by value keeps its layout when it is declared in a toolchain header, and the wrapper emits it as a struct (pair_double_double, with first and second). This covers std::pair in every shape, std::array, std::string_view, and a library struct with one of them as a member. Only what an exported function crosses by value is kept; the standard library's own internals are not. std::tuple, std::optional and std::complex are still ABI traps. A struct that derives from an empty base (std::pair does, and so does a library struct P : Tag { … }) crosses by value; it was an ABI trap.\nArchive dependencies. [dependencies.<n>] type = \"archive\" with url and a required sha256 fetches a tarball (any format Pkg reads), checks the hash before unpacking, and walks it like a git checkout, from subdir or the archive's single top directory. A changed pin fetches again (TOML reference). A local dependency's exclude is now applied, and its headers count as the project's own for the shim-header check.\nWrapper names and types that change — re-wrap, and update callers:\nAn enumerator whose name is also a struct, function, global, constant or another enum's member is bound as <enum>_<name> (Boost.JSON's kind::object is kind_object, because object is a struct). Before, the later definition silently replaced it. An enumerator that collides with nothing keeps its name, and a wrapper that would bind any name twice is refused when it is written.\nA parameter that is a reference to a type Julia cannot hold (long double&, __int128&) is Ptr{Cvoid}, the address of a buffer you own, instead of Ref{Any}.\nC++ overloads that differ only in an integer parameter's width are separate methods: those positions keep their C type (value::Cint, value::Clonglong), and the rest stay ::Integer.\nCalls that crossed wrong:\nA Tier 2 integer argument narrower than its C parameter (Int8 for an int) was read past its storage and gave a different value on every call.\nA function returning a pointer or reference to a standard container (path::native()) was declared as returning the container itself, and the caller got an address on the thunk's stack.\nA record with a * or & inside its template arguments (pair<T*, bool>, result<T&, E>) was treated as a pointer; on Windows that was an access violation.\nSIMD vectors (__m128, vector_size) passed by value, bare or inside a record, crossed lane by lane on both tiers; they now classify by their size or are refused, on Linux and Windows.\nA C++ name containing \\x (a char template argument) failed the whole library's Tier 2 module, and so did two records whose names differ only in punctuation.\nA record outside namespace std named like a standard one (boost::core::string_view) was skipped as standard-library internal.\nBuilds:\nA failed build no longer leaves the previous build's project hash, so the next build cannot report \"project unchanged\" over a broken library. A compiler that never started counts as a failure.\nFedora's binutils are recognised as GNU binutils.\nFixes in what build extracts — rebuild, then re-wrap: the toolchain records and empty bases above; the scope recorded on each struct (the std check); SIMD vector facts, including a 3-lane vector's size; nounwind for a C++ library linked from several IR modules.","title":"v4.4.0 (2026-11-08)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.3.1-(2026-10-31)","page":"Changelog","text":"Multi-library projects build and wrap from CMake's own build graph.\n\nGraph mode. A [graph] section (TOML reference) builds a CMake project of several libraries. RepliBuild runs only CMake's configure step and reads its File API: every target, its sources, flags, defines and include path, including what it inherits from the libraries it links. It runs the build steps that write generated sources, compiles and links each library itself (C libraries with Julia's clang, C++ with the system clang), and extracts each one's metadata. wrap writes one wrapper per library and a graph module that includes them in load order, so G.Shapes.f(…) calls a function of the shapes library. A library named like a Julia binding gets an R (Core → RCore). register and use handle the graph as one unit. Types are still per library: a struct two libraries share is a different Julia type in each wrapper.\nGit submodules. [dependencies.<n>] submodules = [\"libs/math\"] fetches exactly those submodules of a git dependency, at the commits its pinned commit records, so a library inside a superproject such as boostorg/boost no longer needs a dependency of its own.\n[wrap] shim_defines builds a single-header library's implementation (STB_IMAGE_IMPLEMENTATION) from the TOML, in the shim file only.\nC++ finalizers free what they own. A Managed<X> or factory struct calls the library's deleter function for the class, else its deleting destructor; a class with neither gets no owning type. Re-wrap to pick it up.\nA Tier 2 record holding a template member (std::array, std::pair, a project template) keeps its size when it crosses by value.\nclean(toml; deps=false) keeps the vendored git clones, so a rebuild from cold checks their pins instead of fetching them again.\ningest registers the way discover does: only with build=true, wrap=true, after wrapping. A bare call writes the TOML and nothing else.\nPkg.build builds the JLCS dialect on Windows, and rebuilds it after an LLVM minor upgrade.\nFixes in what build extracts — rebuild, then re-wrap:\nTier 2 on Linux classifies a record passed or returned by value from its DWARF facts, not from the body it crosses as. Records with padding, nested records, unions, bitfields, long double or __int128 crossed wrong.\nTier 3 on Linux places a by-value record C aligns to 16 in the stack slot C reads, or refuses the call.\nTemplate parameters are read on Linux, and const void * is recorded as itself instead of an unknown type.\nOn Linux every library binds its own exported functions to itself (-Bsymbolic), and Tier 1 slices name them by alias: a library that Julia also loads (zlib) no longer calls Julia's copy.\nsurface_types_only keeps the records a signature spells with template arguments.\nHeader enums come only from the project's own headers, and scaffold_package includes the wrapper file wrap writes.\nParallel builds no longer clear the caller's LD_LIBRARY_PATH and CPATH.","title":"v4.3.1 (2026-10-31)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.3.0-(2026-10-23)","page":"Changelog","text":"The registry builds a project where it lives; AOT thunks come from wrap. register and use change what they do (below), so this is a minor release. v4.2.1 was never registered: its changes reach users with this release.\n\nABI fixes from the differential fuzzer, which generates C records and functions and checks both tiers against the compiler.\nTier 3 (C): whether a record comes back in registers or through a hidden buffer is decided per target, from real alignment; that buffer has the record's alignment; one long double parameter refuses that function, not the whole wrapper; records and enums named after Julia keywords are spelled as declared; a module name never starts with a digit.\nTier 2 (C++): unions and bitfield records pass and return by value as their bytes; #pragma pack bodies stay packed; bitfields wider than 32 bits get working accessors.\nSwitching Julia or the C++ toolchain rebuilds what the switch affects.\nRebuild, then re-wrap. Member sizes, record alignment, names that several types share, and function-pointer parameters are now recorded in the metadata. The first build also compiles every file once more, because IR file names changed.\nThe registry builds a project where it lives.\nregister builds and wraps the project in place, vendors the build to ~/.replibuild/builds/<hash>/, and adds a Julia package around it to your Julia environment (env, default the @v#.# one). using MyProject loads it from there, and use(\"myproject\") loads it too.\nuse follows edits by rebuilding the project in place, and loads the last vendored build if the project has moved.\nA bare discover no longer registers anything; discover(...; build=true, wrap=true) ends by registering.\nThe registry no longer copies a project's sources into itself, so two projects with the same relative paths can no longer overwrite each other.\nA project registered by an earlier RepliBuild keeps building the copy that version made. Register it again, from the project.\nAOT thunks in one pass. With [compile] aot_thunks = true, wrap compiles the thunks library as its last step, for exactly the thunks the wrapper binds; build no longer compiles thunks. A new package used to need build and wrap twice.\nThe same source wraps the same on Julia 1.12 and 1.13 and after an upstream edit. A regenerated wrapper changes once: lambda closure types are no longer emitted, and where several types share a name, the definition kept is chosen by content.\nA call that passes by value a type whose name several types share is an ABI-trap stub that says why, instead of using whichever layout was kept.\nFunction-pointer types carry their parameter lists, and a callback's @cfunction example comes from its own typedef or DWARF signature, never from a name match.\nWindows links are reproducible: the same IR gives the same DLL, so LIBRARY_SHA changes only when the library does. A link longer than one Windows command line goes through a response file.\nscaffold_package writes a RepliBuild compat entry that admits the RepliBuild that generated the package. It wrote RepliBuild = \"2\", which resolved RepliBuild 2.x.","title":"v4.3.0 (2026-10-23)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.2.1-(2026-10-17,-released-in-v4.3.0)","page":"Changelog","text":"LLVM.jl 10. LLVM.jl compat widens to 9, 10. Tier 1 (experimental, off by default) reads the names LLVM.jl 10 changed the meaning of, so it behaves the same on both, and a Slicer exception demotes that one function instead of failing the wrap. On Windows, [wrap.tier1] allow_setjmp is ignored with a warning. No API change; a 4.2.0 wrapper keeps working.","title":"v4.2.1 (2026-10-17, released in v4.3.0)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.2.0-(2026-10-16)","page":"Changelog","text":"LLVM 23 and Windows, end to end. New: [platform.<host>] tables in replibuild.toml, JSON 1.x, the JLCS dialect on LLVM/MLIR 23, and end-to-end coverage of AOT thunks. Behaviour changes: relative [compile] paths resolve against the project root, codegen runs at [link] optimization_level, and a C++ package compiles its .c files with the system clang. A 4.1.1 wrapper keeps working. Build-time fixes, the libc++ exports among them, need a rebuild; generator fixes need a re-wrap.","title":"v4.2.0 (2026-10-16)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.1.0-and-v4.1.1-(2026-09-24,-2026-09-27)","page":"Changelog","text":"Bounding the wrap surface. RepliBuild.DAGDiff is removed (it was exported), and [wrap] dag is ignored with a warning. New keys, all opt-in: [compile] visibility, [wrap] exclude_symbols, [wrap] surface_types_only and surface_types_extra. [link] link_dirs now emits an rpath as well as -L. 4.1.1 is a patch of generator and DWARF-reader fixes: no new keys, nothing removed, and a 4.1.0 wrapper is unchanged until it is regenerated.","title":"v4.1.0 and v4.1.1 (2026-09-24, 2026-09-27)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#v4.0.0-and-v4.0.1-(2026-09-05,-2026-09-13)","page":"Changelog","text":"Windows is supported. x86_64-w64-windows-gnu (mingw, MSYS2 CLANG64) — not MSVC. See Install. macOS is still refused at load, because the AAPCS64 ABI classifier is not built.\nC bitflag enums are integer aliases, not @enums. This appears when you regenerate a wrapper; existing ones are unchanged until then. An enum whose members are a mask now emits const Flags = Cuint plus named constants, so A | B works and can be passed. Detection is conservative — 59 of 544 enums across the shipped Hub wrappers reclassify — but if you had been matching on an enum instance, or printing one and expecting its name, that is what changed. Ptr{Flags}, ccall arguments typed Flags, and export all behave as before.\n4.0.1 made Julia 1.13 (libLLVM 20) work. No API changes.","title":"v4.0.0 and v4.0.1 (2026-09-05, 2026-09-13)"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#What-still-matters-from-the-3.x-line","page":"Changelog","text":"Calling code written against older generated APIs may need these updates:\n\nA pointer parameter takes a pointer or a Ref. Overloads that accepted a struct value for an S* parameter were removed: the callee got a pointer to a temporary copy. A by-value struct parameter still takes the value.\nchar* returns are Union{String,Nothing}. NULL is nothing, not an exception. Declare malloc'd returns in [wrap.cstring_owned]. Every such function has a raw <name>_ptr sibling.\n[wrap.varargs] lists Julia types, variadic arguments only, as a list of lists: f = [[\"Cint\"]]. C types belong in [wrap.macros], not here.\ndiscover(force=true) preserves hand-edited keys (templates, macros, varargs, shim headers, cstring ownership). Keep the rest of a hand-written config in version control anyway.\nVendored wrappers resolve their .so sibling-first, so a copy in lib/ stays bound to the .so next to it.","title":"What still matters from the 3.x line"},{"category":"section","location":"release-notes/#Where-to-read-more","page":"Changelog","text":"CHANGELOG.md — every release, including the v3.0 generated-API break and the inheritance work in v3.0.1.\nDated engineering notes (contributors): docs/updates/ in the repo.","title":"Where to read more"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#The-inheritance-ABI","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"note: Developer documentation\nThis page is for contributors. To call the generated upcast helpers, see Call a wrapper. The user manual starts at Home.\n\nA reasoning guide to how RepliBuild makes C++ inheritance — multiple inheritance (MI) and virtual inheritance (VMI) — callable from Julia. This page teaches the mental model so you can read a generated wrapper and know what it's doing. For the dated, blow-by-blow record of how it was built, see the update notes: MI + vcall and virtual inheritance. For the dialect these decisions are expressed in — jlcs.vcall, jlcs.type_info and their lowering — see ABI Marshalling as Compiler IR.","title":"The inheritance ABI"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#The-one-invariant","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"Everything below follows from a single rule:\n\nnote: The invariant\nEvery generated Class_method(this, …) wrapper expects this to already point at the Class subobject — not at the start of whatever derived object happens to contain it.\n\nBecause the wrapper knows the static type at each call site, it can choose the right offset to turn a derived pointer into the base pointer the method wants. That conversion is the upcast helper. Dispatch itself stays dumb and class-local; all the difficulty of inheritance is pushed into \"given a Derived, produce the correct base-subobject pointer.\"\n\nSo there is really only one question to answer, in three increasingly hard forms:\n\nCase Where is the base? Upcast\nSingle / primary base offset 0 none — a Derived* already is a Base*\nMultiple inheritance a fixed offset add a compile-time constant\nVirtual inheritance depends on the most-derived type read the offset from the object's vtable at runtime","title":"The one invariant"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#1.-Single-inheritance-—-nothing-to-do","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"The base sits at offset 0, so the derived pointer is bit-for-bit a valid base pointer. No helper is emitted; you pass the handle straight through.","title":"1. Single inheritance — nothing to do"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#2.-Multiple-inheritance-—-add-a-constant","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"For Derived : Base1, Base2, the object is three consecutive regions:\n\nOffset Region Upcast from a Derived* p\n0 Base1 subobject (vptr1, data) none — p already is a Base1*\n16 Base2 subobject (vptr2, data) Derived_as_Base2(p) = p + 16\n32 Derived's own data —\n\nThe +16 is not guessed — it is DW_AT_data_member_location on the DW_TAG_inheritance edge in DWARF, captured at extraction time. The generated helper is exactly:\n\nfunction Derived_as_Base2(obj)::Ptr{Cvoid}\n    p = obj isa Ptr ? Ptr{Cvoid}(obj) : Ptr{Cvoid}(obj.handle)\n    return p + 16                       # static offset, known at compile time\nend\n\nBase1 gets no helper (offset 0). Base members are handled the same way: flatten_struct_members rebases each inherited member by its base's subobject offset, so derived.some_base2_field reads from the right place.","title":"2. Multiple inheritance — add a constant"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#3.-Virtual-inheritance-—-read-the-offset-from-the-vtable","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"A diamond shares one copy of the virtual base. The catch: the shared base's offset is a property of the most-derived object, not of the class. A standalone Left puts VBase at +16; a Left embedded in a Diamond puts it at +32. A Left* cannot know statically which world it is in — only the object's vtable does.\n\nFor Diamond : Left, Right where both Left and Right derive virtually from VBase, there is exactly one shared copy of VBase, and a Diamond* p lays out as:\n\nOffset Region\n0 Left subobject (vptr_L) — p points here\n16 Right subobject (vptr_R)\n28 Diamond's own data (d)\n32 shared VBase (vptr_V) — one copy, reached by both Left and Right\n\nThe offset to that shared VBase isn't a class constant — it lives in the vtable. Left's vtable (what vptr_L points into) carries the vbase-offset entry just below its address point:\n\nVtable position Contents\nvptr_L − 24 vbase-offset = 32 — the entry the upcast reads\nvptr_L (address point) RTTI, &tag(), the virtual method slots\n\nso Left_as_VBase(p) = p + *(vptr_L − 24) = p + 32.\n\nThe DWARF location on a virtual edge is an expression, not a constant (this + *(vptr − 24)); the parser distills it into vbase_vtable_offset = −24. The generated helper does the two-hop read — load the vptr, read the vbase-offset entry below the address point, add it:\n\nfunction Left_as_VBase(obj)::Ptr{Cvoid}\n    p    = obj isa Ptr ? Ptr{Cvoid}(obj) : Ptr{Cvoid}(obj.handle)\n    vptr = unsafe_load(Ptr{Ptr{Int64}}(p))   # load the vtable pointer\n    return p + unsafe_load(vptr + (-24))      # read the dynamic vbase offset\nend\n\ntip: The canary that proves it's dynamic\nLeft_as_VBase is one generated function, yet it resolves +16 on a standalone Left and +32 on a Left* that is really a Diamond — because it reads the answer out of each object's own vtable. Any static implementation gets one of the two wrong. This is the pin in test/vi_test/verify.jl.\n\nWhen a virtual base is reached through a static chain (e.g. Diamond_as_VBase = static +0 to Left, then dynamic), the helper composes: apply the static prefix to p first, then do the vtable read. One helper text is correct for every dynamic type.","title":"3. Virtual inheritance — read the offset from the vtable"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#Virtual-dispatch-and-overrides-—-why-you-never-do-slot-math","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"You might expect dispatching a virtual method on a secondary or virtual base to require walking vtables and adjusting this. It doesn't, because the C++ compiler already did the hard part:\n\nIt re-homes an override into the derived class's own primary vtable — an override of Base2::get_b in Derived reports its slot in Derived's vtable, not Base2's. Diamond::tag re-homes the same way.\nIt plants this-adjusting thunks in the secondary vtables that fix the pointer back before entering the override.\n\nSo RepliBuild dispatches class-local: index the method's own slot, with a class-relative this — vtable_offset = 0, this_offset = 0. The caller-side upcast plus the compiler's thunks do the pointer adjustment; the jlcs.vcall lowering never has to. This is why virtual inheritance needed zero changes to the dialect or the thunk generator — the only new machinery was the dynamic read in the Julia upcast helper.\n\nTwo deliberate exceptions:\n\nDestructors stay direct calls. Managed finalizers and scope-RAII need exact-class destruction, not dynamic dispatch. Polymorphic deletion through a base pointer goes through a small C++-side helper.\nvcall gates on scalar/pointer signatures. Virtual methods that return a struct by value or take a packed struct by value keep the direct-call path (the vcall lowering does no sret/packed coercion yet).","title":"Virtual dispatch and overrides — why you never do slot math"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#Where-each-piece-lives","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"Three seams turn \"what the C++ ABI says\" into \"something Julia can call\":\n\nExtraction — DWARFParser.jl + Compiler.jl. Captures the static base offset (DW_AT_data_member_location) and parses the virtual edge's location expression into vbase_vtable_offset. ClassInfo carries parallel base_offsets / virtual_bases / vbase_vtable_offsets vectors; bases are sorted by subobject offset for determinism.\nMetadata — jlcs.type_info carries two tables: baseNames/baseOffsets (static) and vbaseNames/vbaseVtableOffsets (dynamic). Virtual bases never appear in the static table.\nWrapper — GeneratorCpp.jl. _collect_upcasts! (src/Wrapper/Cpp/GeneratorCpp.jl:1539) walks the base DAG accumulating static offsets, then emits either the static helper (:1591) or the dynamic helper (:1613).","title":"Where each piece lives"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#Reading-and-using-a-generated-wrapper","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"The whole integration contract, at a glance:\n\nX_as_Y(p) = p + N → Y is a normal (MI) base at fixed offset N.\nX_as_Y(p) = p + unsafe_load(vptr + N) → Y is a virtual base; the offset is read from the vtable.\nTo call a Y method on an X object, pass X_as_Y(obj) as the handle. That is the only rule you have to remember — the compiler pre-arranged everything else.\nMembers: MI base members are flattened onto the derived accessor (rebased by offset). Virtual base members are deliberately not flattened — their offset is dynamic, so you reach them by upcasting (X_as_VBase) and using the base's own accessors.","title":"Reading and using a generated wrapper"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#What-is-deliberately-not-built","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"So you don't chase these as bugs — they're unbuilt pieces, tracked alongside the rest in Boundaries:\n\nvcall dispatch for struct-shaped virtual signatures (see the gate above).\nA dialect-level virtual-base upcast op, for IR that must do the adjustment inside a thunk. Nothing needs one today — the caller-side dynamic upcast above composes with class-local vcall coordinates, which is why virtual inheritance needed zero dialect changes.\nThe struct ABI classifier is x86-64 SysV only; Win64 / AAPCS are not modeled.\n\n(MEMORY-class by-value struct arguments were on this list and are now built: they pass as an alloca'd caller-owned copy with llvm.byval(T) and explicit alignment, matching what clang emits. See the lowering.)","title":"What is deliberately not built"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance-abi/#Where-the-executable-truth-lives","page":"The inheritance ABI","text":"test/mi_test/ — compiler-laid-out two-base fixture (38/38): override through a secondary vtable, C++-handed base pointer, a pinned wrong-this canary.\ntest/vi_test/ — the diamond (33/33): the same-helper 16-vs-32 canary, single shared copy across three paths, override dispatch through the vbase vtable.\nDialect level: MLIR templates §8b (MI vcall) and §8d (the diamond).","title":"Where the executable truth lives"},{"category":"section","location":"#RepliBuild","page":"Home","text":"RepliBuild turns the source of a C or C++ library into a Julia module that calls it. It fetches the source, compiles the library, works out every function, struct, enum and class from the result, and writes the module.\n\nIt does everything except what has to be stated, and what has to be stated goes in one file, replibuild.toml. That is the design: where the source is, how the library is meant to be built, and the few things no compiler can know. Using RepliBuild well is knowing what to put in that file.\n\nusing RepliBuild\n\ntoml = RepliBuild.discover(\"path/to/mylib\")     # writes replibuild.toml\nRepliBuild.build(toml)                          # compiles the library\nRepliBuild.wrap(toml)                           # writes julia/Mylib.jl\n\ninclude(\"path/to/mylib/julia/Mylib.jl\")\nusing .Mylib\n\nIt builds from source, on your machine. It does not replace Julia's prebuilt binary packages (JLLs); it is for a library you build yourself, at the version and with the flags you choose.","title":"RepliBuild"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-manual","page":"Home","text":"There are three things to do, and the manual is in that order.\n\n1. Set up. Install the package and run RepliBuild.check_environment(). RepliBuild brings some of its tools and takes the rest from your system; the page says which. C libraries need almost nothing installed. C++ libraries need clang++. Rust crates need cargo.\n\n2. Write the recipe. replibuild.toml is the only file you write.\n\n \nWrap your first library The whole process once, on a small example. Start here.\nWhat goes in the TOML The main page. What to look for in your library, and the entry each thing needs.\nEvery key The lookup table: every key, its default, what it does.\nWhere the source comes from How to feed it source: files in your project, a folder on disk, a git repository, a tarball, or several at once. Each behaves differently.\nSeveral libraries Many sources into one library, a CMake project of several libraries with shared types, or two wrappers that link each other.\nCMake projects The [graph] section in detail.\nHeader-only libraries Libraries, or parts of an API, that live in headers.\nRust crates A Rust crate, wrapped as it is: what cargo builds, the short recipe, and how its values look in Julia.\n\n3. Use the wrapper.\n\n \nCall the wrapper How C and C++ functions, structs, strings, objects and containers look in Julia.\nLoad it anywhere register a finished wrapper and load it by name.\nBuild a package on it Ship a Julia package that contains a wrapper.\nMake it fast What a call costs, C++ stubs compiled ahead of time, precompiling, and compiling a wrapper into a system image.\nDebug it Step from Julia into the library's own source, and through the generated C++ call stubs.\n\nWhen something goes wrong: Troubleshooting, which is indexed by error message.","title":"The manual"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-you-get","page":"Home","text":"The wrapper is a Julia module generated for one build of one library, and checked against that build. It is more than a list of foreign calls:\n\nIt is correct by construction. Every struct's layout, every signature and every enum value comes from the compiled library, not from reading headers. A struct Julia cannot lay out exactly is not guessed at. A function that cannot be called safely becomes one that raises, never one that corrupts memory.\nIt speaks C++. Methods, virtual dispatch, constructors and destructors, overloads, default arguments, classes passed by value, std::string, containers, inheritance. A C++ exception arrives as a Julia exception. Objects can be owned by Julia's garbage collector.\nIt is fast. A C function is called directly, at the cost of a hand-written call. C++ call stubs can be compiled ahead of time, the module precompiles inside a package, and the package can be compiled into a system image, so a program that calls C++ starts in about the time Julia does (Make it fast).\nIt can be debugged, all the way down. The library is built with its debug information, so a crash names the C or C++ source line and a debugger stops in the library's own code. The generated C++ call stubs have readable source too, and a debugger steps through them between your Julia call and the C++ method (Debug it).\nIt documents itself. Every function has a docstring with its Julia signature and C types, and the module answers questions about sizes and offsets.","title":"What you get"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-you-put-in-the-TOML","page":"Home","text":"RepliBuild finds the library's functions and types by itself. The recipe holds what it cannot find:\n\nwhere the source is: files in the project, a folder, a git repository at a commit, a tarball with a hash;\nwhich of those files are tests, examples or tools, and not the library;\nwhether it is one library or several;\nthe flags and defines the library's own build would pass;\nmacros that are part of the API;\nwhich extra arguments a printf-style function should accept;\nwhich returned strings the caller must free, and with what;\nC++ templates you want to use, such as std::vector<double>;\nwhat to leave out, and what to rename.\n\nA missing entry usually does not fail the build. It gives a wrapper that is missing that thing. What goes in the TOML is the checklist to go through for each library.","title":"What you put in the TOML"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-commands","page":"Home","text":"Command Does\ncheck_environment() Checks the tools on this machine and says how to install what is missing.\ndiscover(path) Scans a source folder and writes replibuild.toml. Keeps an existing one.\nbuild(toml) Compiles the library.\nwrap(toml) Writes the Julia module, julia/<Name>.jl.\ninfo(toml) Says whether the library and the module exist.\nclean(toml) Deletes everything built.\nregister(toml) Makes a finished wrapper loadable by name in any session.\nuse(\"name\") Loads a registered wrapper, rebuilding it if the project changed.\nlist_registry(), unregister(\"name\") Lists and removes registered wrappers.\nsearch(\"xml\") Lists ready-made recipes in RepliBuild-Hub.\nscaffold_package(\"Name\") Creates a Julia package that builds a library when installed.\n\nEach takes the path of a replibuild.toml, or of the folder holding one. Running a command again when nothing changed does no work. API has the full signatures.","title":"The commands"},{"category":"section","location":"#Requirements","page":"Home","text":"Linux (x86-64 or AArch64) or Windows (x86-64, with MSYS2). macOS is not supported.\nJulia 1.13 or 1.14. For Julia 1.12, use the RepliBuild 4.5 releases.\nFor C++ libraries, clang++ and LLVM 21 or newer.\n\nSet up has the details. This manual writes .so for a library file; on Windows it is .dll.","title":"Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#CMake-projects","page":"CMake projects","text":"An ordinary recipe builds one library from a list of files and one set of flags. Some projects cannot be described that way: their CMake build produces several libraries that link each other, gives individual files their own flags, or writes headers before compiling.\n\nFor those, add a [graph] section. RepliBuild then asks CMake how the project is built, builds every library that way with its own compiler, and wraps them as one Julia module with a submodule per library.\n\nSeveral libraries compares this with the other ways of handling more than one library.","title":"CMake projects"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#When-to-use-it","page":"CMake projects","text":"The project builds several libraries and you want each one, with one library's types usable in another's functions.\nFiles need their own flags (-mavx2 on some files only), or the project needs a generated config.h.\nSource files are generated during the build.\n\nOnly CMake projects work this way. Projects built with autotools or Meson do not.\n\nOn Linux nothing extra needs installing: RepliBuild brings cmake and ninja. On Windows, install the CMake and Ninja packages (Set up). A C++ library in the project needs the C++ setup as usual.","title":"When to use it"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#The-recipe","page":"CMake projects","text":"The smallest one, for a CMake project in the project folder:\n\n[project]\nname = \"hello_world\"        # the module: julia/HelloWorld.jl\n\n[graph]\nsource  = \".\"               # the folder holding CMakeLists.txt\ntargets = [\"world\"]         # the CMake target to wrap, plus the libraries it links\n\nThe CMake project can also be fetched. This recipe builds four libraries of libwebp from its git repository:\n\n[project]\nname = \"libwebp\"\n\n[dependencies.libwebp]\ntype   = \"git\"\nurl    = \"https://github.com/webmproject/libwebp.git\"\ntag    = \"v1.6.0\"\ncommit = \"4fa21912338357f89e4fd51cf2368325b59e9bd9\"\n\n[graph]\ndependency = \"libwebp\"                    # the CMake project is that dependency\ntargets    = [\"webpdemux\", \"libwebpmux\"]  # webp and sharpyuv come with them\noptions    = [\"-DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON\",\n              \"-DWEBP_BUILD_CWEBP=OFF\", \"-DWEBP_BUILD_DWEBP=OFF\"]\n\nKey What to put there\nsource The folder holding CMakeLists.txt. Default \".\". With dependency, a folder inside it.\ndependency The [dependencies] entry that is the CMake project: git, archive or local (Where the source comes from). Its exclude is not used; CMake decides which files are compiled.\ntargets The library targets you want, by their CMake names (the first argument of add_library). Leave it out for every library in the project.\noptions What you would pass to cmake on the command line. Use it to switch off tests, tools and examples, and to ask for shared libraries (-DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON) when the project leaves that open.\nbuild_type CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE. Default \"Release\".\n\ndiscover does not write a [graph] section. Add it by hand.\n\nThen build and wrap as usual:\n\nRepliBuild.build(\"replibuild.toml\")\nRepliBuild.wrap(\"replibuild.toml\")\nRepliBuild.info(\"replibuild.toml\")     # lists the libraries and their module names","title":"The recipe"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#Which-libraries-are-built","page":"CMake projects","text":"You name the targets you want. Every library those targets link, directly or through another, is built and wrapped too. In the libwebp recipe, webpdemux and libwebpmux both link webp, which links sharpyuv:\n\nsharpyuv ← webp ← libwebpmux\n                ← webpdemux\n\nAll four are built. A library that none of the chosen targets links (webpdecoder here) is not. A program cannot be chosen as a target.\n\nEach library is built as C or as C++ according to its own source files. Static and object libraries inside the project are folded into the shared libraries that use them.","title":"Which libraries are built"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#What-you-get","page":"CMake projects","text":"julia/\n├── Libwebp.jl            # the module you load\n├── graph.toml            # a list of the libraries and their module names\n├── sharpyuv/lib/         # one folder per library: the library file and its wrapper\n├── webp/lib/\n└── …\n\ninclude(\"julia/Libwebp.jl\")\n\nW  = Libwebp.Webp\nMX = Libwebp.Libwebpmux\nW.WebPGetEncoderVersion()\n\nLoad the top module, never one library's wrapper by itself. The libraries depend on each other and the top module loads them in the right order.\n\nModule names. The top module is named after the project (or [wrap] module_name). Each library's submodule is the CamelCase of its CMake target name. A name Julia already uses gets an R in front (Core becomes RCore). info(toml) prints all of them.","title":"What you get"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#Types-shared-between-libraries","page":"CMake projects","text":"A C or C++ type that several libraries use is one Julia type:\n\nHelloWorld.World.Vec2  === HelloWorld.Hello.Vec2     # true\nHelloWorld.World.Happy === HelloWorld.Hello.Happy    # an enum's values too\n\nSo a value, a pointer or an object that one library returns can be passed straight to another library's functions.\n\nTwo types count as the same only when they really are:\n\nSame name in two different namespaces: two types.\nA type declared in a source file, not a header: private to its library.\nThe same type with different layouts in two libraries: wrap stops with an error naming both. It never picks one silently.","title":"Types shared between libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#Rebuilding-and-registering","page":"CMake projects","text":"Unchanged files are not recompiled and unchanged libraries are not linked again. CMake's configure step runs on every build.\nclean(toml) removes everything built, and a fetched project; clean(toml; deps = false) keeps the fetched project.\nregister(toml) registers the whole project as one, and use(name) returns the top module (Load it anywhere).","title":"Rebuilding and registering"},{"category":"section","location":"graph/#Limits","page":"CMake projects","text":"The other recipe keys do not apply. [compile], [types], and the [wrap] keys for macros, variadic functions, renames and exclusions are not used in a graph project: flags come from CMake, and each library is wrapped as it is. A macro or static inline function therefore has no Julia function; call the exported function it forwards to. [link] optimization_level does apply.\nGenerated files that need one of the project's own programs (a code generator the project builds first) are refused, with the program named.\nTypes are shared within one project, not between two separately wrapped projects.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Build-a-package-on-it","page":"Build a package on it","text":"For a script, include the wrapper and call it. This page is the next step: a Julia package of your own that contains a wrapper, loads fast, and gives your users a Julia-style API.\n\nA complete example is BoxWorld in RepliBuild-Hub, a physics sandbox built on the Box2D wrapper.","title":"Build a package on it"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Two-layers","page":"Build a package on it","text":"The generated wrapper: everything in the project's julia/ folder. Copy it into your package unchanged. Never edit it; regenerate it with wrap.\nYour code: the types, defaults, names and lifetimes you want your users to see, written against the wrapper.\n\nAnything specific to how you want the library to feel in Julia goes in your code. That is what lets you regenerate the wrapper when the library is upgraded.","title":"Two layers"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Layout","page":"Build a package on it","text":"Copy the whole julia/ folder into your package, here as lib/:\n\nMyPkg/\n├── Project.toml            # RepliBuild and Libdl listed under [deps]\n├── lib/                    # the project's julia/ folder, unchanged\n│   ├── Box2d.jl\n│   ├── libbox2d.so\n│   └── …                   # the .json files beside them\n├── src/MyPkg.jl\n└── test/runtests.jl\n\nmodule MyPkg\n\nusing RepliBuild\n\ninclude(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"lib\", \"Box2d.jl\"))\nusing .Box2d\n\n# your API here\n\nend\n\nThe generated module loads two packages, RepliBuild and Libdl, and your package must declare both, or it fails to precompile with package MyPkg does not have Libdl in its dependencies:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"MyPkg\")\nPkg.add([\"RepliBuild\", \"Libdl\"])\n\nThe wrapper finds its library beside itself, so the copy works wherever the package is installed. Keep every file from julia/ together; the module reads the .json files when it loads.\n\nA CMake project's wrapper is copied the same way, as a tree: the whole julia/ folder, with its per-library folders left where they are. Include the top module.","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Deployment","page":"Build a package on it","text":"The library file is built for one system. A package that ships it works on that operating system and processor only. For several systems, either ship one build per system, or have the package build the library when it is installed (below).\n\nWhat a machine needs to run a wrapper it did not build:\n\nWrapper Linux Windows\nC RepliBuild, as a Julia dependency The same\nC++ The same. Nothing else to install MSYS2 CLANG64 with the MLIR package, C:\\msys64\\clang64\\bin on PATH, and Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\") (Set up)\n\nOn Linux, RepliBuild downloads its C++ support library by itself, and brings a copy of llvm-dwarfdump, which a C++ wrapper runs when it loads, for machines that have no LLVM. On Windows the support library is built on each machine.\n\nDo not strip the library. A C++ wrapper reads information from it when it loads.","title":"Deployment"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Ship-C-wrappers-with-their-stubs-compiled","page":"Build a package on it","text":"By default a C++ wrapper compiles its call stubs each time it loads. For a package you distribute, have wrap compile them once:\n\n[compile]\naot_thunks = true\n\nwrap then writes a second library, lib<name>_thunks.so, into julia/. Ship it with the rest. Loading is many times faster and calls are faster. Make it fast has the measurements, and how to go further with a system image.","title":"Ship C++ wrappers with their stubs compiled"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#A-package-that-builds-the-library","page":"Build a package on it","text":"RepliBuild.scaffold_package(\"MyLibWrapper\")     # writes ./MyLibWrapper/\n\nThat creates a package whose installation (Pkg.build) compiles and wraps the library on the user's machine:\n\nFile What it is\nProject.toml Depends on RepliBuild and Libdl, the two packages a wrapper loads.\nreplibuild.toml The recipe. If a project named MyLibWrapper or MyLib is registered, its recipe; otherwise a template to fill in.\ndeps/build.jl Runs check_environment, then build and wrap.\nsrc/MyLibWrapper.jl Includes the generated wrapper once it exists.\ntest/runtests.jl, .gitignore A test stub. Built files are ignored by git.\n\nBefore the first build, edit the template recipe: name the sources ([dependencies] or [compile] source_files), set [wrap] language, and set [link] enable_lto = false.\n\nA package scaffolded by a RepliBuild 4.5 release or older has a Project.toml that lists only RepliBuild, and fails to load once its wrapper exists. Add the missing dependency: Pkg.activate(\"MyLibWrapper\"); Pkg.add(\"Libdl\").\n\nEvery machine that installs such a package needs the full setup for the library's language.","title":"A package that builds the library"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Precompilation","page":"Build a package on it","text":"The generated module precompiles as part of your package, and the package can be compiled into a system image (Make it fast). Two rules for your own code:\n\nDo not store addresses in constants. A const computed from dlsym, cglobal or a pointer the library returned is evaluated when the package is precompiled, and is wrong in the next session. Use a Ref and fill it in your module's __init__.\nPlain data is fine. Sizes and offsets from M.struct_size and M.member_offset can be constants.","title":"Precompilation"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#If-a-C-library-fails-to-start","page":"Build a package on it","text":"When a C++ wrapper cannot prepare its calls at load (the support library is missing, or llvm-dwarfdump is not found), loading your package still succeeds. The wrapper logs Failed to initialize JIT for lib….so with the reason, and its C++ functions raise when called. Other wrapped libraries in the same session are not affected.\n\nRepliBuild.check_environment() on that machine says what is missing.","title":"If a C++ library fails to start"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Things-to-wrap-once","page":"Build a package on it","text":"The wrapper is a faithful copy of a C or C++ API. Your package hides the awkward parts behind ordinary Julia functions.\n\nObjects. Give each C++ object a Julia type that holds the pointer, creates the object in its constructor, and releases it in a finalizer:\n\nmutable struct World\n    mem::Vector{UInt8}            # the object's memory; kept alive by this field\n\n    function World(gravity)\n        mem = zeros(UInt8, Box2d.struct_size(:b2World))\n        Box2d.b2World_b2World(pointer(mem), Ref(gravity))     # construct in place\n        w = new(mem)\n        finalizer(destroy!, w)\n        return w\n    end\nend\n\nhandle(w::World) = pointer(w.mem)\n\nfunction destroy!(w::World)\n    isempty(w.mem) && return                                  # already destroyed\n    Box2d.b2World_destroy_b2World(handle(w))                  # run the destructor\n    empty!(w.mem)\n    return\nend\n\nKeep the memory reachable for as long as the object lives. Here the field does that.\nMake destruction safe to call twice, and offer it as a function, so users can release resources at a known point. The finalizer is the safety net.\nWithout aot_thunks, the first call of each C++ function does some one-time preparation, and a finalizer is a poor place for that. Either build with aot_thunks = true, or create and destroy one object in your module's __init__ so the destructor has been called once before any finalizer runs.\nA class whose only destructor wrapper is Class_delete_Class frees its own memory. It must come from the library (new), not from memory you allocated (Call the wrapper).\nWhere the wrapper has a Managed<Class> type, it already does all of this: use it.\n\nSizes and offsets. Take every one from M.struct_size and M.member_offset. They follow the library when it is upgraded; a number written in your code does not.\n\nMissing inline functions. A small function defined in a header outside any class (b2Dot(a, b)) may not be in the wrapper. Either get it compiled in (Header-only libraries) or write it in Julia against the struct's fields.","title":"Things to wrap once"},{"category":"section","location":"using-wrappers/#Testing","page":"Build a package on it","text":"RepliBuild tests that calls reach the library correctly. Test what your package adds:\n\ncreate, use, destroy, and destroy again;\nthe library's behaviour through your API;\nthe finalizer: drop an object, then GC.gc();\nfor a C++ library, at least one call that reaches C++, so that a library that failed to start fails the test.","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Set-up","page":"Set up","text":"Install the package, run the check, and install whatever the check says is missing. That is the whole setup.\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"RepliBuild\")\n\nusing RepliBuild\nRepliBuild.check_environment()\n\ncheck_environment() tries every program a build runs and prints, under each one that failed, the command that installs it. Run it again after each change until the summary says what you need:\n\nSummary line Means\nTier 3 (ccall) — C wrappers will build You can wrap C libraries.\nC++ builds — clang++ and LLVM 21+ found C++ sources will compile.\nTier 2 (MLIR thunks) — C++ methods, … You can wrap C++ libraries.\nRust crates — cargo and rustc found You can wrap Rust crates.\n\nWrapping C needs only the first line. Wrapping C++ needs the first three. Wrapping a Rust crate needs only the last: cargo and rustc, which are yours to install (Rust crates).","title":"Set up"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#What-you-need","page":"Set up","text":"RepliBuild runs on Linux (x86-64 and AArch64) and Windows (x86-64, with MSYS2). macOS is not supported: using RepliBuild refuses to load there.\n\nJulia 1.13 or 1.14. Julia 1.12 is served by the RepliBuild 4.5 releases, the last of which is 4.5.1.\n\nA build runs a compiler, a linker and a handful of other programs. Some of them RepliBuild brings with it, and some it takes from your system:\n\nProgram Used for Linux Windows\nC compiler (clang) Compiling C libraries Comes with RepliBuild Comes with RepliBuild, using MSYS2's headers and libraries\nC library headers and startup files Every build Your system (libc6-dev, glibc-devel) Your system (MSYS2 CLANG64)\nC++ compiler (clang++) and LLVM tools Compiling and running C++ libraries Your system, version 21 or newer Your system (MSYS2 CLANG64)\nLinker Linking C libraries Comes with RepliBuild Your system\nLinker Linking C++ libraries Your system Your system\nreadelf, nm, objdump Reading the built library Comes with RepliBuild Your system (MSYS2)\nllvm-dwarfdump Wrapping a C++ library, and loading its wrapper Your system's; RepliBuild's own copy when there is none Your system's; RepliBuild's own copy when there is none\ngit Git dependencies Comes with RepliBuild Your system\ncmake, ninja CMake projects Comes with RepliBuild Your system (MSYS2)\npkg-config system dependencies only Your system Your system\ncargo, rustc Rust crates only Your system (rustup) Not run yet\nlibJLCS RepliBuild's own C++ support library Downloaded with RepliBuild Built on your machine\n\nSo on Linux a C library needs almost nothing from the system, and a C++ library needs clang++ and LLVM. On Windows everything comes from MSYS2.\n\nThe programs that \"come with RepliBuild\" are a recent addition, and the system's are still used. On Linux RepliBuild tries its own copy of a program first and uses the one on PATH when its own copy cannot start. On Windows it is the other way round: the system's first. REPLIBUILD_TOOLS=system makes Linux prefer the system's programs too, and REPLIBUILD_TOOL_GIT=/path/to/git (and the like) names one program exactly (Settings). When a build behaves differently on two machines, which copy of a program ran is the first thing to compare; the check report prints the path of each.","title":"What you need"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Linux","page":"Set up","text":"","title":"Linux"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#C-libraries","page":"Set up","text":"Nothing to install on most machines. What RepliBuild takes from the system is the C library's headers and startup files, which a machine with gcc already has. If the check reports sysroot — cannot compile a TU that includes <stdio.h>, install them:\n\nsudo apt install libc6-dev        # Debian, Ubuntu\n\nsudo dnf install glibc-devel      # Fedora, RHEL\n\nArch has them as part of glibc.","title":"C libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#C-libraries-2","page":"Set up","text":"Install clang++ and the LLVM tools, version 21 or newer, and put them on PATH. Versions 22 and 23 are the ones tested.\n\n# Debian, Ubuntu: from apt.llvm.org (the distribution's own LLVM is usually too old)\nwget https://apt.llvm.org/llvm.sh && sudo bash llvm.sh 23\nsudo apt install clang-23 llvm-23\nexport PATH=\"/usr/lib/llvm-23/bin:$PATH\"     # add this line to your shell profile\n\nsudo dnf install clang llvm llvm-devel       # Fedora, RHEL\n\nsudo pacman -S clang llvm                    # Arch\n\nRepliBuild looks for these programs by name: clang++, llvm-config, llvm-link, opt and llvm-dwarfdump. On Debian and Ubuntu they are installed as clang++-23 and so on, with the plain names in /usr/lib/llvm-23/bin, which is why that directory goes on PATH. Check with clang++ --version.\n\nThe last piece is RepliBuild's own C++ support library, libJLCS (messages also call it \"the JLCS dialect\"). On x86-64 and AArch64 Linux Pkg.add downloads it ready-made, and you install nothing for it. The check shows it as\n\nOK   libJLCS        prebuilt (JLCS artifact, LLVM and MLIR linked in)\n\nIf the download was skipped (no network during Pkg.add), it is fetched the first time a C++ wrapper needs it, or by Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\").\n\nnote: The 4.5 releases use the system's programs for everything\nThis manual describes RepliBuild 4.6, which needs Julia 1.13. On Julia 1.12, Pkg.add installs a 4.5 release. Those bring only the C compiler: they use the system's readelf, nm, git, cmake, ninja, llvm-dwarfdump and linker, and do not download libJLCS. On those, install binutils and git as well, and build libJLCS as described under Building libJLCS yourself. pkgversion(RepliBuild) says which version you have.","title":"C++ libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Windows","page":"Set up","text":"RepliBuild on Windows builds with MSYS2, in its CLANG64 environment. Not MSVC, and not the MINGW64 or UCRT64 environments: CLANG64 is the one whose libraries are compatible with Julia's own.\n\nInstall MSYS2 from msys2.org into the default location, C:\\msys64. If you choose another, set the environment variable MSYS2_ROOT to it.\nOpen MSYS2 CLANG64 from the Start menu and run pacman -Syu. It closes the window partway through; open it again and repeat until there is nothing left to update.\nIn the same window, install the toolchain:\npacman -S --needed mingw-w64-clang-x86_64-toolchain mingw-w64-x86_64-binutils\nThat is enough for C libraries. For C++ libraries and CMake projects, also:\npacman -S --needed mingw-w64-clang-x86_64-mlir mingw-w64-clang-x86_64-cmake mingw-w64-clang-x86_64-ninja\nThe second package of the first command is spelled differently from the others on purpose (mingw-w64-x86_64-, not mingw-w64-clang-x86_64-): it is the GNU objdump RepliBuild reads libraries with, and the CLANG64 one is a different program.\nAdd C:\\msys64\\clang64\\bin to the Windows PATH (Settings → System → About → Advanced system settings → Environment Variables), then restart Julia. Julia can run from any terminal or editor; it does not have to be started from MSYS2.\nFor sources fetched with git, have a git on the Windows PATH, such as Git for Windows.\nFor C++, build the support library. With the packages above installed:\nusing Pkg; Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\")\nThen run check_environment() again. Pkg writes the build's output to a log file and prints its path.\n\nEach new RepliBuild version needs step 6 again. Pkg does it when it installs the version, as long as the toolchain is there. Do it yourself after MSYS2 upgrades LLVM (pacman -Syu): the check then reports libJLCS — built against LLVM X, but llvm-config is Y. Close every Julia session that is using RepliBuild first, because Windows will not replace a library a running program has open.\n\nSmart App Control. Windows 11 can refuse to load the libraries RepliBuild builds, because they are new and unsigned. The error is An Application Control policy has blocked this file. RepliBuild cannot sign what it builds; whether to change the policy is your decision. Troubleshooting says how to confirm that this is the cause.\n\nWorking from a git clone of RepliBuild on Windows: run git config --global core.longpaths true before cloning.","title":"Windows"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Reading-the-report","page":"Set up","text":"C bucket — Tier 3 (ccall) ~\n  OK   clang (JLL)    clang 20.1.8\n  OK   sysroot        system headers reachable\n\nDWARF extraction — required by every tier ~\n  OK   DWARF dumper   GNU readelf 2.47\n  OK   symbol reader  GNU nm 2.47\n\nC++ — Tier 2 (MLIR thunks) ~\n  OK   llvm-config    LLVM 23.1.5\n  OK   clang++        Clang 23.1.5\n  …\n  OK   libJLCS        prebuilt\n\nEach line is one program or library, with OK, WARN or FAIL, and the fix printed under anything that is not OK. You do not need to know what the section names mean. What each group is for:\n\nGroup Needed for If it fails\nC bucket Every build Nothing can be wrapped. On Linux, install the C library's development files. On Windows, the CLANG64 toolchain is missing or not found.\nDWARF extraction Every build Nothing can be wrapped. On Windows, install mingw-w64-x86_64-binutils.\nC++ C++ libraries only C libraries still work. Install clang++ and LLVM, and check PATH.\nMSYS2 (Windows) C++ libraries The clang++ found is not CLANG64's. Put C:\\msys64\\clang64\\bin first on PATH.\n\nmlir-tblgen and cmake under C++ are needed only to build libJLCS yourself. With the prebuilt library they may show FAIL while the summary still says C++ works.\n\nFrom code:\n\nstatus = RepliBuild.check_environment(verbose = false)\nstatus.ready          # C libraries can be wrapped\nstatus.tier2_ready    # C++ libraries can be wrapped","title":"Reading the report"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Building-libJLCS-yourself","page":"Set up","text":"You need this only on Windows (step 6 above), on a Linux machine that has no prebuilt library (the check says no prebuilt libJLCS for this platform), or when you want it built against your own LLVM.\n\nIt needs the MLIR development packages and CMake in addition to clang++:\n\nsudo apt install llvm-23-dev libmlir-23-dev mlir-23-tools cmake    # Debian, Ubuntu (apt.llvm.org)\n\nsudo dnf install llvm-devel mlir-devel clang-devel cmake           # Fedora, RHEL\n\nyay -S mlir && sudo pacman -S cmake                                # Arch\n\nThen, with llvm-config, mlir-tblgen and cmake on PATH:\n\nENV[\"REPLIBUILD_JLCS\"] = \"local\"\nusing Pkg; Pkg.build(\"RepliBuild\")\n\nA library you built is used in preference to the prebuilt one. It is tied to the LLVM it was built against: build it again after a system LLVM upgrade that changes the version's first or second number. The check tells you when.","title":"Building libJLCS yourself"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Settings","page":"Set up","text":"Environment variables that change where RepliBuild finds things. None is needed for a normal setup.\n\nVariable Effect\nREPLIBUILD_TOOLS Which copy of readelf, nm, objdump, git, cmake, ninja and the C linker is tried first. jll: RepliBuild's own (the default on Linux). system: the one on PATH (the default on Windows). The other is used when the first is missing or cannot start. Any other value is an error.\nREPLIBUILD_TOOL_<NAME> The path of one of those programs, used as given: REPLIBUILD_TOOL_GIT, _CMAKE, _NINJA, _READELF, _NM, _OBJDUMP, _LLVM_DWARFDUMP.\nLLVM_CONFIG The path of the llvm-config whose LLVM compiles C++, when it is not on PATH.\nREPLIBUILD_JLCS artifact for the prebuilt libJLCS, local for the one you built, or the path of a library file.\nMSYS2_ROOT Windows: where MSYS2 is installed, when not C:\\msys64.\nREPLIBUILD_C_SYSROOT Windows: the directory holding the C headers and libraries, when not MSYS2's clang64.\nREPLIBUILD_OBJDUMP Windows: the path of a GNU objdump, when not MSYS2's.\nREPLIBUILD_HOME Where registered builds are kept. Default ~/.replibuild. See Load it anywhere.","title":"Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Machines-that-only-run-a-wrapper","page":"Set up","text":"A machine that loads a wrapper someone else generated needs less than one that builds: see Build a package on it.","title":"Machines that only run a wrapper"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Next","page":"Set up","text":"Wrap your first library.","title":"Next"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Call-the-wrapper","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"wrap writes an ordinary Julia module beside the library it calls. This page is how the library's functions, types and objects look from Julia.","title":"Call the wrapper"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Load-it","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"include(\"path/to/project/julia/Mathlib.jl\")\nusing .Mathlib\n\nml_dot(ml_vec2(1.0, 2.0), ml_vec2(3.0, 4.0))\n\nAfter register, load it by name instead, from any session:\n\nM = RepliBuild.use(\"mathlib\")       # or:  using Mathlib\n\nThree rules:\n\nNever edit the generated file. wrap overwrites it. Change the TOML, or write your own code in your own module.\nKeep the files in julia/ together. The module finds its library beside itself.\nLoad a wrapper at the top level of a script or the REPL, not inside a function that then calls into it. That fails with method too new to be called from this world context; if you must, call through Base.@invokelatest M.f(x).","title":"Load it"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Find-your-way-around","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"names(Mathlib)            # everything the module exports\n?ml_normalize             # a function's Julia signature and its C argument types\nmethods(ml_dot)           # the argument types it accepts\n\nEverything is exported, with one exception: a name that Julia itself already uses. A C type called Number is Mathlib.Number, never plain Number. wrap prints those names when it writes the module.\n\nA few names cannot be used as the library spells them, and are given another. wrap prints each one, with the reason, on a line starting names:.\n\nThe library has The wrapper has Because\nA function String, Ptr, Cint String_, Ptr_, Cint_ The name is a Julia type\nA function eval, include, ccall eval_, include_, ccall_ Julia reserves the name\nA function or method nothing, LIBRARY_PATH nothing_, LIBRARY_PATH_ The wrapper's own code uses the name\nA struct Integer, Bool c_Integer, c_Bool, with methods c_Integer_get The wrapper's own code uses the name\nA value Symbol of enum Kind Kind_Symbol The wrapper's own code uses the name\nA global conf beside struct conf, or a function pool(void) beside struct pool conf_() (its address is still conf_ptr()), pool_() conf() and pool() make the zeroed struct\n\nOther names that Julia also has are kept: a function length, open or string, or a struct Task, is M.length, M.open, M.string, M.Task. To choose a function's name yourself, use [wrap.rename], with the library's name as the key (String, not String_).\n\nA function the wrapper cannot call safely is still defined. Calling it raises an error that starts with ABI Safety Trap and gives the reason: a long double passed by value, a struct whose layout could not be settled, a function the library has no debug information for. The rest of the module is unaffected. To list them:\n\nfilter(l -> occursin(\"Safety Trap\", l), readlines(\"julia/Mathlib.jl\"))","title":"Find your way around"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#How-C-types-appear","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"C Julia\nint, long, size_t, … Pass any Integer. It is converted, and checked for range.\nint32_t, uint8_t, … Int32, UInt8, …\nchar by value Pass any Integer from -128 to 255. A char result is a Cchar.\ndouble, float Pass any Real in a C wrapper. In a C++ wrapper pass a Float64 or Float32: write 2.0, not 2.\nbool Bool\nconst char* argument A String\nchar* result A String, or nothing for NULL\nstruct S by value The struct S\nS* Ref(s), or a Ptr{S}\nvoid* Ptr{Cvoid}\nAn enum Its own type, with its values as constants\nA function pointer Ptr{Cvoid}; make one with @cfunction\nA macro from [wrap.macros] A function: ML_VERSION(), lua_pop(L, 1)\n\nEach function's docstring states exactly what it takes.","title":"How C types appear"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Structs","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A struct is a Julia struct with the same fields:\n\nv = ml_vec2(3.0, 4.0)\nv.x                        # 3.0\nml_vec2()                  # every field zero\n\nStructs are immutable values, because C passes them by value. v.x = 9.0 is an error. Make a new one instead: ml_vec2(9.0, v.y). Where the wrapper defines them, setproperties(v; x = 9.0) and setproperty(v, :x, 9.0) return a changed copy.\n\nA struct argument takes the struct. A pointer argument takes a Ref.\n\nml_dot(v, v)               # double ml_dot(ml_vec2 a, ml_vec2 b)\n\nr = Ref(v)                 # ml_status ml_normalize(ml_vec2 *v)\nml_normalize(r)\nr[]                        # the value the function wrote: ml_vec2(0.6, 0.8)\n\nPassing a bare struct where a pointer is expected is a MethodError.\n\nA struct whose layout Julia cannot reproduce (unusual packing, some bitfields) has no named fields. It holds the raw bytes, and the wrapper gives it a getter and a setter per field. wrap warns when it does this, and names(M) shows the accessors.\n\nUnions hold their bytes and have a getter and a setter per member: get_U_m(u) and set_U_m!(u, v) in a C wrapper, get_m(u) and set_m!(u, v) in a C++ wrapper. U() makes a zeroed one.\n\nBitfields have a getter and a setter per field: get_f(s), and set_f(s, v), which returns a new struct, in a C wrapper; set_f!(s, v), which changes it, in a C++ wrapper. A signed field (int level : 7) reads as a signed number, and its setter takes the values the field can hold.","title":"Structs"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Enums","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"ml_normalize(Ref(ml_vec2(0.0, 0.0))) == ML_EDOM     # true\nInt(ML_EDOM)                                         # 1\nml_status(1)                                         # ML_EDOM\n\nAn enum is its own type, and its values are constants in the module. As in C, any integer of the right size is a valid value: ml_status(7) works, and prints as ml_status(0x00000007) because it has no name. Values combine with |, & and xor, and an Int is accepted where a field or argument wants the enum.\n\nAn enum whose values are bit flags is a plain integer type with named constants.\n\nWhen a value's name is also the name of a type, a function or another enum's value in the same module, it gets its enum's name in front: with a C++ class Rect and an enum class Kind { Circle, Rect }, the values are Circle and Kind_Rect. A comment above the enum in the wrapper lists these.","title":"Enums"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Strings","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A const char* argument takes a Julia String.\nA char* result is copied into a String. A NULL result is nothing.\nIf the caller must free the result, say so in [wrap.cstring_owned], and the wrapper frees it after copying.\nEvery such function also has a <name>_ptr version that returns the raw pointer and frees nothing, for when you need the pointer itself.","title":"Strings"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Variadic-functions","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"Each combination listed in [wrap.varargs] is its own function, named after its extra argument types:\n\nml_log(\"hello\\n\")                                 # fixed arguments only\nml_log_Cint(\"n=%d\\n\", 7)\nml_log_Cstring_Cdouble(\"%s=%g\\n\", \"pi\", 3.14)","title":"Variadic functions"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Callbacks","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A function-pointer parameter takes a Ptr{Cvoid}. Create it with @cfunction; the wrapper function's docstring shows the exact call for that callback's signature.\n\nfunction on_progress(done::Cint, user::Ptr{Cvoid})::Cint\n    println(\"done: \", done)\n    return 0\nend\n\ncb = @cfunction(on_progress, Cint, (Cint, Ptr{Cvoid}))\nrun_with_progress(cb, C_NULL)\n\nThe Julia function must be a named top-level function, not a closure.","title":"Callbacks"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Memory-the-library-allocated","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"Free it with the library's own function. For a buffer the library got from malloc, use the wrapper's M.libc_free(ptr).\n\nDo not use Julia's Libc.free on memory the library allocated. On Windows the library and Julia use different C runtimes, and freeing across them crashes. Libc.free is for memory you got from Libc.malloc.","title":"Memory the library allocated"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Pointers","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A function that returns T* returns a Ptr{T} when the wrapper has the type T, and a Ptr{Cvoid} otherwise. A typed pointer says what it points at, not who owns it: memory the library owns stays the library's.","title":"Pointers"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Global-variables","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A global variable has an accessor function that returns its value, and a <name>_ptr accessor that returns its address. A global named like a struct (extern const struct conf *conf;) has its value accessor renamed, conf_(), since conf() makes a zeroed conf; the address accessor is still conf_ptr().\n\nIn a C++ wrapper a global inside a namespace or a class is named with its scope, as a function is: cfg::depth is cfg_depth(), and a public static member geo::Vec3::live_count is geo_Vec3_live_count(). A private or protected static member has no accessor, and neither does a constexpr member, which has no storage to read. A global whose type is a C++ library type (std::string) has the _ptr accessor only.","title":"Global variables"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Sizes-and-offsets","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"M.struct_size(:ml_vec2)              # 16\nM.member_offset(:ml_vec2, :y)        # 8\n\nThese are not exported; call them with the module's name. They take the numbers from the compiled library, so use them rather than writing a size or offset into your code. An unknown name raises an error; M.STRUCT_SIZES and M.STRUCT_OFFSETS hold every name they know.\n\nM.LIBRARY_PATH is the library file the wrapper calls, and M.BUILD_GENERATOR the RepliBuild version that wrote it.\n\n","title":"Sizes and offsets"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#C","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"","title":"C++"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Names","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A function is named after its namespace, class and name, joined with _:\n\nC++ Julia\nshapes::midpoint(a, b) shapes_midpoint(a, b)\nshapes::Rect::area() shapes_Rect_area(rect)\nshapes::Rect::Rect(w, h) shapes_Rect_Rect(storage, w, h)\nshapes::Rect::~Rect() shapes_Rect_destroy_Rect(rect)\nhol::Box<double>::get() hol_Box_double_get(box)\n\nA method takes the object first, as a pointer. To choose your own names, use [wrap.rename].\n\nAn operator is named with a word: Vec3::operator< is Vec3_operatorless(a, b), operator[] is Vec3_operatorindex(v, i) and operator() is Vec3_operatorcall(v, x). names(M) lists the ones your wrapper has.","title":"Names"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Objects","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"Most C++ libraries hand you objects through functions that return a pointer. Use that pointer as the first argument of the class's methods, and give it back to the library's own function when you are done:\n\nr = shapes_make_rect(2.0, 3.0)        # Shape* make_rect(double, double)\n\nshapes_Rect_area(r)                   # 6.0\nshapes_Shape_rename(r, \"door\")\nshapes_Shape_name_abi_cxx11(r)        # \"door\"\nshapes_Rect_scale(r, 0.5)\n\nshapes_destroy_shape(r)               # the library's own release function\n\nA method of a base class takes a pointer to a derived object as it is.","title":"Objects"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Let-Julia-free-the-object","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"For a class the library can delete, the wrapper defines an owning type, Managed<Class>. Wrap the pointer in it and Julia's garbage collector deletes the object when nothing refers to it any more:\n\ns = ManagedShape(shapes_make_rect(2.0, 3.0))\nshapes_Rect_area(s)                   # pass it wherever the pointer goes\n\nWrap a pointer only if you own the object and it came from the library's allocator. Do not wrap an object the library still owns (a body that belongs to a physics world, for example), and do not also free it yourself.\n\nFor an extern \"C\" function, or a noexcept C++ function, that returns a pointer to such a class, the wrapper also defines <function>_safe, which returns the Managed object directly.\n\nA class has a Managed type when the library has a release function for it (a function taking a pointer to the class whose name contains delete, destroy or free), or when the class has a virtual destructor.","title":"Let Julia free the object"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Construct-an-object-yourself","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A constructor wrapper builds the object in memory you provide, like placement new in C++:\n\np = Ptr{Counter}(Libc.malloc(Shapes.struct_size(:Counter)))\n\nshapes_Counter_Counter(p, 3)          # Counter::Counter(int)\nshapes_Counter_next(p)                # 3\nshapes_Counter_next(p)                # 4\n\nshapes_Counter_destroy_Counter(p)     # Counter::~Counter(): runs the destructor, keeps the memory\nLibc.free(p)                          # the memory was yours\n\nTwo destructor wrappers exist, and they do different things:\n\nName Does Use it on\nClass_destroy_Class(p) Runs the destructor. Does not free the memory. An object you constructed in your own memory.\nClass_delete_Class(p) Runs the destructor and frees the object (C++ delete). An object that came from new. Never free it again afterwards.\n\nA class may have only one of the two: a class with a virtual destructor often has only delete. wrap names those classes, and each function's docstring says which kind it is.\n\nwarning: `Rect()` is not a constructor\nEvery struct type has a zero-argument form that returns a value with all bytes zero. For a C++ class that is not a constructed object: no constructor has run, and calling a virtual method on it crashes. Construct objects with the constructor wrapper or a library function.","title":"Construct an object yourself"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Factory-functions","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"When the library has a free function named create_…, new_…, make_…, alloc_…, init_… or build_… that returns a pointer to a class, the wrapper may turn that class into a Julia type you construct directly: M.Rect(2.0, 3.0) calls the factory, the class's methods get short forms (M.area(r)), and the object is deleted by the garbage collector. methods(M.Rect) shows whether your wrapper has this.\n\nWhen two factories of one class take the same argument types, name the one you want: with make_animal(int) and make_animal_doubled(int), M.Animal(Val(:make_animal), Cint(4)) and M.Animal(Val(:make_animal_doubled), Cint(4)) call each, and a bare M.Animal(Cint(4)) is a MethodError that lists both. The type's docstring names them.","title":"Factory functions"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#What-a-pointer-argument-accepts","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"In a C++ wrapper, every pointer parameter, every reference and the object of a method is checked before the C++ function runs. Each accepts:\n\nYou pass Accepted for\nA Ptr of any type, including C_NULL Everything\nRef(x) or Ref(array, i), where x is a number, a struct, a union or a bitfield record Every pointer except a function pointer\nA Managed object, an object made by a factory, a container such as CppVector, or your own type that defines Base.unsafe_convert(::Type{Ptr{Cvoid}}, x) A pointer to an object, and void*\nA union or bitfield record itself: M.Flags_get_level(f) A pointer to an object, and void*\nAn Array, or a contiguous view, of T T* where T is data (const int*), and void*\nA string char*, and byte pointers such as unsigned char*\nA @cfunction pointer A function pointer\n\nAnything else is a MethodError, before any C++ runs: a bare struct where the object goes, a Julia function where a function pointer goes, a Vector{Float64} for a const int*, a Julia Vector or a Ref{String} where a std::vector or std::string goes.\n\nA string passed for a char* must not contain a zero byte; one that does is an ArgumentError. A std::string parameter has no such limit.\n\nA noexcept function is called directly and is less strict about what it converts: pass it a Ptr, a Ref, an array, or the class's own Managed object.\n\nA wrapper written by an older RepliBuild does not make these checks. Run wrap again.","title":"What a pointer argument accepts"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Overloads-and-default-arguments","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"C++ overloads of one name are one Julia function with several methods. Julia picks the method from your argument types, so where two overloads differ you must pass the right type:\n\nan overload taking const char* takes a String; one taking const void* at the same position takes a pointer;\nan overload taking an enum takes the enum value, not an integer;\noverloads that differ in integer width take that exact type (Int32(1) or Int64(1)).\n\nAn argument that fits no overload is a MethodError. It is never a call to the wrong overload.\n\nA function with default arguments has a shorter method for each argument you may leave out, with the values C++ would use:\n\nshapes_Rect_scale(r)          # void scale(double f = 2.0)\nshapes_Rect_scale(r, 0.5)\n\nThis needs -fstandalone-debug, and covers constructors and member functions.","title":"Overloads and default arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#std::string","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A function returning std::string returns a Julia String.\nA parameter declared std::string or const std::string& takes a Julia String.\n\nNo TOML entry is needed for either. Functions returning a string carry a suffix on Linux: shapes_Shape_name_abi_cxx11. Rename them if you like.\n\nWide strings (std::wstring and the like) are not converted. A non-const std::string&, which the function writes into, is not converted either: pass a pointer to a string object the library gave you.","title":"std::string"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Containers","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"List the containers you want to create in [types] templates. Each gets a constructor function, and behaves like a Julia collection:\n\nv = create_std_vector_double()        # std::vector<double>\npush!(v, 1.0)\npush!(v, 2.0)\nlength(v)                             # 2\nv[1]                                  # 1.0\n\nshapes_total_area(v)                  # double total_area(const std::vector<double>&)\n\nstd::vector, std::string, std::map and std::unordered_map are supported this way.","title":"Containers"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Containers-passed-by-value","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A function that returns a std::vector<int> by value (or a map, a set, a shared_ptr) returns the container object as raw bytes. Pass that value back to a function that takes the container by value, or wrap it in a Ref for one that takes a reference:\n\nv = M.vec_iota(5)            # std::vector<int> vec_iota(int)\nM.vec_sum_val(v)             # int vec_sum_val(std::vector<int>): 10\nM.vec_sum(Ref(v))            # int vec_sum(const std::vector<int>&)\n\nNo copy is made when you pass it by value: the function works on your container's storage. A function that changes its by-value argument (grows it, or moves from it) can leave your value unusable.","title":"Containers passed by value"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Standard-types-passed-by-value","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"When a function takes or returns one of these by value, the wrapper has a struct for it:\n\nC++ Julia\nstd::pair<double, double> pair_double_double, with fields first and second\nstd::array<double, 2> array_double_2UL\nstd::tuple<…>, std::optional<…> A struct of their contents\nstd::complex<double> complex_double, holding one ComplexF64\nstd::string_view Its pointer and length\n\nr = M.minmax_pair(3.0, 1.5)           # std::pair<double, double>\n(r.first, r.second)                   # (1.5, 3.0)\n\nA library class returned by value comes back as a Julia struct holding its bytes. Its destructor is not run. For a class that owns resources, prefer the pointer-returning functions.","title":"Standard types passed by value"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Exceptions","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A C++ exception becomes a Julia exception carrying the C++ message. That includes one thrown by an extern \"C\" function of a C++ library:\n\ntry\n    shapes_checked_sqrt(-1.0)\ncatch e\n    e isa RepliBuild.JITManager.CxxException      # true\n    showerror(stdout, e)                           # C++ exception: negative input\nend","title":"Exceptions"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Inheritance","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"Pass a pointer to a derived object wherever a base-class method wants its object. That works as is for ordinary single inheritance.\n\nA virtual method runs the override of the class the pointer really points at: Animal_legs(p) on a Dog runs Dog::legs. That includes a pure virtual method: Animal_name(p) works although Animal itself has no body for name(). An abstract class cannot be constructed: Animal_Animal(p, 4) raises an error, as C++ refuses Animal a(4);. Construct a class that defines the method, or take the object from the library.\n\nWith multiple or virtual inheritance a base class may not start at the beginning of the object. For those the wrapper defines <Derived>_as_<Base>(p). Call the base's methods on its result:\n\nb = Derived_as_Base2(d)\nBase2_method(b)\n\nIf the wrapper has such a function for a pair of classes, use it. If it has none, pass the pointer directly.","title":"Inheritance"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Small-objects-returned-by-value","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"Some libraries return small handle objects by value (pugixml's xml_node). Methods want a pointer to them. A helper does the conversion:\n\nwithptr(f, h) = (r = Ref(h); GC.@preserve r f(Base.unsafe_convert(Ptr{typeof(h)}, r)))\n\nname = withptr(node -> pugi_xml_node_name(node), root)","title":"Small objects returned by value"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#Types-declared-inside-a-class","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A type declared inside a C++ class has two names in the wrapper, its own and one with the class in front: ClosestRayResultCallback and btCollisionWorld_ClosestRayResultCallback. They are the same type.\n\n","title":"Types declared inside a class"},{"category":"section","location":"calling/#A-CMake-project's-module","page":"Call the wrapper","text":"A CMake project is one module with a submodule per library. Call each library through its submodule. A type that several libraries share is one type, so values pass between them:\n\ninclude(\"julia/HelloWorld.jl\")\nH, W = HelloWorld.Hello, HelloWorld.World\n\nW.Vec2 === H.Vec2                                        # true\nW.hw_travel(H.hw_vec2(0f0, 0f0), H.hw_vec2(3f0, 4f0))    # 5.0f0\n\nLoad the top module, never one library's file by itself.","title":"A CMake project's module"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#Rust-crates","page":"Rust crates","text":"A Rust crate is wrapped as it is. It needs no extern \"C\", no #[repr(C)] and no other change: cargo builds the crate, and RepliBuild writes a Julia module for its public functions.\n\nThe recipe for a crate is short, because almost nothing has to be stated: the crate's name, and the few things only you can decide (how optimized, which features, which types a generic function is wanted for).\n\nnote: Where this runs\nRust crates are wrapped on Linux, x86-64 and AArch64. Windows has not been run yet.","title":"Rust crates"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#What-you-need","page":"Rust crates","text":"cargo and rustc, from rustup or your distribution's rust package. RepliBuild brings no Rust of its own, and a crate needs nothing of the C or C++ setup.\n\nusing RepliBuild\nRepliBuild.check_environment()\n\nThe report has a section for Rust, and the summary says:\n\nSummary line Means\nRust crates — cargo and rustc found You can wrap Rust crates.\n\nREPLIBUILD_TOOL_CARGO and REPLIBUILD_TOOL_RUSTC name particular programs. A rust-toolchain.toml in the crate is respected: cargo is started from the crate's folder.","title":"What you need"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#The-recipe","page":"Rust crates","text":"In the crate's folder, the one that holds Cargo.toml:\n\nRepliBuild.discover(\"path/to/geo\")\n\nThis asks cargo what the crate is and writes replibuild.toml beside Cargo.toml. Nothing is compiled yet, and nothing else is written into the crate.\n\n[project]\nname = \"geo\"                 # the module: julia/Geo.jl\n\n[rust]\nmanifest = \"Cargo.toml\"\nopt_level = 0\n\nThat file is enough to build. What you may want to add, all under [rust]:\n\nKey When to write it\nopt_level 0 to 3. 0 compiles fastest and is what discover writes. 3 is what cargo build --release gives: use it for a library you will call in earnest.\nfeatures The crate has Cargo features you want on: features = [\"simd\"]. default_features = false turns the default ones off.\ninstantiate The crate has generic functions you want to call. See Generic functions.\npackage Cargo.toml is a workspace with several library crates: name the one to wrap. discover writes it for you when the workspace has only one, and lists them when it has more.\ndependency The crate is fetched, not in the project folder. See below.\n\nEvery key is in Every key.\n\ndiscover keeps a [rust] recipe that is already there, with force = true too: there is nothing to scan, since cargo knows the crate's files.","title":"The recipe"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#A-crate-from-a-git-repository","page":"Rust crates","text":"A crate can come from a [dependencies] entry, pinned like any other source (Where the source comes from). This recipe wraps the semver crate from its repository:\n\n[project]\nname = \"rustsemver\"\n\n[dependencies.semver]\ntype   = \"git\"\nurl    = \"https://github.com/dtolnay/semver.git\"\ntag    = \"1.0.28\"\ncommit = \"7625c7aa3f0e8ba21e099d1765bcebcb72aa8816\"\n\n[rust]\ndependency = \"semver\"        # the crate is that dependency\nopt_level  = 3\n\nmanifest is then a path inside the fetched source (\"Cargo.toml\" by default). Nothing is written into the checkout.","title":"A crate from a git repository"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#Build-and-wrap","page":"Rust crates","text":"RepliBuild.build(\"path/to/geo/replibuild.toml\")    # cargo builds the crate\nRepliBuild.wrap(\"path/to/geo/replibuild.toml\")     # writes julia/Geo.jl\n\nor, in one call from nothing:\n\nRepliBuild.discover(\"path/to/geo\", build = true, wrap = true)\nGeo = RepliBuild.use(\"geo\")\n\nThe output folder (julia/ by default) then holds:\n\nFile What it is\nGeo.jl The Julia module.\nlibgeo.so The crate, as a shared library.\nlibgeo_thunks.so The calls a plain Julia call cannot make, compiled when the module was written. Keep it beside the other two.\ncompilation_metadata.json What the build found. The module is generated from it.\n\nwrap reports how many functions it wrapped and names each one it did not, with the reason.\n\nIf wrap says thunks: not compiled ahead of time, the module still works: it compiles those calls the first time one is used. That needs RepliBuild's support library (libJLCS in the check's report) on the machine that runs the module, where the compiled file needs nothing.","title":"Build and wrap"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#Calling-it","page":"Rust crates","text":"The examples use this crate:\n\npub struct Point { pub x: f64, pub y: f64 }\n\nimpl Point {\n    pub fn new(x: f64, y: f64) -> Point { Point { x, y } }\n    pub fn norm(&self) -> f64 { (self.x * self.x + self.y * self.y).sqrt() }\n}\n\npub fn midpoint(a: &Point, b: &Point) -> Point { /* ... */ }\npub fn greet(name: &str) -> String { format!(\"hello, {name}\") }\npub fn squares(n: u32) -> Vec<f64> { (0..n).map(|i| (i * i) as f64).collect() }\npub fn first_even(xs: &[i64]) -> Option<i64> { /* ... */ }\npub fn parse_age(text: &str) -> Result<u32, String> { /* ... */ }\npub fn checked_div(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { a / b }\npub fn apply(f: fn(f64) -> f64, x: f64) -> f64 { f(x) }\n\nA function keeps its name. A method is Type_method, and takes self as its first argument:\n\njulia> Geo.greet(\"Ada\")\n\"hello, Ada\"\n\njulia> Geo.squares(4)\n4-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.0\n 1.0\n 4.0\n 9.0\n\njulia> p = Geo.Point_new(3.0, 4.0);\n\njulia> (p.x, p.y)\n(3.0, 4.0)\n\njulia> Geo.Point_norm(p)\n5.0\n\njulia> m = Geo.midpoint(p, Geo.Point_new(1.0, 0.0)); (m.x, m.y)\n(2.0, 2.0)\n\nWhat you pass and what you get back:\n\nRust Julia\nintegers, floats, bool, char the same kind of number, Bool, Char. An integer argument is converted, and one that does not fit is an error.\n&str, String a String, both ways\n&[T], Vec<T> a Vector, both ways\n[T; N] a tuple out; a tuple or a vector of exactly N in\na tuple a tuple\na struct a Julia value with the same field names (p.x)\nan enum without data a Julia enum; the variant Level::Low is Geo.Level_Low\nan enum with data a value; RepliBuild.RustRuntime.variant(x) names its variant, and the variant's fields are properties\nOption<T> the value, or nothing\nResult<T, E> the value, or a thrown error (below)\n&T the value, or a Ref of it\n&mut T a Ref of the value: Rust changes it in place, and r[] reads it back\nfn(A) -> B any Julia function\n&dyn Trait a value of any type that implements the trait\n\nA &mut String or a &mut Vec<f64> is changed in place too, so it needs an object Rust can grow: s = Geo.RustString(\"hey\") or v = Geo.Vec_f64([1.0]), passed as it is and read back with String(s) or collect(v).\n\njulia> Geo.first_even([3, 5, 8, 9])\n8\n\njulia> Geo.first_even([1, 3]) === nothing\ntrue\n\njulia> Geo.apply(x -> x^2 + 1, 3.0)\n10.0","title":"Calling it"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#Errors-and-panics","page":"Rust crates","text":"A function that returns Result gives the value of an Ok and throws for an Err. The error's own value is in the exception:\n\njulia> Geo.parse_age(\" 42 \")\n0x0000002a\n\njulia> Geo.parse_age(\"old\")\nERROR: RustError: not an age: invalid digit found in string\n\nCatch it as RepliBuild.RustRuntime.RustError; e.value is what the Err held.\n\nA Rust panic does not end Julia. It arrives as a RepliBuild.RustRuntime.RustPanic carrying the panic's message and where it happened:\n\njulia> Geo.checked_div(1, 0)\nERROR: RustPanic: panicked at /path/to/geo/src/lib.rs:36:5:\nattempt to divide by zero","title":"Errors and panics"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#Values-Rust-owns","page":"Rust crates","text":"Some values hold memory that only Rust can free: a Box, or a struct that keeps a String or a Vec inside it. Such a value is a Julia object, and Rust frees it when Julia collects the object. You do nothing.\n\npub struct Inventory { items: Vec<String> }\n\nimpl Inventory {\n    pub fn new() -> Inventory { /* ... */ }\n    pub fn add(&mut self, item: &str) { /* ... */ }\n    pub fn len(&self) -> usize { /* ... */ }\n    pub fn names(&self) -> Vec<String> { /* ... */ }\n}\n\njulia> inv = Geo.Inventory_new();\n\njulia> Geo.Inventory_add(inv, \"rope\"); Geo.Inventory_add(inv, \"lamp\");\n\njulia> Geo.Inventory_names(inv)\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"rope\"\n \"lamp\"\n\nTwo things to know:\n\nPassing such an object to a function that takes it by value moves it, as in Rust. The object is empty afterwards, and using it again is an error. RepliBuild.RustRuntime.isowned(x) says whether it still holds its value.\nRepliBuild.RustRuntime.drop!(x) frees it now instead of when Julia gets to it.\n\nA String and a Vec of plain values are not objects like that: they are copied into a Julia String or Vector, and Rust's copy is freed at once. The module's OWNED lists the types that are.","title":"Values Rust owns"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#Generic-functions","page":"Rust crates","text":"A generic function is not in a library until something uses it with a type:\n\npub fn largest<T: PartialOrd + Copy>(xs: &[T]) -> T { /* ... */ }\n\nSay which types you want, in the recipe, written as Rust names them:\n\n[rust]\ninstantiate = [\"geo::largest::<f64>\"]\n\nEach entry becomes a function of the module named after it:\n\njulia> Geo.largest_f64([2.5, 9.0, 1.0])\n9.0\n\nA method of a generic type is \"geo::Pair::<i32>::sum\". A function that takes impl Trait is named by the type it should have: \"(geo::scale as fn(f32) -> f64)\". An entry that is not a function of the crate is a build error that shows the compiler's message.\n\nNothing else about a crate needs an entry. Its #[inline] functions, its statics (NAME() reads one) and its traits are found by the build.","title":"Generic functions"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#What-was-not-wrapped","page":"Rust crates","text":"A function RepliBuild cannot call yet is still in the module, as a function that raises an error saying why. The module lists them:\n\njulia> Geo.UNSUPPORTED          # name => reason; empty for this crate\nDict{Symbol, String}()\n\njulia> Geo.FUNCTIONS            # every wrapped function\n\nNot wrapped yet:\n\na callback the library keeps and calls later (one called during the call works), and a callback that takes a string, a slice or a struct by value;\na function that returns &dyn Trait, and a Julia type standing in for a Rust trait;\nbuilding a struct that owns memory from its fields in Julia (get one from a Rust function instead);\na generic function for a type the recipe does not name.\n\nTwo things Rust checks at compile time are yours to keep in Julia: a value that borrows from another must not outlive it, and a value should be freed on the thread that made it.","title":"What was not wrapped"},{"category":"section","location":"rust/#Sharing-it","page":"Rust crates","text":"RepliBuild.register and RepliBuild.use work as for any library (Load it anywhere). The crate is built where it lives, and the finished build is kept whole, the thunks file with it, so a later use(\"geo\") only loads it.","title":"Sharing it"}]
}
